    </node>
    <node id="Radiotracer">
      <data key="d0">Radiotracer</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A radiotracer is a radioactive substance used during PET scans to highlight metabolic activity and detect cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiotracer is a substance that emits radiation after being injected into the body, used in imaging tests like bone scans.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiotracer is a substance that releases small amounts of radiation, injected into a vein to help visualize bones in scans.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiotracer is a radioactive substance used in imaging tests like bone scans and PET scans to visualize internal structures and detect abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560436</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Complete Blood Count">
      <data key="d0">Complete Blood Count</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The Complete Blood Count (CBC) is a comprehensive blood test that evaluates overall blood health by measuring the levels of various blood components, including red blood cells (RBCs), white blood cells (WBCs), and platelets. This test provides essential information about the state of these blood cells, assisting healthcare providers in assessing a patient's health status, detecting potential health problems, and diagnosing blood disorders.

The CBC is commonly used to identify abnormal counts that may indicate conditions such as anemia, infections, bleeding disorders, or blood cancers like lymphoma and chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL). It plays a vital role in diagnosing and monitoring these conditions by providing detailed counts of different blood cell types. The test's broad utility makes it a fundamental component of routine health examinations and specific diagnostic investigations related to blood health and related abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560438</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tracer">
      <data key="d0">Tracer</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A radioactive drug injected into the body that accumulates in tissues over time, aiding in imaging metabolic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;A radioactive substance injected into the body to highlight cancer cells in PET scans.&lt;SEP&gt;A radioactive sugar-based substance used in PET scans to visualize cancer cells by highlighting areas of increased sugar usage in tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;A radioactive substance injected into the vein to help detect cancer cells and activity.&lt;SEP&gt;A radioactive drug injected into veins, used to identify cancer cells and other metabolic activities in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;A tracer is a radioactive drug injected into a vein during a PET scan to visualize cancer cells and their activity in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560438</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CT Scan">
      <data key="d0">CT Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A CT scan, or computed tomography scan, is a medical imaging procedure that utilizes X-ray technology combined with computer processing to generate highly detailed three-dimensional images of the internal structures of the body. This technique involves taking multiple X-ray images from various angles and then synthesizing them into cross-sectional, or slice-like, images that provide detailed visualization of tissues and organs inside the body. CT scans are particularly effective for producing comprehensive images of the chest, abdomen, and other internal regions, aiding in the detection of conditions such as cancers and the assessment of disease spread.

Compared to other imaging methods like MRI, CT scans are faster and often preferred when rapid diagnosis is required or when MRI is unavailable. They excel in producing detailed internal images, especially when contrast agents are used to enhance visibility of specific structures. Apart from diagnostic purposes, CT scans are employed during treatment planning, such as in cancer therapy, to accurately identify and visualize the location of tumors for targeted interventions.

Overall, the CT scan is a crucial diagnostic tool in modern medicine, providing detailed, cross-sectional views of the body's internal anatomy swiftly and effectively, thereby supporting accurate diagnosis, treatment planning, and disease monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560439</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast Material">
      <data key="d0">Contrast Material</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Contrast Material refers to substances used during medical imaging tests such as computed tomography (CT) and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) to improve the clarity and detail of internal body images. These substances are specifically designed to enhance contrast within the images, allowing healthcare professionals to better visualize internal organs, blood vessels, and structures. It is important to note that contrast material is not classified as a dye; rather, it is a distinct biochemical agent that facilitates clearer imaging results.

Typically, contrast materials are administered either orally or via intravenous (IV) injection, depending on the type of scan and the area being examined. The type of contrast agent varies between CT and MRI procedures, tailored to optimize image quality for each modality. After the imaging process, contrast substances are usually absorbed into the bloodstream and are subsequently expelled from the body through urine, often within a short period. Patients are generally advised to report any prior allergic reactions to contrast agents to ensure safety during the procedure.

Overall, contrast materials play a critical role in medical diagnostics by significantly enhancing the visibility of internal structures, thus aiding accurate diagnosis and treatment planning. Despite their differences from dyes, they are essential tools in imaging tests, functioning effectively to improve image quality and are safely eliminated from the body primarily through urination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560439</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="PET/CT Scan">
      <data key="d0">PET/CT Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A combined PET/CT scan overlays PET and CT images for enhanced diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET/CT scan is an imaging procedure that takes images of the whole body to detect cancer and may include additional tests like ultrasound of testicles if needed.&lt;SEP&gt;A combined imaging procedure that uses PET and CT scans for more detailed visualization, often using a radiotracer called fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560439</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Tests">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging tests are diagnostic procedures that produce visual representations of the interior of the body, aiding in the detection, evaluation, and staging of various cancers and associated internal abnormalities. Common modalities include CT scans, MRI, PET scans (such as FDG-PET/CT), ultrasound, endoscopy, bone scans, and other specialized imaging techniques. These tests are instrumental in visualizing tumor size, extent, and spread beyond primary sites, facilitating accurate staging and treatment planning.

In the context of specific cancers, imaging studies help determine the presence and extent of disease. For esophageal cancer, imaging scans assist in visualizing the primary tumor and its dissemination. Similarly, for mouth, kidney, ovarian, breast, and prostate cancers, these procedures locate primary tumors and assess metastasis, including lymph node involvement, organ infiltration, and distant spread. For prostate cancer, imaging helps ascertain whether the disease has extended beyond the prostate, while for breast cancer, various imaging techniques like mammograms, ultrasound, breast MRI, and bone scans aid in detection and staging.

Imaging tests are also vital in assessing blood-related cancers such as leukemia, where they help locate leukemia cells in organs like the CNS or testicles and identify complications outside the bone marrow and bloodstream. They are used to evaluate liver, stomach, and neck cancers (such as nasopharyngeal carcinoma), providing detailed images of internal structures to detect tumors, evaluate tumor size, and establish the presence of metastases. Additionally, these procedures are used to locate cancer deposits in bones and other tissues, supporting diagnosis and monitoring of disease progression.

Overall, imaging tests are crucial diagnostic tools across a wide spectrum of cancers, providing comprehensive internal visualization to guide clinical decisions, monitor treatment response, and detect recurrence or metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560440</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Core Needle Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Core Needle Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Core needle biopsy is another biopsy technique for assessing lymph node involvement.&lt;SEP&gt;A core needle biopsy (CNB) involves a larger needle to obtain a tissue core for diagnostic purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Core needle biopsy (CNB) uses a substantial needle to remove a tissue core for analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;A minimally invasive biopsy that removes tissue with a hollow needle, typically performed by an interventional radiologist or surgeon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560441</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical (Open) Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Surgical (Open) Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical procedure involving an incision to remove a tissue sample, performed by a surgeon in the operating room.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560441</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Test">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Test</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Tests that examine bone marrow, including aspirate and biopsy, usually from the hip bone, to evaluate liquid and cellular components.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560441</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Aspirate">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Aspirate</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A procedure involving drawing liquid bone marrow through a needle from the pelvis, used for diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;A bone marrow aspirate is a sample of liquid bone marrow used for diagnosing and genetic testing in leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure to remove a sample of bone marrow tissue for testing to diagnose AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow aspirate is a bedside procedure that involves extracting liquid marrow using a hollow needle from the pelvis.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure to obtain a sample of bone marrow for diagnosing AML.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure that retrieves liquid and cells from the bone marrow using a hollow needle, typically from the back of the pelvic bone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560441</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="MRI Scan">
      <data key="d0">MRI Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) scan is a diagnostic imaging procedure that employs radio waves and strong magnets to generate detailed images of the body's internal structures without the use of ionizing radiation such as X-rays. This non-invasive technique produces high-resolution images, enabling precise visualization of internal tissues and organs. MRI scans are particularly valuable in detecting and assessing various medical conditions, including cancers such as prostate cancer and lymphomas, by providing comprehensive views of internal areas like the central nervous system.

The MRI process involves the application of powerful magnetic fields and radiofrequency pulses to excite hydrogen nuclei in the body. As these nuclei relax back to their original state, they emit signals that are captured and transformed into detailed images by sophisticated computer algorithms. Enhancements such as contrast agents may be utilized to improve image clarity and diagnostic accuracy. Due to its ability to produce clear images without radiation exposure, MRI is considered a safe and essential tool in modern medical diagnostics, especially for detecting tumors, assessing disease extent, and guiding treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560442</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Cancer Risk Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Cancer Risk Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic cancer risk testing is used to identify inherited genetic mutations that may increase the risk of developing breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing evaluates inherited gene mutations that may increase the risk of developing breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Testing for genetic mutations or markers that indicate an increased risk of developing certain cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing assesses individual hereditary risk factors for developing cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic cancer risk testing assesses inherited genetic variants that influence cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing using blood or saliva to identify inherited gene mutations that increase cancer risk and can be passed to offspring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560443</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A radiologist is a highly specialized medical doctor trained in the interpretation of various medical imaging tests, including X-rays, computed tomography (CT), magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), positron emission tomography (PET), bone scans, and ultrasound. Their primary role is to analyze these diagnostic images to identify, diagnose, and monitor a wide range of medical conditions. 

Radiologists produce detailed reports based on their interpretations, which are then provided to healthcare providers to aid in diagnosis, treatment planning, and disease management. They play a crucial role in the detection of cancers such as prostate cancer, leukemia, primary CNS lymphoma, and thyroid cancer, assisting in staging, diagnosis, and treatment decisions. For example, radiologists interpret imaging results to help stage cancer, analyze tissue effects, and support radiation therapy planning. Their expertise is essential not only in initial diagnosis but also in ongoing monitoring and assessment of treatment response, making them integral members of the healthcare team across numerous medical specialties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560443</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gene Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Gene Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic alterations that influence subtypes and treatment responses of AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic alterations used to subtype AML, inform prognosis, and guide targeted treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic alterations that can influence leukemia behavior and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic alterations relevant for diagnosis and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic alterations used to classify AML subtypes and guide treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Inherited or acquired changes in genes that can increase the risk of developing cancer, identified through genetic testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560443</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="PET Scan">
      <data key="d0">PET Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A PET scan, or positron emission tomography, is a sophisticated imaging technique that employs radioactive tracers—commonly sugar-based compounds—to detect and visualize metabolically active cells within the body. Its primary application is identifying cancer cells, as these cells typically exhibit higher metabolic activity and increased sugar uptake compared to normal cells. When a radioactive tracer is injected into a vein, it distributes throughout the body, highlighting areas of heightened metabolic activity on the resulting images. Bright spots on the scan generally indicate regions where cancer cells are present and actively consuming sugar, thus enabling clinicians to locate and assess the size and activity of tumors.

In addition to cancer detection, PET scans are valuable for evaluating the metabolic functions of tissues, assessing tumor growth, and monitoring response to therapy. The technique can be combined with Computed Tomography (CT) scans to provide detailed anatomical and functional imaging, thereby enhancing diagnostic accuracy. While highly effective for identifying malignancies, PET scans can sometimes produce false-positive results by highlighting regions of inflammation or infection, which also exhibit increased metabolic activity. Overall, PET scanning is a crucial tool in oncology, providing vital information about the location, activity, and extent of cancer within the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560444</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mutations are errors in the genetic code that may contribute to cancer progression and influence treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Mutations are alterations in the genetic code, including deletion, insertion, inversion, amplification, translocation, and point mutations, influencing leukemia development.&lt;SEP&gt;Mutations are genetic alterations that can affect cancer behavior and treatment response.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic alterations that can influence cancer development and response to therapy, identified through biomarker testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Mutations are abnormal changes in DNA that can disrupt cell behavior and cause diseases like cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560444</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="MRI">
      <data key="d0">MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) is a sophisticated, non-invasive diagnostic imaging technique that utilizes magnetic fields and radio waves to generate highly detailed images of internal body structures. Its versatility allows for the examination of various organs and tissues across multiple medical contexts. MRI is particularly effective in visualizing soft tissues, making it invaluable in detecting, characterizing, and localizing tumors in regions such as the brain, spine, liver, rectum, and pelvis, including organs like the ovaries, uterus, cervix, and vagina.

In oncology, MRI assists clinicians in staging cancers, guiding biopsy procedures, and planning treatments such as brachytherapy by providing precise images of tumor invasion depths and tissue structures. It is often combined with other imaging modalities like PET or ultrasound to enhance diagnostic accuracy. MRI's capability to visualize internal organs without the use of ionizing radiation makes it a preferred choice for diagnosing cancer spread and for detailed internal visualization, including the breast tissues, prostate, and urinary tract.

The technology employs various MRI systems, including both open and closed configurations. Closed MRI machines are enclosed, potentially causing claustrophobia in some patients, who are advised to discuss their concerns with healthcare providers. Open MRI systems feature an open-top and open ends, reducing discomfort but potentially offering different imaging qualities.

Specialized MRI procedures may involve the use of gels or enemas, particularly when imaging regions such as the liver or rectum. Additionally, MRI plays a role in assessing tumor invasion depths, aiding in accurate staging and treatment planning. Overall, MRI is a critical tool across numerous medical fields, providing detailed internal images that support diagnosis, treatment planning, and disease monitoring without exposing patients to radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560445</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ewing Sarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Ewing Sarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Ewing sarcoma is a bone cancer that begins in the bone marrow, commonly in long bones or flat bones.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of cancer characterized by a specific gene fusion resulting from chromosomal translocation, notably involving the EWS gene on chromosome 22 and members of the ETS gene family.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560445</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gene Fusions">
      <data key="d0">Gene Fusions</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic abnormalities where parts of two chromosomes break and switch places, forming fusion genes associated with certain cancers like Ewing sarcoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560445</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Translocation">
      <data key="d0">Translocation</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Translocation involves swapping parts between two chromosomes, e.g., t(8;21), which is a specific chromosomal abnormality.&lt;SEP&gt;Translocation is a chromosomal abnormality involving the exchange of segments between chromosomes, such as t(8;21).&lt;SEP&gt;A chromosomal abnormality where segments of two chromosomes break and exchange places, e.g., t(11;22), associated with Ewing sarcoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560445</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="MSI-H/dMMR Mutation">
      <data key="d0">MSI-H/dMMR Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Microsatellite instability-high (MSI-H) and deficient mismatch repair (dMMR) are genetic features indicating defective DNA repair mechanisms in cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic defect where errors in microsatellite DNA sequences are not properly repaired due to defective mismatch repair proteins, leading to microsatellite instability and deficient mismatch repair.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560446</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Radioactive Drug">
      <data key="d0">Radioactive Drug</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A radioactive drug, called a tracer, is used during PET scans to visualize metabolic activity of cancer cells by highlighting areas of increased sugar uptake.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560446</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fluorodeoxyglucose">
      <data key="d0">Fluorodeoxyglucose</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG) is a radiotracer made of fluoride and glucose used in FDG-PET/CT scans to detect high glucose metabolism areas, often cancerous.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560446</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chromosomes">
      <data key="d0">Chromosomes</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromosomes are tightly packed structures containing genetic information, with humans having 23 pairs.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromosomes are structures within cells that carry genetic information; translocations involving chromosomes 11 and 22 are common in Ewing sarcoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560446</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chromosomal Translocation">
      <data key="d0">Chromosomal Translocation</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromosomal translocation involves parts of two chromosomes switching places, such as t(8;21).&lt;SEP&gt;A chromosomal abnormality where parts of two chromosomes break and switch places, e.g., t(11;22), often associated with Ewing sarcoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560446</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell skin cancer is a common and treatable skin cancer that originates from squamous cells in the skin's outer layer.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer is a common form of skin cancer that can develop in old wounds, burns, or scars and may be harder to treat if it forms in Marjolin’s ulcer or spreads deeper into the skin.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer is a type of skin cancer treated with chemotherapies like platinum drugs and targeted therapies like EGFR inhibitors.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer (SCC) is a malignant skin tumor that can be treated with surgery, radiation, or systemic therapy, depending on risk factors and spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560446</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Marjolins Ulcer">
      <data key="d0">Marjolins Ulcer</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Marjolin’s ulcer is a type of squamous cell skin cancer that forms in old wounds, burns, or scars and can be more difficult to treat.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560446</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CSCC (Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma)">
      <data key="d0">CSCC (Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">CSCC is a type of skin cancer that can be tested for through health history, lesion examination, skin biopsy, and imaging scans if it spreads.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560446</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Health History">
      <data key="d0">Health History</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A comprehensive health history is used to help diagnose and assess skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Patient's medical background including previous illnesses and treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;A record of a patient's past and current health problems, treatments, and relevant medical information used for prostate health assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;A comprehensive review of past and current health details to inform diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560446</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Skin Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A skin biopsy involves removing a sample of skin tissue to test for cancer, crucial for diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;A skin biopsy involves removing a sample of skin tissue for testing to detect cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Removes a small skin sample to diagnose skin inflammation or abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560447</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Scans">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Scans</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging scans are used if cancer may be deep or spread to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging scans are used to determine if skin cancer has spread deeper or to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging scans help detect enlarged lymph nodes or tissues affected by FL, aiding diagnosis and monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;Procedures including MRI, CT, PET, ultrasound, and bone scans utilized to detect and locate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560447</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A lymph node biopsy is performed when spread to lymph nodes is suspected.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph node biopsy involves surgically removing lymph nodes to examine for the presence of cancer cells, helping to determine metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure to remove part or all of a lymph node for testing to diagnose conditions such as CLL or SLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560447</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An oncologist is the cancer doctor involved in the treatment planning for squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;An oncologist is a medical doctor who receives information from radiologists to determine cancer staging and plan treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;An oncologist is a medical doctor specializing in the diagnosis and treatment of cancer, performing follow-up physical exams.&lt;SEP&gt;A doctor specializing in diagnosing and treating cancers, including blood cancers such as leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;An oncologist treats cancer patients and develops survivorship care plans detailing treatment history and follow-up recommendations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560447</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dermatologist">
      <data key="d0">Dermatologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A dermatologist is a medical specialist who examines and treats skin conditions, including skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A dermatologist diagnoses and treats skin conditions, including skin cancer, and performs skin exams.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560447</data>
      <data key="d6" />
