    <node id="FDG-PET/CT">
      <data key="d0">FDG-PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">FDG-PET/CT, or fluorodeoxyglucose positron emission tomography/computed tomography, is a comprehensive imaging modality that combines the functional imaging capabilities of PET with the anatomical detail provided by CT. This procedure utilizes a radiotracer called F-18 fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG), which is composed of fluoride and glucose, and is designed to highlight areas of high glucose metabolism typically associated with cancerous activity.

The primary purpose of FDG-PET/CT is to assist in the detection and localization of cancer, including identifying primary tumors, metastatic sites, and lymph node involvement. It is particularly useful for revealing sites of distant metastasis beyond blood and bone marrow, such as extramedullary leukemia manifestations, and plays an important role in cancer staging, restaging, and post-treatment evaluation. By detecting heightened metabolic activity, FDG-PET/CT enhances the accuracy of cancer diagnosis and helps guide treatment planning.

In clinical practice, FDG-PET/CT is employed in various contexts, including the detection of tumor recurrence, assessment of treatment response, and evaluation of metabolic activity in tissues suspected of malignancy. Its use is especially prominent in certain cancers, such as nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC), where it is utilized for evaluating distant metastases. The combined imaging technique allows practitioners to obtain detailed anatomical information from CT alongside metabolic data from PET, thereby improving diagnostic precision and therapeutic decision-making.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560449</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Cells">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer cells are abnormal, rapidly dividing cells involved in the development and progression of various cancers, including skin and prostate cancers. They are characterized by genetic mutations and altered behaviors that distinguish them from normal cells, notably their ability to grow uncontrollably, invade surrounding tissues, and spread to distant parts of the body through bloodstream and lymphatic vessels, leading to metastasis. 

In skin cancer, cancer cells can be visualized as bright spots on PET scans due to their increased metabolic activity, which allows for their detection and localization. Similarly, in prostate cancer, cancer cells exhibit abnormal growth patterns that are used to determine Gleason grades, aiding in diagnosis and treatment planning. These cells are targeted by systemic therapies such as chemotherapy and targeted treatments, which aim to eliminate or control their proliferation. Additionally, cancer cells in certain cancers like inflammatory breast cancer can obstruct lymph vessels, contributing to disease progression.

Cancer cells differ from normal cells not only in their uncontrolled growth and invasive potential but also in their ability to form primary tumors and metastasize. Their abnormal behavior is influenced by genetic mutations and biomarker status. They can crowd out normal cells, impair bodily functions, and pose significant health risks. Detection often relies on imaging techniques like PET scans, where their increased metabolic activity reveals their presence as bright spots, facilitating diagnosis and monitoring.

Overall, cancer cells are a hallmark of malignant diseases, defined by their genetic abnormalities, aggressive growth, invasive capacity, and ability to spread, all central to understanding cancer biology, diagnosis, and treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560449</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
