    <node id="Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Biopsy is a vital diagnostic procedure used across various medical disciplines to identify, confirm, and characterize cancers and other diseases. It involves the removal of tissue or fluid samples from the body—such as skin lesions, lymph nodes, organs, or tumors—for laboratory examination under a microscope or for molecular analysis. The primary purpose of a biopsy is to establish the presence or absence of malignancy, determine the type and grade of cancer, and assist in disease staging and treatment planning.

Different techniques are employed based on the location and nature of the suspected pathology. Common methods include shave, punch, excisional, fine-needle aspiration, core needle, and surgical biopsies. For instance, skin biopsies may be used to diagnose basal cell skin cancer or mouth cancer, while biopsies of lymph nodes can confirm Hodgkin lymphoma or primary CNS lymphoma. In organ-specific cases, such as lung, ovarian, prostate, kidney, liver, bone, or thyroid cancers, biopsies are performed during surgery, endoscopy, or with image guidance like ultrasound. These procedures often require specialized skills and may involve risks such as infection or bleeding.

Biopsies also play a critical role in diagnosing less common cancers and diseases, including gliomas, bladder cancer, cervical cancer, stomach cancer, pancreatic cancer, and various lymphomas. For example, in prostate cancer, biopsies help evaluate cancer presence and Gleason scores, guiding treatment decisions. Additionally, in diseases like AML (acute myeloid leukemia) or follicular lymphoma, biopsies facilitate genetic and molecular testing by analyzing cell markers. For certain diagnoses, such as primary CNS lymphoma or thyroid nodules, biopsies are performed with specific considerations to maximize accuracy and mitigate complications. 

Overall, biopsy is a cornerstone of diagnostic pathology that provides essential information for confirming a diagnosis, determining cancer subtype, assessing genetic features, and informing prognosis and treatment approaches across a broad spectrum of conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560450</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pharmacist">
      <data key="d0">Pharmacist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A pharmacist prepares and dispenses medicines, including those for cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A pharmacist prepares chemotherapy and medicines, offers treatment advice, and plays a role in cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560450</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Geneticist">
      <data key="d0">Geneticist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A geneticist studies inheritance patterns, diagnoses genetic disorders, and provides genetic counseling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560450</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Oncology Nurse">
      <data key="d0">Oncology Nurse</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An oncology nurse provides hands-on cancer care, manages side effects, and helps patients cope.&lt;SEP&gt;An oncology nurse provides hands-on care during cancer treatment, managing side effects and assisting patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560450</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Superficial Treatments">
      <data key="d0">Superficial Treatments</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Superficial treatments affect only the top layer of the skin and are used for early skin cancers like Bowen’s disease and actinic keratoses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560451</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Photodynamic Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Photodynamic Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Photodynamic therapy uses a lightsensitive drug to kill cancer cells when activated by light.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560451</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cryotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Cryotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Cryotherapy freezes and destroys precancerous and cancerous cells with liquid nitrogen or a tool.&lt;SEP&gt;Cryotherapy involves freezing and destroying cancer cells using very cold liquids or tools.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560451</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Topical Therapies">
      <data key="d0">Topical Therapies</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Topical therapies involve applying medicated creams such as 5-fluorouracil, imiquimod, or calcipotriene directly to the skin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560451</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="5-Fluorouracil">
      <data key="d0">5-Fluorouracil</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A chemotherapy cream used to treat early CSCC and actinic keratoses.&lt;SEP&gt;5-Fluorouracil (5-FU) is a chemotherapy applied directly to the skin, used in treating actinic keratosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560451</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Imiquimod">
      <data key="d0">Imiquimod</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A prescription cream that stimulates immune response to treat skin cancer lesions.&lt;SEP&gt;Imiquimod is a drug belonging to immune response modifiers that helps fight abnormal skin growths by activating the immune system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560451</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Calcipotriene">
      <data key="d0">Calcipotriene</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A vitamin D derivative used with other treatments to activate the skin’s immune system for actinic keratoses.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcipotriene (Sorilux, Dovonex) is a cream containing a form of vitamin D that activates the skin's immune system to treat actinic keratosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560451</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bowens Disease">
      <data key="d0">Bowens Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Bowen’s disease is an early form of squamous cell skin cancer affecting the top layer of the skin, often treatable with superficial treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560451</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Actinic Keratoses">
      <data key="d0">Actinic Keratoses</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Actinic keratoses are rough, scaly skin patches caused by sun exposure that may turn into squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Actinic keratoses are pre-cancerous skin lesions that can develop into squamous cell skin cancer and may be treated with topical therapies or superficial treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Actinic keratoses are pre-cancerous skin lesions that can be treated with photodynamic therapy, cryotherapy, or topical treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560451</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Conditions">
      <data key="d0">Skin Conditions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A general category encompassing various skin issues, including skin cancer, Bowen’s disease, and actinic keratoses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560451</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="PET/CT">
      <data key="d0">PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">PET/CT combines positron emission tomography and computed tomography to locate cancer cells more accurately than either scan alone.&lt;SEP&gt;PET/CT is an advanced imaging test used to detect further spread of cancer beyond lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;PET/CT is a combined imaging modality that utilizes both PET and CT scans for comprehensive analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;PET/CT combines positron emission tomography with computed tomography to identify areas of increased sugar uptake by cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560451</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="FDG-PET">
      <data key="d0">FDG-PET</data>
      <data key="d1">technique</data>
      <data key="d2">FDG-PET is a type of PET scan that uses a radioactive sugar to detect active cancer cells based on their higher sugar consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;FDG-PET uses a radioactive sugar (18-fluorodeoxyglucose) tracer to detect active cancer cells that metabolize sugar faster than normal cells, highlighting them as brighter in images.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560452</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="SSTR-PET">
      <data key="d0">SSTR-PET</data>
      <data key="d1">technique</data>
      <data key="d2">SSTR-PET is a PET scan that uses tracers targeting somatostatin receptors, useful for certain tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;SSTR-PET uses a tracer that binds to somatostatin receptors on cell surfaces, aiding in identifying tumors with these receptors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560452</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Biochemical Tests">
      <data key="d0">Biochemical Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">Biochemical tests measure substances like hormones in blood, urine, and saliva to detect effects of adrenal tumors or syndromes caused by them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560452</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Skin">
      <data key="d0">Skin</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Skin is the body's largest organ, consisting of three layers: epidermis, dermis, and hypodermis, serving as a barrier and the site where skin cancers develop.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin is the body's largest organ, providing a waterproof barrier, temperature regulation, and sensory functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin is the outer organ of the body that can develop conditions such as actinic keratosis and squamous cell carcinoma, and is the site of application for various topical therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin is the body's largest organ, involved in wound healing, and can be treated with various surgical and radiation methods.&lt;SEP&gt;The outer layer of the breast that can show changes such as redness or thickening in inflammatory breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The skin is the outer covering of the body, involved in detecting changes, and is examined in skin exams for cancer detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560452</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Epidermis">
      <data key="d0">Epidermis</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The outermost skin layer, made up of cells including basal cells, squamous cells, and melanocytes; basal cells are involved in basal cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Epidermis is the outermost layer of skin containing squamous, basal, and melanocytes cells.&lt;SEP&gt;The epidermis is the outermost layer of skin involved in protecting the body and is often sampled during biopsies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560452</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cells">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cells are flat cells found in the upper part of the epidermis, and are involved in other types of skin cancer such as squamous cell carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cells are thin, flat cells in the epidermis involved in forming the skin's surface and originating skin cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cells are flat cells lining surfaces such as the anal canal and skin, involved in squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560453</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Basal Cells">
      <data key="d0">Basal Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">Basal cells are located in the lower part of the epidermis and can become abnormal, leading to basal cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Basal cells are located in the lower epidermis, dividing to produce new cells, and can give rise to basal cell carcinomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560453</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Melanocytes">
      <data key="d0">Melanocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">Melanocytes are cells in the lower epidermis producing melanin, responsible for skin, hair, eye color, and protecting against UV radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Melanocytes produce melanin, influencing skin color and protecting against UV damage; they are not typically involved in squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560453</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sunlight/Outdoor Tanning">
      <data key="d0">Sunlight/Outdoor Tanning</data>
      <data key="d1">riskfactor</data>
      <data key="d2">Prolonged sun exposure and outdoor tanning increase the likelihood of developing squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560453</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d0">Biomarker Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomarker testing is a diagnostic and therapeutic tool that involves analyzing biological molecules, including genes and proteins, within tumor cells or tissue samples to inform clinical decision-making. This process is utilized to detect specific genetic mutations, molecular features, or protein markers that can influence diagnosis, prognosis, and treatment strategies.

Specifically, biomarker testing often involves analyzing tumor tissue or blood samples to identify genetic alterations such as mutations, homologous recombination deficiency (HRD), microsatellite instability, and expression levels of markers like HER2 or p16 (to determine HPV involvement in cancers). These detailed molecular assessments help identify patients who are suitable candidates for targeted therapies or immunotherapies by evaluating tumor markers and molecular features that predict treatment response.

In oncology, biomarker testing plays a critical role in personalizing cancer treatment. By detecting specific molecular characteristics, such as high or low expression of certain proteins or the presence of particular genetic mutations, clinicians can tailor treatment plans more precisely. This includes guiding decisions on targeted therapies, immunotherapies, and assessing the likelihood of cancer spread or progression. Laboratory analysis of biopsy samples is the primary method used, facilitating detection of relevant DNA mutations, protein expressions, or other molecular features essential for optimal treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560454</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Indoor Tanning">
      <data key="d0">Indoor Tanning</data>
      <data key="d1">riskfactor</data>
      <data key="d2">Use of indoor tanning devices significantly raises the risk of skin cancers, including squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560454</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Environmental Toxins">
      <data key="d0">Environmental Toxins</data>
      <data key="d1">riskfactor</data>
      <data key="d2">Exposure to pollutants like tobacco smoke, vehicle exhaust, and arsenic can damage skin cells and increase cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Environmental toxins include pollutants such as tobacco smoke, vehicle exhaust, and arsenic that can cause skin damage leading to skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560454</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Scars and Chronic Wounds">
      <data key="d0">Scars and Chronic Wounds</data>
      <data key="d1">riskfactor</data>
      <data key="d2">Scars and non-healing wounds, such as ulcers and burns, can develop into squamous cell carcinoma through a process called Marjolin’s ulcer.&lt;SEP&gt;Scars and chronic wounds like ulcers and burns can develop into squamous cell carcinoma, known as Marjolin's ulcer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560454</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenal Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Adrenal Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Adrenal tumors are abnormal growths in the adrenal glands, which may secrete hormones or be nonfunctional, and are diagnosed through various imaging and biochemical tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560455</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Marjolin">
      <data key="d0">Marjolin</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Marjolin refers to squamous cell carcinoma that develops in scars or chronic wounds, often characterized by aggressive growth in scar tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560455</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Somatic Mutation">
      <data key="d0">Somatic Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Gene mutations acquired in tumors that are not inherited but are present within the cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Somatic mutations occur in individual cells during a person’s lifetime and are not inherited.&lt;SEP&gt;A gene mutation occurring spontaneously in some cells during a person’s lifetime and not inherited.&lt;SEP&gt;Somatic mutations are mutations that occur only in tumor cells and are not inherited.&lt;SEP&gt;A somatic mutation or somatic change refers to DNA mutations or alterations that occur in body cells, affecting the genetic information without being inherited.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560455</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="MSI-H">
      <data key="d0">MSI-H</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">MSI-H (Microsatellite Instability-High) and dMMR are biomarkers indicating certain cancers may respond to immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsatellite instability-high (MSI-H) is a condition where tumors exhibit high levels of microsatellite instability, often due to defective mismatch repair.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560455</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="dMMR">
      <data key="d0">dMMR</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Deficient mismatch repair (dMMR) refers to tumors that have a defect in mismatch repair genes, leading to microsatellite instability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560455</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair Proteins">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair Proteins</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Proteins responsible for fixing errors in DNA microsatellites, involved in MSI/dMMR.&lt;SEP&gt;Mismatch repair (MMR) proteins are responsible for fixing errors in DNA during cell replication, preventing mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560455</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Microsatellites">
      <data key="d0">Microsatellites</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Microsatellites are short, repeated DNA sequences; instability in these regions can signal certain cancer traits.&lt;SEP&gt;Short, repeated DNA sequences whose instability indicates mismatch repair defects.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsatellites are short, repeated DNA sequences prone to errors during replication, which are normally corrected by MMR proteins.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560455</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="MSI-H/dMMR Tumors">
      <data key="d0">MSI-H/dMMR Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">MSI-H/dMMR tumors are cancers exhibiting high microsatellite instability and deficient mismatch repair mechanisms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560455</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologist">
      <data key="d0">Pathologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A pathologist is a medical expert and specialist who plays a critical role in the diagnosis and characterization of various diseases, primarily cancer. They examine tissue and cell samples obtained through biopsies, surgeries, or other procedures, often using microscopy to detect the presence of malignancies. In the context of cancer diagnosis, pathologists analyze tissue samples to confirm the presence of cancer cells, identify the specific type and histological features of tumors (such as in skin, ovarian, prostate, or thyroid tissues), and determine the grade or aggressiveness of the disease.

Beyond initial detection, pathologists are integral to staging cancers, assessing how far a disease has progressed by examining removed tissues, lymph nodes, bone marrow, or other tissues. For example, they review bone marrow samples to diagnose hematological cancers like acute myeloid leukemia (AML) or leukemia, and analyze lymph nodes or surgical specimens to establish tumor invasion and spread. In particular cases, such as cervical or skin cancers, they aid in confirming the diagnosis and understanding tumor margins and invasion depth.

Their expertise extends to evaluating cellular features and tumor histology, which can influence treatment decisions and prognosis. Pathologists also compare cancer cells with normal cells to assess grades, determine malignancy severity, and identify tumor subtypes. Their work is fundamental in clinical pathology, histopathology, and hematopathology, supporting the accurate classification, diagnosis, and staging of a broad spectrum of diseases, especially cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560456</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pembrolizumab (Keytruda)">
      <data key="d0">Pembrolizumab (Keytruda)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Pembrolizumab (Keytruda) is a medication used to treat MSI-H/dMMR tumors, acting as an immune checkpoint inhibitor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560456</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Mutational Burden">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Mutational Burden</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">TMB measures the number of mutations in tumor DNA, influencing immunotherapy decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutational burden measures the number of mutations within a tumor genome, guiding treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;TMB is evaluated to predict response to immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutational burden (TMB) refers to the number of mutations per million base pairs of tumor DNA, with high TMB (TMB-H) indicating 10 or more mutations per million.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutational burden refers to the number of mutations per million base pairs in tumor DNA, influencing response to immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutational burden (TMB) measures the number of mutations per million base pairs in tumor DNA, which can predict response to immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutational burden (TMB) refers to the number of mutations per million base pairs in tumor DNA, with high TMB (TMB-H) indicating more mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560457</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="TMB-H">
      <data key="d0">TMB-H</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor mutational burden-high (TMB-H) indicates a high number of mutations in tumor DNA, affecting immunotherapy decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;TMB-H (Tumor Mutational Burden–High) is a biomarker associated with potential responsiveness to immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutational burden-high (TMB-H) describes tumors with 10 or more mutations per million base pairs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560457</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Mutation Testing">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Mutation Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor mutation testing analyzes genetic mutations in tumor DNA, identifying targeted therapy options.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutation testing analyzes tumor DNA or blood samples to identify specific DNA mutations for targeted therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutation testing examines tumor samples for specific DNA mutations to guide targeted therapy selection.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutation testing involves analyzing tumor or blood samples to identify specific DNA mutations in cancer cells, often using NGS or PCR.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560457</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gene">
      <data key="d0">Gene</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The gene is a fundamental unit of heredity and a segment of DNA within chromosomes that encodes instructions for the synthesis of proteins essential for various cellular functions. Genes serve as biological templates, carrying genetic information that influences cell behavior, development, and disease processes. They are located within the cell nucleus and are subject to mutations, which can impact their normal function and potentially lead to disease manifestations.

For example, the gene BCR::ABL1 is involved in chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) and encodes an abnormal protein that promotes uncontrolled cell growth, contributing to disease progression. Similarly, the BCR::ABL gene encodes a protein that plays a pivotal role in CML. In the context of cancer diagnostics and treatment, certain genes such as BRCA1, BRCA2, ATM, CHEK2, MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PALB2, PMS2 are crucial in hereditary and tumor testing, helping to assess cancer risk and guide therapies. Additionally, genes involved in DNA repair processes (e.g., BRCA1, BRCA2, ATM, PALB2, FANCA) are analyzed in tumor testing to identify molecular markers relevant to diagnosis and treatment planning.

Specific gene fusions are characteristic of certain cancers, such as Ewing sarcoma, where genes like EWSR1, FLI1, ERG, ETV1, ETV4, and FEV fuse to create abnormal gene products that drive tumor development. Furthermore, mutations in genes can influence the development of other cancers, such as bladder cancer, highlighting their importance in disease etiology and progression. Genes are also analyzed to detect mutations that may inform personalized medical approaches, including targeted therapies. Overall, genes are crucial components of genetic instruction, hereditary traits, and disease mechanisms, with their study providing vital insights into health and disease management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560457</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Genomic Aberration Testing">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Genomic Aberration Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor genomic aberration testing analyzes tumor DNA to identify mutations, guiding targeted therapy decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560457</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS)">
      <data key="d0">Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">NGS is a detailed DNA sequencing method for detecting gene mutations in tumor or normal tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560458</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR)">
      <data key="d0">Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">PCR is a technique used to amplify DNA or RNA, enabling detection of rare abnormalities within tumor cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560458</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="IDH1 Mutation">
      <data key="d0">IDH1 Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">IDH1 mutation is a genetic alteration in some cancers, targeted with specific therapies such as ivosidenib.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560458</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ivosidenib (Tibsovo)">
      <data key="d0">Ivosidenib (Tibsovo)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Ivosidenib (Tibsovo) is a targeted therapy for patients with IDH1-mutant cancers, such as certain chondrosarcomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560458</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Bone Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone cancer includes primary tumors that originate in bones or cartilage and secondary tumors that spread to bones from other sites.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone cancer is a malignant tumor affecting bone tissue, requiring biopsy and genetic testing for diagnosis and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560458</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarkers">
      <data key="d0">Biomarkers</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomarkers are proteins found on the surface or inside white blood cells, used as targets in immunophenotyping to identify cell types and disease states.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarkers are specific features on cancer cells that targeted therapy and immunotherapy utilize to increase treatment effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarkers are proteins or molecules tested in tumor samples to help guide treatment decisions and understand tumor characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarkers are substances found in the body that indicate a disease or abnormality, used in tumor testing for diagnosis and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560458</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Considerations">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Considerations</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Certain bone cancer treatments can affect fertility; patients should consult fertility specialists before treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560459</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Staging">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer staging describes the extent of disease, including tumor size, spread to lymph nodes, and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging describes the size, location, and spread of cancer, often used to guide prognosis and treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of determining the extent of cancer using systems like the FIGO system, which involves tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging describes tumor size, location, and spread to lymph nodes or other organs, guiding treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging describes the extent to which cancer has grown and spread in the body, based on tumor size, invasion, and spread to other tissues or organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560459</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="TNM System">
      <data key="d0">TNM System</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The TNM system is used to stage throat cancer based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The tumor, node, metastasis (TNM) system is a standardized framework created by cancer experts to describe cancer extent based on test results.&lt;SEP&gt;A cancer staging system assessing tumor size (T), lymph node involvement (N), and metastasis (M).&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system is used to stage breast cancer, evaluating tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system is used to stage throat and nasopharyngeal cancers by assessing tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system uses tumor size (T), lymph node involvement (N), and metastasis (M) to stage cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560459</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Factors">
      <data key="d0">Risk Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk factors refer to variables, conditions, or behaviors that increase the likelihood of developing certain diseases or cancers. These factors vary depending on the specific type of cancer but often include environmental exposures, lifestyle choices, genetic predispositions, and health-related conditions.

In the context of skin cancer, risk factors such as sun exposure, indoor tanning, environmental toxins, and skin scars have been identified to elevate the risk of squamous cell skin cancer. For lung cancer, key risk factors include smoking, environmental exposures, and genetic traits. For pancreatic cancer, significant risk factors encompass smoking, heavy alcohol use, unhealthy diet, exposure to chemicals, diabetes, chronic inflammation, and family history. Similarly, prostate cancer risk increases due to age, race, genetics, and family history.

Cervical cancer risk factors primarily include HPV infection, smoking, high parity (number of pregnancies), and immunosuppression. Thyroid cancer risk is associated with exposure to radiation and certain genetic syndromes. For anal cancer, human papillomavirus (HPV) infection is a prominent risk factor, along with immune suppression, history of sexually transmitted infections, certain sexual practices, smoking, alcohol consumption, and radiation exposure.

Overall, these risk factors can influence the development and progression of various cancers, as well as impact treatment outcomes and disease severity. Recognizing and understanding these factors is crucial for prevention, early detection, and targeted interventions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560460</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Formation">
      <data key="d0">Bone Formation</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone is a living tissue composed of cells like osteocytes, calcium, phosphate, hydroxyapatite, and collagen, involved in growth and remodeling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560460</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ossification">
      <data key="d0">Ossification</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Ossification is the process of new bone formation that occurs during fetal development and adolescence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560460</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Ultrasound is a versatile diagnostic imaging technique that employs high-frequency sound waves to produce detailed images of internal body structures. Its non-invasive, painless nature, combined with the absence of ionizing radiation, makes it a widely used initial assessment tool across various medical fields. Ultrasound can generate real-time images of organs such as the liver, kidneys, ureters, bladder, thyroid, testicles, ovaries, uterus, and surrounding tissues, aiding in the detection and evaluation of abnormalities, including small cancers and suspicious nodules.

In oncology, ultrasound is essential for detecting and assessing tumors in multiple regions. It is frequently utilized to evaluate adrenal tumors, ovarian masses, breast lesions, prostate abnormalities, thyroid nodules, and neck lymph nodes. Ultrasound-guided biopsies are common, especially in prostate cancer diagnosis via transrectal ultrasound (TRUS). It also plays a role in screening for liver cancer and monitoring thyroid tumors during active surveillance. Additionally, ultrasound imaging extends to cardiac assessments through echocardiograms, providing valuable insights into heart function, and can evaluate testicular involvement in lymphoma spread.

Due to its real-time imaging capabilities, portability, and safety profile, ultrasound serves as a frontline diagnostic tool in various medical specialties, including oncology, cardiology, gynecology, and general internal medicine, facilitating early detection, diagnosis, and management of many internal health conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560460</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Remodeling">
      <data key="d0">Remodeling</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone remodeling is a continuous process where old bone is broken down by osteoclasts and new bone is formed by osteoblasts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560460</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Osteocytes">
      <data key="d0">Osteocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">Osteocytes are mature bone cells derived from osteoblasts, involved in maintaining bone tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560460</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Osteoblasts">
      <data key="d0">Osteoblasts</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">Osteoblasts are cells responsible for forming new bone during ossification and remodeling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560460</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Osteoclasts">
      <data key="d0">Osteoclasts</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">Osteoclasts are cells that break down bone tissue, releasing calcium into the bloodstream.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560461</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Epiphysis">
      <data key="d0">Epiphysis</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The epiphysis is the end part of a long bone, covered with cartilage to form a joint.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560461</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Metaphysis">
      <data key="d0">Metaphysis</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The metaphysis is the part of the bone located between the epiphysis and diaphysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560461</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Diaphysis">
      <data key="d0">Diaphysis</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The diaphysis is the middle or shaft region of a long bone, providing structural support.&lt;SEP&gt;The diaphysis is the central shaft of a long bone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560461</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Physis">
      <data key="d0">Physis</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The physis, or growth plate, is made of cartilage and located at the ends of long bones, playing a key role in bone growth.&lt;SEP&gt;The physis, or growth plate, is made of cartilage and is involved in bone growth during development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560461</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="TNM">
      <data key="d0">TNM</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">TNM is a standardized system used to describe the extent of cancer spread, based on Tumor size, Node involvement, and Metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system classifies cancer based on tumor size (T), lymph node involvement (N), and metastasis (M).&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system stages most bone cancers by assessing tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis based on imaging and biopsy results.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560461</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Grading">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Grading</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer grade describes how abnormal cancer cells look under a microscope, influencing prognosis and treatment choices.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer grading assesses the aggressiveness and growth rate of cancer cells, classified as low grade or high grade based on cell appearance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560462</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Spinal Column">
      <data key="d0">Spinal Column</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The spinal column consists of vertebrae, including cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacrum, and coccyx, supporting the body and protecting the spinal cord.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560463</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vertebrae">
      <data key="d0">Vertebrae</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Vertebrae are individual bones that form the spinal column, including cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacrum, and coccyx.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560463</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T (Tumor)">
      <data key="d0">T (Tumor)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">T indicates the size of the primary tumor and potential outside growth, with higher numbers reflecting larger tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;The T category describes the size and extent of primary tumor in cancer staging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560463</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="N (Node)">
      <data key="d0">N (Node)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">N indicates whether cancer has spread to lymph nodes, with higher numbers indicating more node involvement.&lt;SEP&gt;The N category indicates whether cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560463</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="M (Metastasis)">
      <data key="d0">M (Metastasis)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">M indicates if cancer has spread to distant parts of the body outside the pelvis, with higher numbers indicating metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The M category describes whether cancer has metastasized to distant parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560463</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="G (Grade)">
      <data key="d0">G (Grade)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Classification reflecting how abnormal tumor cells look under microscope.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer grade, indicating the degree of abnormality of tumor cells, affects tumor growth and spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560463</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sacroiliac Joint">
      <data key="d0">Sacroiliac Joint</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The sacroiliac joint (SIJ) connects the sacrum to the pelvis and is involved in tumor staging when tumors cross or involve this joint.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560463</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Size">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Size</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor size, measured in centimeters, affects the risk level; tumors over 4 cm are considered very high risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor size (T) indicates how large the primary tumor is and whether it has invaded nearby tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Measurement of tumor dimensions in centimeters used to define cancer stages; examples include 7 cm for a baseball, 4 cm for a golf ball, and 1 cm for a pea.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor size refers to the measurement of the main tumor and is expressed in centimeters, influencing staging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560463</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lung">
      <data key="d0">Lung</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Lungs are a pair of organs in the chest responsible for gas exchange, taking in oxygen and expelling carbon dioxide.&lt;SEP&gt;The lung is a common site for metastatic esophageal cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;The lungs are the primary organs involved in oxygen exchange, with structures like lobes, bronchi, and alveoli.&lt;SEP&gt;The lung is a vital respiratory organ involved in gas exchange and can be affected by metastatic cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The lung is a respiratory organ where metastasis can occur in cancer progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Lung is a common site of metastasis for liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lungs are vital organs that may be examined during chest imaging to evaluate cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560464</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvis">
      <data key="d0">Pelvis</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The pelvis is a structure of bones in the lower part of the torso that connects the spine to the legs and supports various organs.&lt;SEP&gt;The pelvis is a basin-shaped structure supporting the spinal column and protecting abdominal organs, with possible metastasis involvement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560464</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grade">
      <data key="d0">Grade</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Grades 1, 2, and 3A of follicular lymphoma are categorized based on cell types and proportions, influencing prognosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Grade describes how abnormal tumor cells look under a microscope, indicating the aggressiveness of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Grade describes the abnormality of tumor cells, with G1 being well differentiated and G3 poorly differentiated, influencing cancer growth and spread.&lt;SEP&gt;A classification indicating how abnormal tumor cells look and how quickly the tumor may grow, rated from 1 to 4.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560464</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="G">
      <data key="d0">G</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">G indicates the tumor grade, with higher grades correlating with faster growth and spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560464</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Numbered Stages">
      <data key="d0">Numbered Stages</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Numbered stages in cancer range from stage I to stage IV, combining T, N, M, and G scores to determine cancer advancement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560464</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility">
      <data key="d0">Fertility</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility is the biological ability to have or conceive children, a capacity that can be influenced by various factors, including cancer treatments. Cancer therapies such as chemotherapy, radiation therapy, endocrine therapy, and treatments for specific cancers like bone or breast cancer may impair fertility temporarily or permanently. These treatments can cause damage to reproductive organs or hormonal function, leading to a reduced or lost capacity for conception.

Given the potential impact of cancer treatments on reproductive health, fertility preservation options are often discussed prior to commencing therapy. Such options may include sperm banking, oocyte or embryo cryopreservation, or other medical interventions designed to maintain reproductive potential. Patients planning to have children in the future are encouraged to consult fertility specialists beforehand to explore these options and understand the associated risks and procedures.

Fertility considerations are integral to treatment planning, and healthcare providers typically address concerns about future pregnancy, discussing the possible risks involved and the feasibility of parenthood post-treatment. Patients are advised to communicate their reproductive plans with their care team early, especially when treatments like radiation or endocrine therapy are involved, as these can have long-term effects on reproductive capacity. Overall, fertility remains an important aspect of supportive care for cancer patients, emphasizing the need for informed decision-making regarding reproductive health before, during, and after cancer therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560466</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic testing is a comprehensive diagnostic and predictive tool used to analyze DNA to identify both inherited and somatic mutations that have significant implications for disease diagnosis, prognosis, and treatment planning. It encompasses various applications, including the detection of specific genetic alterations such as the BCR::ABL gene and other mutations relevant to leukemia, enabling clinicians to tailor therapeutic strategies and assess disease progression. In oncology, genetic testing plays a crucial role by analyzing tumor tissue samples and blood specimens for mutations like BRCA, FAP, Lynch syndrome-associated genes, and other genetic biomarkers. These tests can reveal hereditary predispositions to cancers such as ovarian, breast, colon, liver, and prostate cancers, especially in individuals with a family history or specific risk factors.

Tests performed on normal tissue or tumor samples help differentiate between inherited mutations and tumor-specific genetic changes, facilitating personalized treatment approaches. For example, testing for BRCA mutations informs risks and management strategies for ovarian and breast cancers, while analysis of DNA from leukemia cells guides targeted therapies and prognosis. In hereditary cancer syndromes, genetic testing identifies mutations in genes such as CDKN2A, FGFR3, PIK3CA, and ERBB, which are linked to increased risks of multiple cancer types. Additionally, genetic testing of biopsy samples can aid in diagnosing invasive features of thyroid cancers when histopathology is inconclusive.

Overall, genetic testing provides vital insights into genetic predispositions, cancer risk assessment, and tumor-specific alterations. It involves analyzing blood, tissue, or family member DNA to identify inherited mutations, assess individual and familial risk, and inform personalized medical management across a spectrum of diseases, particularly various cancers. Its versatility makes genetic testing an essential component in modern precision medicine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560467</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gallbladder">
      <data key="d0">Gallbladder</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The gallbladder is a small organ that stores and concentrates bile, with its inner surface covered by a thin, moist serous membrane called serosa.&lt;SEP&gt;The gallbladder is a small organ located near the liver that stores and releases bile.&lt;SEP&gt;The gallbladder stores and releases bile into the small intestine to help digest fats.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560467</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bone">
      <data key="d0">Bone</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The term "Bone" refers to the rigid organs that constitute part of the vertebrate endoskeleton, forming a vital component of the skeletal system. These bones are composed of living tissue that includes cells, collagen, blood vessels, and marrow, contributing to their strength, resilience, and ability to regenerate after damage. The skeleton, built from bones, provides structural support and facilitates movement, with specific regions such as the diaphysis and physis playing roles in growth and development.

In medical contexts, bones are significant as they are involved in various diagnostic and pathological processes. They can absorb tracers during imaging procedures, enabling the detection of damage or disease. Bones are also common sites for primary tumors or metastases, especially in the case of cancers such as prostate, thyroid, and liver cancer. Bone metastasis is a critical concern in oncology, classified as M1b in cancer staging, indicating distant metastasis, and often necessitates intervention due to associated complications and disease progression.

Furthermore, bones can be affected indirectly through cancer spread or radiation therapy, especially in thyroid cancer treatment, leading to side effects. They serve as a focal point for tumor invasion and tissue staging in mouth cancer, emphasizing their importance in both structural and clinical contexts. Additionally, bones are notable for their strength and lightness, with an inherent capacity for growth and regeneration, making them a vital component of vertebrate anatomy and a significant focus in medical diagnosis and treatment of various cancers and skeletal diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560467</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A tumor is an abnormal mass of tissue resulting from uncontrolled cell growth. It can be benign or malignant and is often associated with cancer development across various organs and tissues. Tumors are characterized by their location, size, shape, and degree of invasion into surrounding structures, with these features playing a crucial role in cancer staging, prognosis, and treatment decisions.

Tumors can be detected through imaging tests such as mammograms, X-rays, MRI, or endoscopic procedures, and they are often sampled via biopsies for diagnostic and molecular analysis. These analyses may include testing for specific markers like PDL1, HER2, hormone receptors, and genetic mutations, which assist in personalized treatment planning, including immunotherapy and targeted therapies.

The size of a tumor is typically measured in centimeters or millimeters and, along with the extent of local invasion into structures like vessels, bones, or adjacent tissues, influences the tumor's stage—an essential factor in determining prognosis and management. Tumor staging categories, such as T1 or T2, often reflect the size and invasion level, especially relevant in cancers like colorectal, ovarian, bile duct, and CNS tumors.

In specific contexts, such as prostate, breast, or rectal cancers, the tumor's growth pattern, stage, and molecular profile guide treatment choices. Tumors can also invade nearby organs or tissues, potentially leading to metastasis, which complicates treatment. For example, tumors in the brain, like gliomas, or in the oropharynx, can grow into adjacent tissues and require surgical removal or other interventions.

In summary, a tumor is a complex abnormal tissue growth with diverse characteristics influenced by its location, size, molecular features, and invasive behavior. These factors are vital in diagnosing, staging, and devising effective treatment strategies across a broad spectrum of cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560468</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bile">
      <data key="d0">Bile</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Bile is a yellow or green fluid made in the liver, containing cholesterol, bilirubin, salts, water, and other elements, aiding in fat digestion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560468</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Blood Supply">
      <data key="d0">Liver Blood Supply</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The liver receives blood from the hepatic artery and portal vein and releases it via the hepatic veins into the vena cava.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560469</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Liver Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Primary Liver Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary liver cancer originates in the liver tissue, with hepatocellular carcinoma being the most common type.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560469</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Secondary Liver Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Secondary Liver Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Secondary liver cancer occurs when other cancer types spread to the liver, such as metastatic colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560469</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatocellular Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Hepatocellular Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is the most common primary liver cancer in adults, sometimes occurring as fibrolamellar hepatocellular carcinoma (FLHC).&lt;SEP&gt;HCC is the most common primary liver cancer in adults and the main focus of the provided information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560469</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Intrahepatic Cholangiocarcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Intrahepatic Cholangiocarcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma is a primary liver cancer affecting the bile ducts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560469</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Liver Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver tumors include rare types like sarcomas and mixed tumors involving both hepatocellular carcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560469</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Cancer Spread">
      <data key="d0">Liver Cancer Spread</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver cancer can spread directly within the liver or to distant sites like lung and bone, termed metastatic liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560470</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatic Artery">
      <data key="d0">Hepatic Artery</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The hepatic artery supplies oxygen-rich blood directly to the liver from the systemic circulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560470</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Portal Vein">
      <data key="d0">Portal Vein</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The portal vein supplies nutrient-rich blood from the gastrointestinal tract to the liver.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560470</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatic Veins">
      <data key="d0">Hepatic Veins</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The hepatic veins carry blood away from the liver into the inferior vena cava.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560470</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Liver Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver cancer can spread directly through the liver or to distant organs such as the lung and bone, with hepatocellular carcinoma being the most common type.&lt;SEP&gt;Liver cancer includes all malignant tumors that originate in the liver, with HCC being the most common type.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560470</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Metastasis">
      <data key="d0">Metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastasis is the process by which cancer cells spread from their original, primary site to other parts of the body. This dissemination occurs through the bloodstream or lymphatic system, enabling cancer to establish secondary tumors in distant organs such as the lungs, bones, liver, lymph nodes, and other tissues. The spread often requires confirmation via biopsy of the metastatic lesions to verify the presence of cancer cells outside the primary tumor.

In the context of various cancers, such as prostate or stomach cancer, metastasis involves cancer cells traveling through blood vessels or lymphatic channels to reach and invade new areas. Imaging methods are frequently employed to assess the extent of metastasis, with specific classifications like M1b indicating metastasis to bones or other distant sites. The detection and confirmation of metastasis are critical for staging, treatment planning, and prognosis.

Overall, metastasis significantly impacts cancer prognosis and treatment strategies, representing the transition from localized to systemic disease. Its presence often indicates an advanced stage, necessitating comprehensive diagnostic procedures to identify affected organs and inform appropriate therapeutic approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560473</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cirrhosis">
      <data key="d0">Cirrhosis</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Cirrhosis is long-term scarring of the liver that can increase the risk of liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cirrhosis is a condition characterized by scarring of the liver tissue, serving as a risk factor for liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560473</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Liver">
      <data key="d0">Liver</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The liver is a vital and large organ located on the right side of the body, playing a crucial role in various metabolic, detoxification, and digestive functions. It is responsible for filtering blood, removing toxins, and processing nutrients, which are essential for maintaining overall health. The liver produces bile, aiding in the digestion of fats, and synthesizes enzymes and bilirubin, with these substances' levels measured through liver function tests to assess the organ's health.

As a central hub for metabolism and detoxification, the liver processes chemicals in the body and is involved in blood clotting, fat breakdown, and sugar regulation. Its importance is underscored by its involvement in multiple disease conditions, including liver cancer and metastatic spread of cancers originating elsewhere, such as esophageal, rectal, prostate, stomach, and ovarian cancers. In cases of advanced ovarian cancer (notably Stage 4B), metastasis to the liver is common. Similarly, the liver can be affected by metastases from rectal, stomach, and anal cancers, which are typically evaluated using imaging techniques such as MRI and CT scans. For prostate cancer, the liver can also be involved either as a site of metastasis or through biopsies taken to diagnose metastatic spread.

The health of the liver can be compromised by various diseases, including primary liver cancer and metastases, and is often assessed through physical examinations, imaging, and liver function tests. In addition to its metabolic and detoxification roles, the liver plays a key role in blood filtering and blood clotting functions, making it an integral component in maintaining physiological balance and responding to disease processes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560473</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatitis B Virus">
      <data key="d0">Hepatitis B Virus</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatitis B virus is an infection that can cause liver damage, cirrhosis, and increase liver cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560473</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph nodes are small, bean-shaped structures that are integral components of the lymphatic system. They serve primarily as immune system organs involved in filtering lymph fluid, trapping foreign particles, and facilitating immune responses against infections. They are commonly found throughout the body, particularly in regions such as the neck, armpits (axillary lymph nodes), groin, pelvis, abdomen, and near vital organs like the prostate, stomach, ovaries, and colon.

A key function of lymph nodes is their role as sites where immune cells, including B-cell lymphoblasts, proliferate and initiate immune responses. Due to their strategic location and filtering ability, lymph nodes are often involved in the spread (metastasis) of various cancers, including skin cancer, breast cancer, ovarian cancer, prostate cancer, lung cancer, throat, nasopharyngeal, stomach, and other malignancies. Enlargement of lymph nodes frequently indicates infection, inflammation, or cancer spread, and their assessment is vital in cancer staging and diagnosis.

Medical evaluation of lymph nodes includes physical examinations and imaging techniques to detect enlargement or abnormality, which may signal disease progression. During cancer surgeries, lymph nodes may be biopsied or removed to determine the extent of metastasis, guide treatment decisions, and stage the cancer accurately. For example, in ovarian and prostate cancers, lymph nodes affected by metastasis play a crucial role in prognosis and treatment planning.

In summary, lymph nodes are small, bean-shaped immune structures that filter lymph, harbor immune cells, and serve as critical pathways for the spread of various cancers. Their condition, whether enlarged or involved in metastasis, provides essential information for diagnosing, staging, and managing numerous diseases, particularly cancers affecting the skin, breast, reproductive organs, and other regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
