    <node id="Hepatitis C Virus">
      <data key="d0">Hepatitis C Virus</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatitis C virus is another infection linked to liver scarring, cirrhosis, and liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560475</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Alcohol Use">
      <data key="d0">Alcohol Use</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">High alcohol consumption damages the liver and is a risk factor for liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560475</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer is a broad disease characterized by the uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells that can invade, spread to, or metastasize within various tissues and organs throughout the body. This unchecked cell proliferation often leads to tissue damage, physical and mental health deterioration, and poses significant treatment challenges. Cancer encompasses numerous specific disease types, including skin cancers like cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC), Hodgkin lymphoma, diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, prostate cancer, thyroid cancer, leukemia, bladder cancer, and cancers of the cervix, anus, and other locations.

The progression and severity of cancer are assessed through staging systems such as the TNM system, which classifies cancers from stage 0 to stage 4 based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis. For example, intrahepatic bile duct cancer is staged according to tumor size (measured in centimeters or millimeters) and its spread to blood vessels, liver, or exterior structures. Similarly, other cancers may involve multiple stages reflecting their extent and progression.

Cancer can spread via mechanisms such as lymphatic and hematogenous routes, affecting lymph nodes, bones, lungs,brain, and other distant sites. Metastasis is a key feature of cancer, indicating the migration of malignant cells from their primary location to secondary sites, often complicating treatment and prognosis. The disease may recur after initial treatment, necessitating ongoing monitoring and sometimes multiple, complex therapies.

Detection and diagnosis of cancer involve various procedures including biopsy, genetic testing, imaging, PET scans (which highlight increased sugar uptake in cancer cells), and endoscopic examinations of the throat, esophagus, and airway. Treatments for cancer are diverse and may include surgery, radiation therapy, systemic therapies such as chemotherapy, targeted drugs, immunotherapy, and participation in clinical trials. Therapeutic approaches often depend on the cancer type, stage, and genetic characteristics, with some treatments aimed at destroying cancer cells directly, such as high-dose chemotherapy.

Risk factors for developing cancer include age, family history, genetic mutations, race, diet, lifestyle choices such as smoking, and exposure to carcinogens. Certain genetic conditions like chronic myeloid leukemia (CML), caused by chromosomal translocation, exemplify the genetic basis underlying some cancers. Additionally, some cancers, like colon cancer, often originate from precursor lesions such as polyps, which can be detected and removed to prevent progression.

Overall, cancer is a complex group of diseases involving abnormal cell growth that, without proper intervention, can invade neighboring tissues, spread to distant organs, and significantly impact the patient's health. Its diagnosis, staging, treatment, and monitoring are critical components of comprehensive cancer care, aiming to improve outcomes and quality of life for affected individuals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe827