    <node id="Stage 4A">
      <data key="d0">Stage 4A</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 4A signifies presence of cancer cells in the fluid around the lungs (malignant pleural effusion).&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 4A involves cancer cells in the fluid around the lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560479</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 4B">
      <data key="d0">Stage 4B</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 4B signifies spread of ovarian cancer to the inside of the liver or spleen, distant lymph nodes, or other organs outside the abdomen.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 4B involves spread to inside the liver or spleen, distant lymph nodes, or other organs outside the abdomen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560479</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphatic System">
      <data key="d0">Lymphatic System</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The lymphatic system is a vital component of the body's immune system, comprising a complex network of tissues, organs, and vessels that work together to defend against infections and diseases. It includes key structures such as lymph nodes, spleen, thymus, tonsils, lymph vessels, and bone marrow. These organs and tissues produce and transport lymphocytes—white blood cells that play critical roles in immune responses, including fighting infections and lymphomas such as CBCL.

A primary function of the lymphatic system is the circulation of lymph fluid through a network of vessels designed to drain excess interstitial fluid from tissues. This process helps maintain fluid balance within the body while enabling immune surveillance. The lymphatic vessels and lymph nodes act as filters, trapping pathogens, cancer cells, and other debris, and facilitating immune cell activation.

The lymphatic system's role extends to involvement in the spread of cancer, particularly in the metastasis of prostate cancer. It provides pathways for cancer cells to disseminate from primary tumors to distant sites. The system's ability to carry lymph, which may contain malignant cells, underscores its importance in both immune defense and the potential for cancer metastasis. Specifically, lymph nodes serve as critical sites for immune response activation and are often examined during cancer staging and treatment.

In summary, the lymphatic system is an integral part of the immune defense, supporting immune function through lymphocyte activity and fluid transport, and also playing a significant role in the dissemination of cancer cells, highlighting its importance in health and disease management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560480</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph">
      <data key="d0">Lymph</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph is a clear, watery fluid that plays a crucial role in the body's immune and nutrient transport systems. It primarily functions to carry lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell responsible for fighting infections, throughout the body via the lymphatic system. Lymph circulates through a network of lymph vessels and passes through lymph nodes, where it is filtered to help eliminate pathogens, such as bacteria and viruses, thereby supporting immune function.

In addition to its immune role, lymph transports essential nutrients and provides water to cells, aiding in overall cellular health and function. It can also carry tumor cells, which is significant in the context of cancer spread. Specifically, lymph's involvement in the lymphatic system makes it a pathway through which cancers like breast cancer can metastasize to other parts of the body. The fluid’s composition and circulation are vital for maintaining immune surveillance and tissue homeostasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560480</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Neoadjuvant Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Neoadjuvant Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Neoadjuvant therapy refers to chemotherapy given before surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Neoadjuvant therapy is preoperative systemic drug treatment given to reduce tumor size before surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560481</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood">
      <data key="d0">Blood</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood is a vital bodily tissue and fluid essential for maintaining numerous physiological functions in the human body. It is primarily composed of plasma, which serves as a transport medium, along with various cells including red blood cells (erythrocytes), white blood cells (leukocytes), and platelets. Blood's key roles include transporting oxygen from the lungs to body tissues, delivering nutrients such as glucose and amino acids, removing waste products like carbon dioxide and metabolic byproducts for excretion via the kidneys, and supporting immune responses through circulating immune cells such as lymphocytes. Additionally, blood contributes to clotting processes that prevent excessive bleeding and plays a role in the spread and containment of infections.

Blood serves as an essential component in medical diagnostics. Blood tests analyze blood samples to detect abnormalities, diagnose conditions like anemia, infections, and blood cancers such as leukemia and acute myeloid leukemia (AML). These tests include various assays like Complete Blood Count (CBC), Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP), alkaline phosphatase (ALP), and pregnancy tests, which assess organ function, organ damage, or the presence of disease markers. Blood is also a sample source for genetic testing and stem cell harvesting, especially in procedures like stem cell rescue therapies where stem cells are obtained from blood and later transfused.

In the context of cancer, particularly hematologic malignancies like leukemia, blood can contain abnormal cells such as blast cells (immature white blood cells) which can comprise at least 20% of the blood in certain cases of AML. Blood also functions as a conduit for cancer cells during metastasis, transporting circulating tumor cells through blood vessels to establish secondary sites of disease. Its circulation through organs like the liver can facilitate the spread of cancerous cells.

Overall, blood is an essential, complex tissue and fluid that sustains life by supporting oxygenation, nutrient delivery, waste removal, immune defense, and plays a crucial role in disease diagnosis, monitoring, and treatment processes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560481</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adjuvant Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Adjuvant Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Adjuvant chemotherapy refers to treatment given after surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
