    <node id="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemotherapy is a systemic cancer treatment that employs the use of drugs to kill or inhibit the growth of cancer cells throughout the body. It predominantly targets rapidly dividing cells, including various cancer types such as squamous cell skin cancer, ovarian cancer, leukemia, primary CNS lymphoma, cervical cancer, glioma, perianal cancers, and metastatic or recurrent cancers like anal cancer and anaplastic thyroid cancer. Chemotherapy drugs are often administered intravenously via infusion in cycles to allow the body to recover between treatments, although they can also be given orally or through intraperitoneal injections depending on the specific case.

A wide range of anti-cancer medications are used in chemotherapy regimens, including platinum compounds like cisplatin and carboplatin, as well as drugs such as cyclophosphamide, docetaxel, doxorubicin, mitomycin, capecitabine, and 5-FU. These drugs are frequently combined with other therapies such as radiation or targeted therapy, either concurrently or sequentially, to enhance treatment efficacy. Chemotherapy may be administered before surgery to shrink tumors, after surgery to eliminate residual cancer cells, or as the primary treatment when surgery isn't immediately feasible.

While effective in destroying cancer cells, chemotherapy also affects healthy, rapidly dividing cells, leading to side effects like nausea, fatigue, and hair loss. It is often used in combination with other treatments such as radiation (chemoradiation) and can be adapted for different modes of delivery depending on the cancer type and stage. The treatment plays a crucial role in managing metastatic, recurrent, or aggressive cancers, making it a cornerstone in oncology therapies across various cancer types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560487</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cisplatin">
      <data key="d0">Cisplatin</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug widely utilized in the treatment of various cancers, including squamous cell skin cancer, cervical cancer, bladder cancer, and anal cancer. It is often administered intravenously and incorporated into combination treatment regimens, such as with 5-fluorouracil (5-FU) or alongside radiation therapy, depending on the specific cancer type. For example, in cervical cancer, Cisplatin is commonly given weekly during chemoradiation, while in anal cancer, it is used in conjunction with 5-FU and radiation therapy.

As a potent chemotherapeutic agent, Cisplatin functions by damaging the DNA of cancer cells, thereby inhibiting their proliferation. However, its administration is associated with significant side effects, notably nephrotoxicity (kidney damage), which requires careful management and monitoring. Additional adverse effects include ototoxicity (ringing in the ears and hearing problems), bleeding, bruising, and nerve damage. These side effects are well-documented across various treatment protocols involving Cisplatin.

While Cisplatin is effective in tackling multiple cancer types, its efficacy may diminish if previously exposed to the drug in prior treatments. Despite its broad utility, healthcare providers consider its toxicity profile when planning treatment, balancing the benefits of cancer control against potential adverse effects. Overall, Cisplatin remains a cornerstone in oncology for its effectiveness, with ongoing research aimed at optimizing its use and mitigating its side effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560488</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Health Issues and Treatments">
      <data key="d0">Health Issues and Treatments</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A list of illnesses, injuries, and treatments that history provides to assist diagnosis and care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
