    <node id="Family Members">
      <data key="d0">Family Members</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Family members provide emotional, logistical, and practical support during health diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Family members or relatives with cancer history are relevant for assessing inherited cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood relatives providing health history information, including cancers, other diseases, and health issues.&lt;SEP&gt;Family members are blood relatives whose health history, including any instances of cancer or genetic testing, provides important information for assessing breast cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Relatives whose health histories are relevant for assessing hereditary risk for liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560490</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Physical Examination">
      <data key="d0">Physical Examination</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A routine assessment where healthcare providers check vital signs, body organs, and lymph nodes for abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical assessment involving physical checks, including size and condition of lymph nodes, spleen, and liver.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560490</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stomach">
      <data key="d0">Stomach</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The stomach is a digestive organ that connects with the esophagus at the esophagogastric junction.&lt;SEP&gt;The stomach is a digestive organ receiving food from the esophagus.&lt;SEP&gt;Stomach is a digestive organ involved in processing food, absorbing nutrients, and removing waste, with a layered wall structure including mucosa, submucosa, muscle, subserosa, and serosa.&lt;SEP&gt;The stomach is an organ in the digestive system that processes food using muscles, enzymes, and acids.&lt;SEP&gt;The stomach is a large, hollow muscular organ in the digestive system that secretes enzymes and acid for digestion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560490</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Digestive System">
      <data key="d0">Digestive System</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The digestive system includes organs like the esophagus, stomach, intestines, liver, gallbladder, and pancreas, and functions to break down food, absorb nutrients, and remove waste.&lt;SEP&gt;The organ system responsible for processing food, absorbing nutrients, and expelling waste, including the stomach and intestines.&lt;SEP&gt;The digestive system is responsible for taking in, breaking down food, absorbing nutrients, and removing waste from the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560491</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chyme">
      <data key="d0">Chyme</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chyme is the liquid mixture formed when food is broken down by the stomach's contractions, enzymes, and acid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560491</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Small Intestine">
      <data key="d0">Small Intestine</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The small intestine absorbs nutrients from chyme after digestion in the stomach.&lt;SEP&gt;The small intestine is a part of the digestive system where nutrients from chyme are absorbed into the bloodstream.&lt;SEP&gt;The small intestine is used to create the Indiana pouch and a part of it forms the one-way valve.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560491</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Large Intestine">
      <data key="d0">Large Intestine</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The large intestine reabsorbs water from remaining food matter and prepares waste for excretion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560491</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Esophagus">
      <data key="d0">Esophagus</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The esophagus is a muscular tube transporting food from the throat to the stomach, located behind the trachea.&lt;SEP&gt;The esophagus is a muscular tube that connects the throat (pharynx) with the stomach, consisting of multiple layers including mucosa, submucosa, muscularis propria, and tunica adventitia.&lt;SEP&gt;The esophagus is a swallowing tube examined during esophagoscopy for abnormal growths.&lt;SEP&gt;The esophagus is a muscular tube that transports food from the mouth to the stomach during digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;The esophagus is a muscular tube connecting the throat to the stomach through which food passes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560491</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Digestive System Components">
      <data key="d0">Digestive System Components</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The digestive system includes organs like the stomach, liver, gallbladder, pancreas, and intestines involved in digestion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560492</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stomach Wall">
      <data key="d0">Stomach Wall</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The stomach wall is a muscular layer lining the stomach, secreting enzymes and acid, and involved in digestion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560492</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stomach Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stomach Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stomach cancer is a malignant disease originating in the stomach lining, often starting as precancerous changes in the mucosa.&lt;SEP&gt;Most stomach cancers, called adenocarcinomas, originate from the inner lining cells that produce mucus.&lt;SEP&gt;A malignant tumor arising in the stomach, potentially spreading and affecting digestion and overall health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560492</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stomach Cancer Spread">
      <data key="d0">Stomach Cancer Spread</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stomach cancer can spread to nearby lymph nodes, blood vessels, organs like the liver, pancreas, and spleen, and can grow into vessels, forming ulcers or masses.&lt;SEP&gt;The process by which stomach cancer extends beyond its original site to surrounding tissues or other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560492</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Records">
      <data key="d0">Medical Records</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical records include health history, treatment details, and test outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Comprehensive compilation of patient’s test results, imaging, and treatment reports.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical records include test results, reports, and history that are essential for ongoing care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560492</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Organ">
      <data key="d0">Organ</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">An anatomical structure composed of tissues, evaluated in histology for disease diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;The organ refers to parts like the liver, stomach, lungs, etc., involved in bodily functions or disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560492</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN.org">
      <data key="d0">NCCN.org</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN.org is a website providing patient guidelines on cancer, including information about mouth cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is a website providing access to patient guidelines and information about cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is the website where the NCCN Guidelines for Patients for Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia and other cancers can be accessed.&lt;SEP&gt;Website providing information on fertility preservation for adolescents and young adults.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is a website providing patient feedback and health information related to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN.org website offers resources and guidelines for cancer patients and healthcare providers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560492</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Patientguidelines">
      <data key="d0">Patientguidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Patientguidelines is a resource offering guidelines for patients regarding health conditions and treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560492</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Throat">
      <data key="d0">Throat</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The throat is part of the respiratory tract through which air passes on its way to the lungs.&lt;SEP&gt;The throat is a hollow, muscular tube inside the neck that acts as a passageway for air, food, and liquid and includes structures such as the pharynx, larynx, and tonsils.&lt;SEP&gt;The general area inside the neck examined in various endoscopic procedures, including the larynx and hypopharynx.&lt;SEP&gt;Throat, also known as the oropharynx, is a part of the throat that includes the back of the roof of the mouth, the tonsils, the sides and back walls of the throat, and the base of the tongue.&lt;SEP&gt;The throat is the region near the windpipe, where enlarged thymus in T-ALL can cause breathing problems.&lt;SEP&gt;The throat is a bodily passage that includes the oropharynx.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560493</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Oropharynx">
      <data key="d0">Oropharynx</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The oropharynx is part of the throat that includes the back of the roof of the mouth, the tonsils, the side and back walls of the throat, and the base of the tongue.&lt;SEP&gt;The oropharynx is a part of the throat that includes the back of the roof of the mouth, the tonsils, sides and back walls of the throat, and the base of the tongue.&lt;SEP&gt;The oropharynx is a part of the throat examined during scoping procedures, located behind the mouth.&lt;SEP&gt;Part of the mouth and throat examined during panendoscopy.&lt;SEP&gt;Part of the throat behind the mouth that includes the back of the roof of the mouth, tonsils, and base of the tongue.&lt;SEP&gt;The oropharynx is a part of the throat, located inside the mouth, and is not always easily visible when opening the mouth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560493</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pharynx">
      <data key="d0">Pharynx</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The pharynx is a part of the throat that connects behind the nose to the larynx and esophagus, comprising three parts: nasopharynx, oropharynx, hypopharynx.&lt;SEP&gt;A muscular tube inside the neck that connects the back of the nose and mouth to the voice box and esophagus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560493</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Head and neck cancers are a group of cancers arising in the head or neck area, including cancers of the mouth, nose, throat, voice box, and surrounding structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancers are malignant growths occurring in the head and neck region, including the throat.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancers are a group of cancers that originate in the head or neck areas of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;A group of cancers originating in the head or neck area, including nasopharyngeal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560493</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Carcinoma of Head and Neck">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Carcinoma of Head and Neck</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell carcinoma of the head and neck (SCCHN or HNSCC) is the most common type of head and neck cancer that develops in the flat, thin cells of mucous membranes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560494</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Physical Exam">
      <data key="d0">Physical Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A physical exam is a comprehensive clinical procedure performed by healthcare providers to assess an individual's overall health status. This examination typically involves the measurement of vital signs such as blood pressure, pulse, respiration rate, and temperature, which provide essential information about the patient's physiological state. In addition to vital signs, the physical exam includes inspection and palpation of various body parts, including the head, neck, ears, nose, throat, and other organ systems, to detect any physical abnormalities or signs of disease.

Furthermore, a physical exam often entails thorough checks of specific regions like the oral cavity, breasts, lymph nodes, spleen, and liver. These assessments aim to identify abnormalities or signs indicative of conditions such as mouth or liver cancer, and to evaluate the function and health of internal organs. Specialized tools may sometimes be used to enhance the inspection and palpation process. The examination also involves evaluating physical abilities and general health metrics, which can aid in diagnosing a range of health issues.

Overall, the physical exam is an essential clinical procedure that combines visual inspection, physical palpation, and sometimes auscultation to gather comprehensive information about a patient’s health. It is performed during medical consultations to detect signs of illness, assess organ function, and identify potential health concerns, playing a vital role in preventive care, diagnosis, and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560494</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Throat Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Throat Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Throat cancer begins in the oropharynx and is primarily characterized by squamous cell carcinoma, affecting structures such as the soft palate, tonsils, and base of the tongue.&lt;SEP&gt;Throat cancer is a disease that can be diagnosed through testing and imaging, affecting the throat area.&lt;SEP&gt;Throat cancer is a type of cancer affecting the throat, often associated with HPV infection.&lt;SEP&gt;Throat cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the throat, with stages described using TNM scores and specific tumor characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560494</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Medical History">
      <data key="d0">Medical History</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Medical History refers to a comprehensive record of an individual's past health issues, illnesses, injuries, treatments, medications, and symptoms. This information is crucial for healthcare providers to accurately diagnose conditions, devise suitable treatment plans, and understand the patient's health background. The medical history encompasses details about previous illnesses, infections, injuries, medicines, supplements, and sometimes family medical history, which collectively inform clinical decision-making.

This record plays a vital role in various medical contexts, especially in diagnosing complex conditions such as those related to head and neck health, where detailed knowledge of past health issues can influence treatment options. By documenting past health issues and treatments, medical history aids healthcare professionals in establishing a thorough understanding of the patient's health trajectory, ensuring that medical interventions are appropriate and tailored to individual needs. Overall, maintaining an accurate and comprehensive medical history is fundamental for effective diagnosis, treatment planning, and ongoing healthcare management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560495</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Larynx">
      <data key="d0">Larynx</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The larynx, or voice box, is a structure within the throat involved in voice production and is a site where cancer can develop.&lt;SEP&gt;The larynx, or voice box, contains the vocal cords and is essential for speech; it is examined during laryngoscopy.&lt;SEP&gt;The voice box, examined during panendoscopy.&lt;SEP&gt;The larynx is a part of the body involved in the structure of cartilage and is mentioned in relation to cartilage tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560495</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Family History">
      <data key="d0">Family History</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Family history is a comprehensive record of health information pertaining to an individual's relatives, primarily focusing on inherited genetic predispositions and familial patterns of diseases. It encompasses details about various health conditions such as cancer (including breast, ovarian, pancreatic, colorectal, prostate, stomach, liver, and thyroid cancers), blood disorders like leukemia and chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL), heart disease, diabetes, and other hereditary ailments. Family history typically includes information about the types of diseases, ages at diagnosis, causes of death, and genetic testing results within a family, which collectively aid in assessing an individual's hereditary health risks.

This information plays a critical role in medical risk assessment and decision-making. For instance, a family history of specific cancers, such as prostate, breast, ovarian, or colorectal cancer, can increase an individual's likelihood of developing similar conditions, thereby influencing screening strategies, preventive measures, and treatment options. The presence of related genetic mutations or hereditary syndromes detected through family history can prompt considerations for germline testing and personalized medicine approaches. 

In particular, family history of leukemia, pancreatic, stomach, or head and neck cancers can inform diagnostic and treatment pathways. The pattern of disease inheritance, including the occurrence of cancers and genetic conditions among relatives, helps healthcare providers evaluate the hereditary component of disease risk. Overall, gathering comprehensive family health history allows for early identification of at-risk individuals, enables tailored health management plans, and informs preventive health strategies based on familial tendencies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560495</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tonsils">
      <data key="d0">Tonsils</data>
      <data key="d1">anatomicalstructure</data>
      <data key="d2">Tonsils are small masses of lymph tissue located at the back of the throat that trap pathogens entering through the nose or mouth.&lt;SEP&gt;Tonsils are lymphoid tissues located in the oropharynx that can be affected by cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560495</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Base of the Tongue">
      <data key="d0">Base of the Tongue</data>
      <data key="d1">anatomicalstructure</data>
      <data key="d2">The base of the tongue is part of the oropharynx and can be the site of tumor development in throat cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560495</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="p16 (HPV)">
      <data key="d0">p16 (HPV)</data>
      <data key="d1">biomarker</data>
      <data key="d2">p16 (HPV) testing is used as a biomarker to assist in diagnosing and determining prognosis of throat cancers related to HPV infection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560496</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms refer to patient-reported indicators and clinical signs suggesting the presence of various diseases or health conditions. The nature and presentation of symptoms depend heavily on the specific type and location of the disease. For example, symptoms of small cell lung carcinoma (SCLC) are influenced by tumor location and spread, often manifesting as breathing issues and symptoms associated with paraneoplastic syndromes. Similarly, in ovarian cancer, symptoms such as bloating, pelvic or abdominal pain, difficulty eating, and a feeling of fullness are common indicators.

Certain systemic symptoms are collectively known as B symptoms, which include fever, night sweats, fatigue, and weight loss, frequently associated with lymphomas. In prostate health, symptoms may include urinary issues such as increased frequency, difficulty urinating, blood in urine or semen, burning sensations, and sexual problems, which can also be associated with prostate cancer and impact quality of life. 

Urological symptoms like hematuria, frequent urination, and discomfort during urination can point to bladder cancer, while symptoms such as bone or back pain and unexplained weight loss may suggest metastasis or other advanced conditions.

In relation to neurological and central nervous system (CNS) disorders, primary CNS lymphoma and gliomas exhibit neurological symptoms like nerve changes, difficulty with speech or swallowing, headaches, nausea, seizures, personality, and memory changes, and coordination problems. Brain tumors often cause symptoms such as nausea, vomiting, vision disturbances, sensory and motor deficits, mood or personality alterations, and blood clots, reflecting pressure effects within the brain.

Liver cancer and other hepatic conditions may present with signs such as jaundice or other liver-related symptoms, although specific details are less explicitly described in the provided list.

Overall, symptoms serve as crucial clinical indicators that can encompass pain, neurological deficits, organ-specific signs, and systemic effects. These symptoms are key for disease detection, diagnosis, and monitoring, reflecting both tumor effects and possible treatment-related side effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560496</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mucus Membranes">
      <data key="d0">Mucus Membranes</data>
      <data key="d1">anatomicalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Mucus membranes are thin tissues in the mouth, nose, and throat where squamous cell carcinoma commonly develops.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560496</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nasal Cavity">
      <data key="d0">Nasal Cavity</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The nasal cavity is part of the head and neck region and is involved in the formation of head and neck cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;The space inside the nose that connects to the nasopharynx and is involved in nasopharyngeal carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560496</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Paranasal Sinuses">
      <data key="d0">Paranasal Sinuses</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Paranasal sinuses are air-filled spaces near the nose that can also harbor cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560496</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="High-Volume Center">
      <data key="d0">High-Volume Center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A high-volume center is a specialized medical facility with extensive experience in treating head and neck cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560496</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Carcinoma of the Head and Neck">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Carcinoma of the Head and Neck</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">It is the most common type of head and neck cancer, forming in the flat cells lining the mucous membranes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560497</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Colon">
      <data key="d0">Colon</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The colon is the large intestine responsible for water absorption and waste formation.&lt;SEP&gt;The colon is a part of the digestive system, specifically the large intestine, where polyps can form.&lt;SEP&gt;The colon is a major part of the large intestine where water is absorbed from food and stool is formed.&lt;SEP&gt;The colon is part of the large intestine where polyps can grow and where colon cancer can develop.&lt;SEP&gt;The colon is part of the large intestine, where polyps develop on the inner lining and can be surgically removed.&lt;SEP&gt;The colon is part of the large intestine involved in absorbing water and forming stool.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560497</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rectum">
      <data key="d0">Rectum</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The rectum is the final section of the large intestine, storing feces.&lt;SEP&gt;The rectum is the last part of the large bowel, responsible for holding stool until defecation.&lt;SEP&gt;The rectum is the last 6 inches of the large bowel where stool is stored until it exits the body through the anus.&lt;SEP&gt;The rectum is part of the lower digestive tract evaluated for cancer spread in MRI.&lt;SEP&gt;The rectum is the final section of the large intestine, storing stool before elimination.&lt;SEP&gt;The rectum is the last several inches of the large bowel, involved in stool storage before excretion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560497</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pancreas">
      <data key="d0">Pancreas</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The pancreas can be affected by metastatic esophageal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The pancreas produces digestive enzymes and hormones, located behind the stomach.&lt;SEP&gt;The pancreas is a digestive gland involved in enzyme and hormone production, affected by pancreatic cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The pancreas is a gland that helps digest food and control blood sugar, and is the primary organ affected by pancreatic cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The pancreas produces enzymes and hormones, including insulin, that assist in digestion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560497</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stomach Parts">
      <data key="d0">Stomach Parts</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The parts of the stomach include the cardia, fundus, body, antrum, and pylorus, each with specific functions during digestion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560497</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cardia">
      <data key="d0">Cardia</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The cardia is the opening where the esophagus connects to the stomach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560497</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fundus">
      <data key="d0">Fundus</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The fundus is the upper part of the stomach that stores undigested food and gases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560498</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Body">
      <data key="d0">Body</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to the inside of the body examined through imaging tests.&lt;SEP&gt;The body is the main part of the stomach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560498</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Antrum">
      <data key="d0">Antrum</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The antrum is the lower portion of the stomach where food mixes with enzymes and acids.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560498</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pylorus">
      <data key="d0">Pylorus</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The pylorus connects the stomach to the duodenum and functions as a valve.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560498</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gastroesophageal Junction">
      <data key="d0">Gastroesophageal Junction</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The gastroesophageal junction (GEJ) is where the esophagus connects to the stomach just below the diaphragm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560498</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Layers of Stomach Wall">
      <data key="d0">Layers of Stomach Wall</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The stomach wall comprises five layers: mucosa, submucosa, muscle layer, subserosa, and serosa.&lt;SEP&gt;The stomach wall consists of five main layers: mucosa, submucosa, muscle, subserosa, and serosa, which are involved in the process of cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560498</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mucosa">
      <data key="d0">Mucosa</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The innermost lining of the esophagus, composed of epithelium, lamina propria, and muscularis mucosa.&lt;SEP&gt;The mucosa is the inner membrane that contacts food, consisting of surface epithelium, lamina propria, and muscularis mucosae.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560499</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Surface Epithelium">
      <data key="d0">Surface Epithelium</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The surface epithelium forms the interior lining of the stomach and secretes mucus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560499</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lamina Propria">
      <data key="d0">Lamina Propria</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Lamina propria is a connective tissue layer found beneath epithelial tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;The lamina propria is the connective tissue layer beneath the epithelium in the oral mucosa.&lt;SEP&gt;The lamina propria is a connective tissue layer supporting stomach glands.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560499</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Muscularis Mucosae">
      <data key="d0">Muscularis Mucosae</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A thin muscle layer separating the mucosa from the submucosa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560499</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Submucosa">
      <data key="d0">Submucosa</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Layer of connective tissue below the mucosa, containing blood vessels and glands.&lt;SEP&gt;The submucosa contains connective tissue, blood vessels, nerve cells, and lymph vessels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560499</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Muscle Layer">
      <data key="d0">Muscle Layer</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A layer of tissue within the rectal wall; invasion indicates a T2 tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;The muscle layer helps move food through the stomach.&lt;SEP&gt;The bladder layer affected by invasive bladder cancer and targeted by specific therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560499</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Subserosa">
      <data key="d0">Subserosa</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A layer of connective tissue supporting the serosa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560499</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Serosa">
      <data key="d0">Serosa</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The serosa is the outer membrane covering the gallbladder and bile ducts, also called visceral peritoneum.&lt;SEP&gt;The serosa, also called the serous membrane or visceral peritoneum, is a thin membrane covering surfaces inside the body.&lt;SEP&gt;The serosa is an outer membrane that facilitates smooth movement of the stomach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560499</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Intestinal Type">
      <data key="d0">Intestinal Type</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The intestinal type of stomach adenocarcinoma forms tube- and gland-shaped structures and is more treatable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560499</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Diffuse Type">
      <data key="d0">Diffuse Type</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The diffuse type of stomach adenocarcinoma appears scattered over a wide area and is less common.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560499</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Esophageal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Esophageal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Esophageal cancer is a disease characterized by uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells in the esophageal epithelium, with two main types: esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and esophageal adenocarcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Esophageal cancer affects the esophagus and can involve HPV infection.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer originating in the esophagus or at the esophagogastric junction, often involving the stomach's cardia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560499</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Esophagogastric Junction">
      <data key="d0">Esophagogastric Junction</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The esophagogastric junction (EGJ), also called the gastroesophageal junction (GEJ), is where the stomach and esophagus meet just beneath the diaphragm.&lt;SEP&gt;The esophagogastric junction (EGJ) is where the esophagus connects to the stomach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560500</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Plan">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A treatment plan is a comprehensive, individualized strategy created to guide the management and therapy of a patient's medical condition, particularly cancer. It is developed based on diagnostic tests, imaging studies, biopsy results, disease staging, and risk assessments. These plans are tailored to the specific subtype of the disease, patient health status, and personal preferences, ensuring that interventions such as surgery, chemotherapy, radiation, or active surveillance are appropriate and effective.

In the context of oncology, a treatment plan encompasses various modalities, including surgical procedures, medication regimens, clinical trials, and radiation therapy. For example, in Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML), the treatment plan involves a personalized schedule of therapies determined by test results, with considerations for disease subtype and patient-specific factors. Similarly, for primary Central Nervous System lymphoma, the plan includes diagnostic assessments and spread evaluation to inform therapy choices.

Specialized treatment plans, such as those in brachytherapy, are designed using advanced imaging techniques to optimize applicator placement and radiation dose distribution. Overall, a treatment plan not only details the current course of medical interventions but also describes treatment goals, potential outcomes, and possible adjustments based on ongoing assessments and new information. This personalized and adaptable approach aims to achieve the most effective management tailored to each patient's unique clinical situation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560500</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Early-Stage Stomach Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Early-Stage Stomach Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Early-stage stomach cancer is confined to the mucosa, the innermost layer, and has not extended beyond.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560500</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Precancerous Changes">
      <data key="d0">Precancerous Changes</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Precancerous alterations occur in the stomach's mucosa and often progress slowly without early symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560500</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Vessels">
      <data key="d0">Blood Vessels</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood vessels are channels that can carry cancer cells from the primary tumor to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood vessels can serve as pathways for esophageal cancer to spread to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood vessels are channels that carry blood and can serve as pathways for cancer cell dissemination during metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Vessels supplying blood to and from the breast tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood vessels in and around the thyroid are areas where follicular and oncocytic thyroid cancers may invade, impacting diagnosis and staging.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood vessels are structures that carry blood; stomach cancer can invade nearby veins and arteries to spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560500</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ulcer">
      <data key="d0">Ulcer</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">An ulcer is an open sore that can develop in the stomach wall due to cancer growth or other causes, and can cause symptoms or complications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560500</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN">
      <data key="d0">NCCN</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is a prominent organization composed of leading cancer centers that develops and disseminates evidence-based clinical practice guidelines for the diagnosis, treatment, and management of various cancers. These guidelines cover a broad spectrum of cancer types, including lung cancer, breast cancer, colorectal cancer, and primary CNS lymphoma, providing directives on screening, diagnosis, treatment options, and patient care. The NCCN offers comprehensive resources tailored not only for healthcare providers but also for patients, including information on immunotherapy side effects, fertility preservation, survivorship, and enrollment in clinical trials.

Specifically, the NCCN provides detailed guidelines on skin cancer follow-up and surveillance schedules, as well as specific recommendations for lung cancer screening, management, and understanding different lung cancer types, symptoms, and causes. For breast cancer, the organization supplies diagnostic and treatment guidelines, ensuring standardized care across different clinical settings. Moreover, the NCCN emphasizes cancer survivorship and treatment, offering guidance to enhance patient outcomes following initial treatment.

In addition to treatment protocols, the NCCN recommends participating in clinical trials for certain cancers, such as primary central nervous system (CNS) lymphoma, to advance research and improve therapeutic options. Overall, the NCCN's mission is to improve the quality, effectiveness, and efficiency of cancer care through the development of comprehensive, evidence-based guidelines and resources that support both clinicians and patients in making informed decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560501</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mass">
      <data key="d0">Mass</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A mass in the stomach refers to an abnormal growth or tumor that may be associated with stomach cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560501</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Peritoneum">
      <data key="d0">Peritoneum</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The peritoneum is the abdominal lining that can be involved in metastasis of stomach cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560501</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Stomach Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Stomach Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic stomach cancer describes advanced disease where cancer has spread beyond the stomach to distant organs or tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560501</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="H. pylori">
      <data key="d0">H. pylori</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">H. pylori is a bacteria associated with increased risk of stomach cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560501</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Planning">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Planning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment planning begins with test results to determine appropriate medical interventions for throat cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment planning involves assessing the stage of the disease and medical factors to decide on surgery or drug treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment planning involves using test results and patient information to develop a personalized approach to managing breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Includes steps like simulation, CT imaging, and software to develop precise radiation treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment planning for stomach cancer begins with test results to guide therapy options like surgery, chemotherapy, or targeted therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of designing a medical approach based on diagnostic findings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560501</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Glioma">
      <data key="d0">Glioma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Glioma is a type of tumor that grows in the brain from glial cells, representing the most common malignant brain tumor in adults.&lt;SEP&gt;Glioma is a type of tumor that develops from glial cells in the brain or spinal cord and can invade normal brain tissue, making it difficult to treat.&lt;SEP&gt;Gliomas are brain tumors that can be characterized and identified by specific biomarkers, affecting diagnosis and treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of brain tumor originating from glial cells, characterized by specific histology and genetic markers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560501</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Astrocytoma">
      <data key="d0">Astrocytoma</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Astrocytomas are a common type of glioma made of star-shaped glial cells called astrocytes, which maintain the chemical environment for nerve communication.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of glioma characterized by tumor cells resembling astrocytes, often linked to mutations in the IDH gene.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560503</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Oligodendroglioma">
      <data key="d0">Oligodendroglioma</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Oligodendrogliomas are a type of glioma composed of oligodendrocytes, glial cells that protect electrical signaling in nerve cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A glioma type characterized by tumor cells resembling oligodendrocytes, identifiable under microscopy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560503</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Glioblastoma">
      <data key="d0">Glioblastoma</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Glioblastomas are the most destructive and dangerous form of glioma tumor, originating from precursor cells developing into astrocyte-like or oligodendrocyte-like cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Glioblastoma is an aggressive type of glioma and the most common and malignant form, known for its rapid growth and high recurrence rate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560503</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Glial Cells">
      <data key="d0">Glial Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Glial cells are supportive cells in the brain from which gliomas develop.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560503</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Spinal Cord">
      <data key="d0">Spinal Cord</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The spinal cord can be another site of glioma development, although less common.&lt;SEP&gt;The spinal cord is part of the central nervous system that can be affected by gliomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560503</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Normal Cells">
      <data key="d0">Normal Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Normal cells refer to healthy cells in the body affected alongside cancer cells by systemic therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Normal cells follow the body's rules, increasing only when needed and dying when damaged or old, unlike cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Cells that are non-cancerous, their similarity or dissimilarity to abnormal cells influences tumor grading.&lt;SEP&gt;Healthy cells used as a reference point for comparison with cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560503</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Normal Brain Tissue">
      <data key="d0">Normal Brain Tissue</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Normal brain tissue is the healthy part of the brain affected by glioma invasion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560504</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Astrocytomas">
      <data key="d0">Astrocytomas</data>
      <data key="d1">type</data>
      <data key="d2">Astrocytomas are a common type of glioma arising from astrocytes, a type of glial cell.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560504</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Oligodendrogliomas">
      <data key="d0">Oligodendrogliomas</data>
      <data key="d1">type</data>
      <data key="d2">Oligodendrogliomas are gliomas originating from oligodendrocytes, a type of glial cell.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560504</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Support Cells">
      <data key="d0">Support Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Glial cells support nerve cell function in the brain and spinal cord, and are the origin of gliomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560504</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Tests">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Procedures including blood, marrow, imaging, and genetic tests to diagnose AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Tests such as imaging scans, biopsies, and genetic tests used to confirm the presence of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Tests such as imaging, biopsy, and lab testing are used to diagnose and determine the type of glioma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560504</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Prognosis">
      <data key="d0">Prognosis</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Prognosis refers to the clinical assessment of the likely course and outcome of a disease, providing an estimate of the future progression, survival prospects, and overall trajectory of the condition. This prediction is based on various factors including the disease stage, tumor type and grade, genetic and biomarker information, treatment response, and the patient's overall health status. 

In the context of cancer, prognosis helps predict how the disease will develop over time, the likelihood of survival, and potential responses to treatment. For example, prognosis in prostate cancer, glioma, bladder cancer, and acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is often determined by specific characteristics such as tumor staging, genetic markers, and associated biomarkers like beta-2 microglobulin. Specifically, prognosis for AML can be influenced by genetic and biomarker data, while for prostate cancer, it involves considerations of tumor progression and expected outcomes.

Overall, prognosis serves as an essential tool in guiding clinical decision-making, informing patients about their expected disease course, and tailoring treatment strategies accordingly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560507</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Brain">
      <data key="d0">Brain</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The brain is a vital organ within the central nervous system (CNS) responsible for regulating a wide array of functions, including thinking, speech, movement, sensation, perception, behavior, and bodily processes. It is housed within the skull and serves as the primary control center of the body. The brain can be visualized in imaging studies, such as PET scans, where it may show bright spots that are generally considered normal.

As a common site for primary CNS lymphoma, the brain's deep tissue regions are particularly susceptible, with tumors developing in areas like the gliomas, often located in the upper parts of the brain. These tumors can impair various functions depending on their specific location, leading to symptoms such as weakness, cognitive and speech difficulties, and vision problems. The development of such tumors within the brain may require careful management, as surgical removal is often risky given the tissue's delicate nature.

The brain can also be affected by metastatic spread from other cancers, such as thyroid cancer, which may necessitate treatments like radiation therapy to control the disease. Its role as an organ susceptible to various pathological conditions underscores its importance and vulnerability within the central nervous system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560508</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mutation">
      <data key="d0">Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mutation refers to a change or error in the genetic code or DNA sequence of an organism's genes. These mutations can manifest as various types of genetic alterations, including deletions, insertions, inversions, amplifications, translocations, and point mutations. Such genetic changes may occur spontaneously or be inherited, and they have the potential to impair the normal function of genes by damage or modification of DNA sequences.

Mutations can have significant biological consequences, influencing cell behavior and contributing to disease processes such as cancer. In the context of oncology, mutations may alter tumor behavior and affect responses to treatment. Certain mutations are associated with abnormal cell growth and proliferation, which can lead to the formation of tumors such as gliomas. These genetic abnormalities can cause normal cells to become cancerous, often resulting in abnormal cell growth and proliferation.

Mutations can be classified broadly into hereditary (germline) mutations, which are inherited and present in every cell, and acquired (somatic) mutations, which occur spontaneously in specific cells during a person's lifetime. Both types of mutations have the potential to impact gene function and contribute to the development of diseases, notably cancer. Overall, mutations are fundamental genetic abnormalities that can lead to various health conditions, influence disease progression, and affect therapeutic outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560508</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Adenocarcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenocarcinoma is a type of cancer characterized by its origin from glandular cells that produce mucus or secretory substances. It is the most common form of non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), developing from the epithelial cells lining the alveoli in the lungs, which are responsible for gas exchange and mucus production. Beyond its pulmonary manifestation, adenocarcinoma frequently occurs in other organs where glandular epithelium is present.

In the gastrointestinal tract, adenocarcinoma commonly arises from benign glandular structures such as adenomas or polyps. For example, in the colon, adenocarcinoma develops from adenomatous polyps, transitioning from pre-cancerous lesions into invasive cancer. Similarly, in the stomach, gastric adenocarcinomas originate from mucus-secreting cells lining the stomach wall, making it the predominant subtype of stomach cancer. These cancers tend to proliferate invasively, often requiring specific treatment strategies tailored to their location and histology.

In the female reproductive system, adenocarcinoma can develop from glandular cells of the endocervix, specifically arising in the cervical canal. It represents a significant subset of cervical cancers and shares histological features with adenocarcinomas of other organs. In the urinary bladder, adenocarcinoma is relatively rare but can originate from glandular tissue within the bladder lining, which is not typical for most bladder cancers that are usually urothelial in origin.

Furthermore, adenocarcinoma can develop in other organs such as the rectum, where it arises from adenomas, which are benign glandular growths that can transform into invasive cancer. The term also encompasses other specific types like anal adenocarcinoma, which may require distinct treatment approaches due to its unique location and pathology.

Overall, adenocarcinoma is a malignant tumor arising from glandular epithelial cells across various organs, often linked to pre-existing benign lesions or glandular tissue, with diverse clinical presentations depending on the site of origin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560508</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lab Testing">
      <data key="d0">Lab Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Lab testing includes blood tests, imaging, and other assessments to aid in diagnosis and staging.&lt;SEP&gt;Laboratory tests analyze tumor tissue or fluid to support diagnosis and guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560509</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-up">
      <data key="d0">Follow-up</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Periodic follow-up visits and additional rounds of treatment are recommended due to glioma recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Follow-up involves scheduled medical assessments, imaging, and examinations to monitor for cancer recurrence after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560510</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Survival Statistics">
      <data key="d0">Survival Statistics</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Survival statistics are derived from research conducted years ago and may not reflect current outcomes due to advances in diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560510</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Brain Function Problems">
      <data key="d0">Brain Function Problems</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Gliomas can cause various neurological issues, such as slower thinking, memory loss, difficulty moving or speaking, personality changes, and loss of self.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560510</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Caregivers">
      <data key="d0">Caregivers</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Caregivers provide physical, emotional, and practical support to patients with gliomas, assisting with appointments and treatment management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560510</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Research">
      <data key="d0">Research</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Research is conducted to determine the best treatment approaches, including surgery and drug regimens based on cancer markers.&lt;SEP&gt;The latest scientific studies and methods conducted at top cancer centers that inform best practices for prostate cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Research is ongoing into new therapies and clinical trials aimed at improving glioma treatment and patient outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560510</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Support">
      <data key="d0">Support</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Caregivers and loved ones should gather support, help with treatment, and use resources to assist patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Providing support to patients with gliomas involves managing physical, mental, emotional, and logistical challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Support refers to emotional, social, and practical assistance available to cancer survivors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560510</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gliomas">
      <data key="d0">Gliomas</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Gliomas are a type of brain tumor that can cause symptoms like nausea, vomiting, vision problems, sensory disturbances, and blood clots, and are diagnosed using imaging techniques such as MRI and CT.&lt;SEP&gt;Gliomas are a type of brain tumor classified into grades 1 through 4 based on their abnormality and aggressiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560510</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CT">
      <data key="d0">CT</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Computed Tomography (CT) employs X-ray imaging to produce detailed cross-sectional images to identify cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Computed tomography (CT) uses X-rays and contrast to produce detailed images of colon cancer and other internal structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging technique for visualizing internal structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Computed Tomography (CT) scans are employed to assist in the accurate placement of applicators during brachytherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Computed tomography (CT) scan uses X-rays and computer processing to generate 3D images of the body, alternative to MRI when MRI is unavailable or not recommended.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560510</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Resection">
      <data key="d0">Resection</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical removal of a larger portion of the rectum when polyps or cancer are extensive.&lt;SEP&gt;Resection involves surgically removing the tumor or as much of it as possible, potentially alleviating symptoms and extending survival.&lt;SEP&gt;Resection is a surgical procedure to remove bladder tissue for diagnostic or treatment purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560511</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stereotactic Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Stereotactic Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A stereotactic biopsy is a specialized procedure performed by a neurosurgeon to obtain tissue samples from hard-to-reach brain tumors like primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A stereotactic biopsy involves inserting a thin, hollow needle into the brain to extract tumor tissue for testing.&lt;SEP&gt;A precise biopsy technique used for hard-to-reach or critical brain tumor areas.&lt;SEP&gt;A precise biopsy technique using a frame or markers to guide a small, hollow needle into a hard-to-reach or vital area of the brain to remove a tissue sample.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560511</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Open Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Open Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An open surgical procedure to remove tissue or tumors to confirm histology in cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgical biopsy involving opening the skull to obtain tissue, used when accessible and safe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560511</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Neurosurgeon">
      <data key="d0">Neurosurgeon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A neurosurgeon is a medical specialist performing stereotactic biopsy procedures to diagnose brain tumors such as primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A neurosurgeon is a medical specialist who performs surgical procedures like biopsies and tumor resections.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical specialist trained to perform brain surgery, including biopsies and tumor removals in an operating room.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560511</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Clots">
      <data key="d0">Blood Clots</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood clots, or deep vein thrombosis, are risks associated with surgery, especially in cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood clots associated with high-grade gliomas can occur in the legs and lungs, causing swelling, chest pain, and shortness of breath.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560511</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Brain Functions">
      <data key="d0">Brain Functions</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Different areas of the brain control various functions: parietal lobe (intelligence, language, sensation), occipital lobe (vision, color perception).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560511</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nausea">
      <data key="d0">Nausea</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Nausea is a neurological symptom associated with increased intracranial pressure due to lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A side effect involving a feeling of sickness during radiation treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Nausea is a feeling of sickness that can be caused by increased pressure from a brain tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;A common side effect of chemotherapy characterized by feeling sick.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560511</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vomiting">
      <data key="d0">Vomiting</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Vomiting can occur as a neurological symptom of increased pressure in the CNS caused by lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Vomiting, or throwing up, can result from brain tumor effects such as increased pressure or irritation.&lt;SEP&gt;A side effect of chemotherapy involving forceful expulsion of stomach contents.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560511</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Parietal Lobe">
      <data key="d0">Parietal Lobe</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The parietal lobe of the brain is involved in intelligence, language, reading, sensation, and sensory perception.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560512</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Occipital Lobe">
      <data key="d0">Occipital Lobe</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The occipital lobe is responsible for vision and color perception.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560512</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Neurological Exam">
      <data key="d0">Neurological Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Neurological exams involve tests of alertness, balance, reflexes, and senses to evaluate neurological function related to primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A neurological exam involves tests to evaluate alertness, thinking, balance, coordination, reflexes, strength, hearing, and senses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560512</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Reconstruction">
      <data key="d0">Reconstruction</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The process of surgical removal of the tumor or part of it, potentially providing symptom relief and pathological information.</data>
