    <node id="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation therapy is a widely used cancer treatment modality that employs high-energy radiation, such as X-rays, gamma rays, protons, or electrons, to destroy or damage cancer cells. It can be administered through various techniques, including external beam radiation therapy (EBRT), internal methods like brachytherapy, or advanced approaches such as intensity-modulated radiation therapy (IMRT). The primary purpose of radiation therapy is to kill cancer cells, shrink tumors, and alleviate pain caused by cancer, often in a targeted manner to minimize damage to surrounding healthy tissues.

Radiation therapy is utilized in multiple clinical scenarios. It is frequently employed after surgery to eradicate residual cancer cells, especially when complete surgical removal is not feasible or when cancer recurs. It serves as an alternative or adjunct treatment for cases where surgery is contraindicated or insufficient, including specific cancers such as basal cell skin cancer, prostate cancer, cervical cancer, gliomas, bladder cancer, and cancers of the breast, chest wall, lymph nodes, or brain. In some cases, radiation therapy is used alone, while in others, it complements systemic therapies like chemotherapy, particularly for unresectable tumors.

The delivery of radiation therapy involves precise targeting of affected areas, which can include entire regions such as the brain, or specific tumor sites like the eye or prostate. The treatment is typically administered in short, repetitive sessions, with considerations for potential side effects, which vary depending on the treated area. For example, external beam radiation for cervical cancer may impact ovarian function, and treatments near the neck can cause side effects affecting that region. Radiation therapy's benefits and risks must be carefully weighed, especially due to concerns like nerve damage or tissue toxicity. As an advanced technique, IMRT allows for precise dose modulation, improving effectiveness and sparing healthy tissues.

In summary, radiation therapy is a versatile and essential cancer treatment that leverages high-energy radiation to destroy tumor cells, reduce tumor burden, and manage pain. Its varied applications, from external beams to internal implants, and its combination with other therapies, make it a cornerstone of oncologic care across numerous cancer types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560515</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging">
      <data key="d0">Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging encompasses a range of diagnostic techniques used to produce detailed internal images of the body for detecting, staging, and monitoring various cancers. These techniques include commonly used modalities such as computed tomography (CT), magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), positron emission tomography (PET), ultrasound, mammography, dental X-rays, and bone scans. Its primary purpose is to visualize internal structures to identify tumors, assess their size and extent, locate metastases, and guide biopsies, particularly of lymph nodes and metastatic sites.

In the context of cancer diagnosis and management, imaging plays a vital role in evaluating specific cancers such as pancreatic, prostate, gliomas of the brain or spinal cord, cervical, CNS lymphoma, and anal cancers. For example, MRI and CT scans are crucial for identifying primary CNS lymphoma, guiding treatment decisions, and monitoring for recurrence or spread of lymphoma outside the CNS and eyes. Imaging also aids in detecting prostate cancer, assessing tumor spread within the body, and evaluating the extent of localized or metastatic disease.

Furthermore, imaging techniques such as PET, especially FDG-PET/CT, are valuable in detecting recurrent disease, determining cancer stage, and planning surgical interventions. In specific cases like cervical cancer, imaging helps ascertain tumor size, extent, and whether the cancer has spread beyond the primary site. For cancers requiring surveillance, such as stage II and III cancers, imaging (including MRI and CT scans) is employed to monitor for recurrence post-treatment, often with recommended yearly scans over several years.

Overall, imaging provides vital visual information that informs diagnosis, staging, treatment planning, and ongoing monitoring in oncology. The combination of different imaging modalities offers comprehensive insights into tumor characteristics and disease progression, making it an indispensable component of cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560515</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Molecular (Genetic) Testing">
      <data key="d0">Molecular (Genetic) Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Laboratory analysis to identify genetic mutations and features of the tumor, such as biomarkers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560515</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Histology">
      <data key="d0">Histology</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Histology, histopathology, or hematopathology review involves examining bone marrow samples under a microscope to diagnose leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Histology is the study of tissues viewed under a microscope, crucial for diagnosing tumor subtypes and guiding treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;Histology refers to the microscopic examination of tissue samples, often used in the diagnosis of AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Histology is the microscopic study of cells, tissues, and organs used to make treatment decisions in cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Histology involves the microscopic examination of tissue samples, such as those obtained via biopsy, to determine cancer type and characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Histology examines tumor cell appearance to determine grade and aggressiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Microscopic examination of tumor tissue to classify cell type and structure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560515</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cell">
      <data key="d0">Cell</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Basic unit of life, studied in histology for understanding cellular structures in tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Basic unit of life in tissue, examined under a microscope to classify tumor type.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560516</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Astrocyte">
      <data key="d0">Astrocyte</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of glial cell that appears in certain gliomas, such as astrocytomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560516</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Oligodendrocyte">
      <data key="d0">Oligodendrocyte</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A glial cell type that appears in oligodendrogliomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560516</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Low-grade Gliomas">
      <data key="d0">Low-grade Gliomas</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Gliomas classified as grades 1 and 2, generally growing slowly and less aggressive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560517</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="High-grade Gliomas">
      <data key="d0">High-grade Gliomas</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Gliomas classified as grades 3 and 4, more aggressive and rapidly growing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560517</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="IDH Mutant">
      <data key="d0">IDH Mutant</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A biomarker indicating a mutation in the IDH gene, associated with certain gliomas such as astrocytomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560517</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Histologic Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Histologic Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An examination process where tumor cells are analyzed under a microscope to classify disease and tumor type.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560517</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Laboratory">
      <data key="d0">Laboratory</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A laboratory is a medical facility where tissue samples are analyzed for cancerous cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A facility where tissue samples are analyzed to determine tumor type, malignancy, and genetic features.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560517</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Abdominal and Pelvic Exam">
      <data key="d0">Abdominal and Pelvic Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A physical examination involving palpation of the abdomen and pelvis to assess organ size, shape, presence of pain, or fluid buildup related to ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560517</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Transabdominal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Transabdominal Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An ultrasound procedure where a probe is moved across the abdomen with gel to visualize reproductive organs, useful for ovarian tumor detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560517</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Transvaginal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Transvaginal Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An ultrasound procedure where a probe is inserted into the vagina to get clearer images of ovaries and detect masses.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound imaging via the vaginal route for pelvic assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;Transvaginal ultrasound involves inserting a probe into the vagina to visualize the cervix and surrounding areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560517</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Computed Tomography (CT)">
      <data key="d0">Computed Tomography (CT)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">CT scan is an imaging technique used to assess the spread of cancer to nearby lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;An imaging technique that uses X-rays to produce detailed 3D images of the abdomen, pelvis, and chest, useful for detecting spread of ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A imaging technique that uses contrast to produce detailed cross-sectional images.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560517</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI)">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An imaging technique utilizing radio waves and magnets to produce detailed images of internal body structures without radiation exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI is an imaging technique that uses powerful magnets to visualize the inside of the body without radiation, with variations known as closed and open MRI designs.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of imaging that uses magnetic fields and contrast agents to create detailed images.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560517</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Positron Emission Tomography (PET)">
      <data key="d0">Positron Emission Tomography (PET)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An imaging test that, when combined with CT or MRI, measures cellular activity, such as sugar uptake, to detect cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560517</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Speculum">
      <data key="d0">Speculum</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical instrument used to view the vagina and cervix during pelvic examinations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560518</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pap Test">
      <data key="d0">Pap Test</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A screening procedure that involves collecting cells from the cervix to detect cervical cancer or pre-cancer, not ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560518</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cervix">
      <data key="d0">Cervix</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervix is the lower part of the uterus that connects to the vagina and plays a role in reproduction and childbirth.&lt;SEP&gt;The lower part of the uterus that connects to the vagina, examined during pelvic exams and through Pap tests to identify cervical cancer or precancerous changes.&lt;SEP&gt;The cervix is the narrow, lower part of the uterus, connecting to the vagina and involved in cervical cancer development.&lt;SEP&gt;The cervix is the lower part of the uterus connecting to the vagina, comprising the ectocervix and endocervix.&lt;SEP&gt;The cervix is the lower part of the uterus that connects to the vagina, and is the site of cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The cervix is part of the female reproductive system, examined in MRI scans for cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;The cervix is the lower part of the uterus, a site for cervical cancer development and lymph node involvement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560518</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Uterus">
      <data key="d0">Uterus</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The uterus is a female reproductive organ where the fetus develops during pregnancy and is involved in menstruation.&lt;SEP&gt;An organ in the female pelvis involved in reproductive functions, assessed during pelvic exams.&lt;SEP&gt;The uterus is where a baby develops; it connects with the cervix.&lt;SEP&gt;The uterus is an internal reproductive organ that can be visualized clearly by MRI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560518</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">An abnormal growth in the ovary that can be benign or malignant, detected through imaging tests such as ultrasound, CT, or MRI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560519</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Uterine Lining">
      <data key="d0">Uterine Lining</data>
      <data key="d1">anatomicalpart</data>
      <data key="d2">The innermost layer of the uterus which may be biopsied to rule out uterine cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560519</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rectovaginal Exam">
      <data key="d0">Rectovaginal Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A physical examination that assesses the space between the rectum and vagina for abnormalities related to ovarian or pelvic cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560519</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumors associated with FL can form in lymph nodes, spleen, liver, bone marrow, or other tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Growths that can affect hearing and may be impacted by head and neck treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumors are abnormal tissue masses that vary in shape and size, which complicates direct comparison between tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumors in the head and neck regions can impact hearing capacity.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumors refer to abnormal growths of tissue, which can occur in the liver, lungs, or bone and require treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumors are abnormal growths that can be benign or malignant, with different grades indicating their severity and behavior in tissue invasion and growth rate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560519</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 1 Gliomas">
      <data key="d0">Grade 1 Gliomas</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade 1 gliomas are slow-growing, usually benign tumors more common in children, often treatable with surgery alone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560520</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 2 Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Grade 2 Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade 2 tumor cells look somewhat abnormal, grow slowly, can invade tissue, and may recur as higher-grade gliomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560520</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 3 Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Grade 3 Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade 3 tumor cells are markedly abnormal, grow rapidly, invade tissue, and require additional therapy after surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560520</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 4 Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Grade 4 Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade 4 tumor cells are very abnormal, grow and spread quickly, with glioblastomas being the most common type.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560520</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Brain Tissue">
      <data key="d0">Brain Tissue</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep within the brain, primary CNS lymphoma develops, making surgical removal risky.&lt;SEP&gt;Sampled tissue from glioma patients used for biomarker testing to guide diagnosis and treatment plans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560520</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Instructions">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Instructions</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">DNA contains instructions that, when mutated, may lead to abnormal cell growth and cancer, including gliomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560520</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarker Test">
      <data key="d0">Biomarker Test</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomarker tests analyze tissue or blood samples to detect specific molecular markers for glioma classification.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560520</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic lymphocytic leukemia affects white blood cells in the bone marrow, contributing to immune suppression.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) is a cancer characterized by the accumulation of abnormal lymphocytes, primarily in the blood and bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of blood cancer characterized by the accumulation of monoclonal B lymphocytes in blood, lymph nodes, and marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;CLL is a type of blood cancer characterized by the abnormal growth of white blood cells, affecting blood counts and immune function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560520</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="SLL">
      <data key="d0">SLL</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">Small lymphocytic lymphoma (SLL) is a form of cancer similar to CLL, mainly found in lymph nodes and the spleen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560521</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph System">
      <data key="d0">Lymph System</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The lymph system includes lymph nodes, vessels, spleen, tonsils, and thymus, playing a key role in fighting infections and involved in CLL/SLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560521</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Performance Status">
      <data key="d0">Performance Status</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Performance Status (PS) is an assessment that measures a person's overall health, general well-being, and ability to perform daily activities. It is widely used in medical settings, particularly in oncology, to evaluate a patient's physical functioning and functional capacity. By assessing these aspects, healthcare providers can make informed decisions regarding treatment options and planning.

Performance status is often quantified using standardized scales such as the Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) performance status scale and the Karnofsky Performance Score (KPS). These scales provide numerical or categorical scores that reflect a patient's level of activity, independence, and capacity to carry out daily tasks. Generally, a higher performance status indicates better functional ability and fitness, whereas a lower score may suggest greater impairment or frailty.

The primary purpose of measuring performance status is to guide clinical decision-making, including determining suitability for specific treatments, estimating prognosis, and tailoring therapeutic strategies to the patient's functional capacity. It serves as a crucial component in evaluating a patient's overall condition and helps predict treatment tolerance and outcomes.

In summary, Performance Status is a vital metric in healthcare that encapsulates a patient's physical health and functional ability, with the goal of optimizing treatment planning and improving care outcomes based on the individual's capacity to perform daily activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560521</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thymus">
      <data key="d0">Thymus</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The thymus is a small organ in the upper chest where lymphocytes develop into T lymphocytes (T cells).&lt;SEP&gt;The thymus is a small organ in front of the windpipe involved in T-cell development, which can enlarge in T-ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;The thymus is a small organ in front of the windpipe involved in T-cell development and can enlarge in T-ALL cases.&lt;SEP&gt;The thymus is a small organ in front of the windpipe that can become enlarged in T-ALL, leading to breathing problems.&lt;SEP&gt;Thymus is an organ in the lymph system involved in T cell development and part of the immune response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560521</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Test">
      <data key="d0">Blood Test</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood tests, including tumor marker assessments, are used to monitor for signs of recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood testing involves analyzing blood samples to identify genetic changes associated with leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood tests are used to help diagnose AML by examining blood cell counts and abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;A diagnostic procedure involving drawing blood to analyze components including PSA levels and other biomarkers for prostate health.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood tests like CBC are used to assess the extent of lymphoma and aid in treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood tests are used to confirm the presence of CLL by analyzing blood samples for abnormal lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;A laboratory analysis of blood samples to determine cell counts and chemical levels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560522</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Flow Cytometry">
      <data key="d0">Flow Cytometry</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Flow cytometry is a laboratory technique that identifies, counts, and analyzes cells by passing dyed cells through a light beam, useful in detecting blood cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Flow cytometry is a laboratory technique used to detect subtle differences between blood cell types and identify cellular markers, especially in the immune system.&lt;SEP&gt;Flow cytometry is a laboratory technique used to detect, identify, and count specific cells using light-sensitive dyes and a machine.&lt;SEP&gt;Flow cytometry is a laboratory technique used to analyze cell surface proteins for diagnosing CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;A laboratory technique used to detect and measure physical and chemical characteristics of cells, especially identifying cell surface proteins.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560522</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ovaries">
      <data key="d0">Ovaries</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The ovaries are essential female reproductive organs situated in the pelvic region that play a crucial role in reproduction and hormonal regulation. They are responsible for producing eggs (ova) necessary for fertilization and secreting key hormones such as estrogen and progesterone, particularly during premenopause, which regulate menstrual cycles, reproductive functions, and secondary sexual characteristics.

As part of the female reproductive system, the ovaries are also considered glands that can develop tumors or other pathological conditions, which are often assessed through pelvic examinations and imaging studies. They may be affected by various treatments, including cancer therapies, which can damage the ovaries and consequently impair fertility. In clinical practice, the ovaries can be surgically repositioned to prevent damage from radiation therapy, and during procedures like hysterectomy, they may be preserved or removed depending on the treatment plan and the patient's long-term hormonal and reproductive health. Overall, the ovaries are vital organs that influence hormonal balance, reproductive capability, and overall female health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560523</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Immunophenotype">
      <data key="d0">Immunophenotype</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunophenotype refers to the pattern of surface proteins on cells, used to identify CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;The specific pattern of proteins on cell surfaces used to identify and classify blood cancers such as CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;The pattern of proteins expressed on the surface of cells, used to classify blood cancers like CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560523</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CD20">
      <data key="d0">CD20</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">CD20 is a protein expressed on the surface of certain immune cells and cancer cells, targeted by drugs like rituximab.&lt;SEP&gt;CD20 is a surface protein commonly present on CLL cells, aiding in diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560523</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast">
      <data key="d0">Contrast</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Contrast refers to substances used during medical imaging procedures, specifically CT and MRI scans, to improve the clarity, detail, and visualization of internal structures. These agents are administered either via injection into veins or by mixing with liquids during the imaging process. Their primary function is to enhance the differentiation of tissues, organs, and structures such as the thyroid, neck, lymph nodes, and cervical areas, thereby facilitating more accurate diagnoses.

Contrast agents are not dyes but serve as enhancers that improve the quality and detail of the images obtained. Their usage may vary depending on the specific imaging modality; for instance, contrast may or may not be employed during MRI procedures based on clinical indications. During CT scans, contrast agents are particularly useful for visualizing cervical and neck structures more distinctly. 

While contrast agents significantly aid in medical imaging, they can also induce adverse reactions in some individuals. Common allergic responses include flushing, hives, or more serious allergic reactions. Despite these risks, their benefits in enhancing diagnostic accuracy typically outweigh potential side effects, making them vital tools in modern imaging techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560524</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarker">
      <data key="d0">Biomarker</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A biomarker is a biological molecule, genetic feature, or protein used to detect, classify, and monitor diseases, especially cancer. They include genetic mutations, such as BRCA mutations, BRAF V600E, IDH mutations, and features like HRD, MSI, MMR, TMB, and specific antigens such as PD-L1. Biomarkers can be identified through laboratory tests and are found in tumors, blood, or specific cells, providing valuable information for diagnosis, prognosis, and treatment planning.

In cancer management, biomarkers play a crucial role in guiding targeted therapy and immunotherapy options. They help identify patients who are likely to respond to specific treatments based on the presence of molecules like HER2, RET, NTRK, FRα, and others. Examples include p16 in certain cancers, or molecules like TMB-H (Tumor Mutational Burden-High) and MSI-H (Microsatellite Instability-High), which are used to determine eligibility for immune checkpoint inhibitors.

Biomarkers are also employed for disease staging and classification, aiding clinicians in tailoring personalized treatment approaches. For example, in acute myeloid leukemia (AML), specific proteins and gene features are used to subtype the disease and predict patient outcomes, while in other cancers like bladder or thyroid, tumor-specific markers assist in identification and surveillance.

Overall, biomarkers are essential in modern medicine for their capacity to indicate the presence of disease, inform therapeutic decisions, and monitor disease progression or response to treatment, thereby enabling more precise and effective healthcare interventions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560524</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CD200">
      <data key="d0">CD200</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">CD200 is a surface protein expressed on CLL cells, used as a diagnostic marker.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
