    <node id="Hematopathologist">
      <data key="d0">Hematopathologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A hematopathologist specializes in examining blood and marrow tissues for leukemia diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;A specialized medical professional who examines blood, marrow, and lymph node samples to diagnose blood cancers like CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;A healthcare professional who examines blood and tissue samples to diagnose blood disorders and cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560525</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Immunophenotyping">
      <data key="d0">Immunophenotyping</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunophenotyping is a diagnostic technique used to identify cell surface markers for FL classification.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunophenotyping uses flow cytometry to detect specific markers on cells, especially in the immune system, for identifying different blood cell types.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunophenotyping is a process that uses antibodies to detect antigens (biomarkers) on white blood cells, aiding in cell identification.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunophenotyping employs antibodies to identify white blood cell antigens called biomarkers, aiding in AML treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A laboratory technique used to identify characteristic surface proteins on cells, aiding in the diagnosis of CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunophenotyping analyzes biomarkers and genetic changes in lymphomas to assist in diagnosis and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
