    </node>
    <node id="Blood Cell">
      <data key="d0">Blood Cell</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood cells are the components produced in bone marrow, affected in leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood cells are specialized cells in the bloodstream, including white blood cells, red blood cells, and platelets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560528</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mouth and Neck Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Mouth and Neck Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mouth and neck cancer refer to a group of cancers that originate in the head or neck regions, affecting areas such as the mouth, throat, nasal cavity, and voice box. They are grouped under head and neck cancers and include various types like oral cavity cancer, oropharyngeal cancer, nasopharyngeal cancer, and laryngeal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560528</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Mouth">
      <data key="d0">The Mouth</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The mouth, also called the oral cavity or buccal cavity, is the first part of the digestive and respiratory systems, involving structures such as lips, cheeks, palate, teeth, and tongue, essential for breathing, talking, chewing, tasting, swallowing, eating, and drinking.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560528</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lips">
      <data key="d0">Lips</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Lips surround the opening of the mouth and are involved in holding food in the mouth and forming words.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560528</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="DNA">
      <data key="d0">DNA</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">DNA, or deoxyribonucleic acid, is the molecule that constitutes the fundamental genetic material within living cells. It resides primarily within chromosomes and contains the comprehensive set of instructions necessary for the development, growth, and proper functioning of an organism. DNA encodes genes that serve as instructions for producing proteins essential for cellular functions and biological processes. These genetic instructions are crucial not only for normal cellular activities but also play a significant role in hereditary traits and genetic diversity.

In addition to its essential biological functions, DNA is a key focus in medical and genetic research. It is often extracted from biological samples such as skin biopsies or blood to analyze inherited genetic factors that influence disease susceptibility, including the risk of conditions like leukemia. DNA analysis is fundamental in genetic testing, mutation detection, and understanding the molecular basis of diseases. Mutations in DNA, especially within tumor cells, can significantly influence the behavior of cancers such as gliomas and may impact treatment strategies.

Moreover, certain chemical modifications to DNA, such as methylation, are relevant in medical treatments. For instance, DNA contains methyl groups that can be targeted by hypomethylating agents used in specific therapeutic contexts. Overall, DNA's role extends from carrying genetic instructions to serving as a critical biomarker in diagnostics and personalized medicine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560529</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tongue">
      <data key="d0">Tongue</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The tongue is a muscular organ aiding in chewing, speaking, and breathing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560529</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gums">
      <data key="d0">Gums</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Gums, or gingiva, surround the teeth and help keep them in place.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560529</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hard Palate">
      <data key="d0">Hard Palate</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The hard palate is the bony roof of the mouth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560529</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Buccal Mucosa">
      <data key="d0">Buccal Mucosa</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The lining inside the cheeks and lips where the mouth touches the teeth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560529</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The lymph node is a small, bean-shaped structure that is a vital component of the lymphatic and immune systems. These small organs function primarily to filter lymph fluid, enhancing immune responses by producing immune cells and trapping pathogens, infected cells, or foreign particles. They are distributed throughout the body and play a crucial role in defending against infections. 

In the context of cancer, lymph nodes are significant indicators for the spread or metastasis of various malignancies, including skin, prostate, and anal cancers. They can harbor metastatic cancer cells, which can be detected through biopsies. The status of lymph nodes, often evaluated as the N component in cancer staging, indicates whether cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes and how many are affected. The presence or absence of metastasis in lymph nodes influences treatment decisions, such as surgical removal of involved nodes. 

Particularly noteworthy is the sentinel lymph node, which is the first node where cancer cells are most likely to disseminate from the primary tumor. Examining this node helps determine the extent of cancer spread and guides surgical procedures. Lymph nodes involved in metastasis may be biopsied or removed, especially when they are implicated in disease progression or recurrence, as in cases of anal cancer. Overall, lymph nodes serve both as a crucial immune defense mechanism and as key indicators in the diagnosis, staging, and management of various cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560529</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Alveolar Ridge">
      <data key="d0">Alveolar Ridge</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The bony area in the upper and lower jaws that hold the teeth sockets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560529</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone marrow is the soft, spongy tissue located within bones that plays a vital role in blood cell production. It serves as the primary site for hematopoiesis, where blood-forming stem cells reside and differentiate into various blood components, including red blood cells, white blood cells such as lymphocytes and lymphoblasts, and platelets. The marrow's specialized environment supports the continuous renewal and development of these cells, which are essential for oxygen transport, immune responses, and clotting.

Within bone marrow, there are distinct regions: red marrow, which contains active hematopoietic tissue responsible for blood cell generation, and yellow marrow, predominantly comprised of fat. The marrow's functionality is crucial in diagnosing and understanding blood disorders, such as leukemia (including AML, acute myeloid leukemia), where abnormal proliferation of blood cells occurs. Bone marrow biopsies and aspirates are routine procedures used to examine marrow cell populations, detect malignancies, and perform genetic or cytogenetic testing, including karyotyping. Additionally, bone marrow is involved in the development and progression of leukemia, with abnormal lymphocytes sometimes accumulating in conditions like chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL).

The tissue also serves as the site for therapeutic interventions such as stem cell transplants, crucial in treating various hematological diseases. Bone marrow samples are analyzed to assess disease status, monitor responses to treatment, and investigate blood cell deficiencies. The marrow's health and functional integrity are affected by treatments like chemotherapy, which may temporarily inhibit its ability to produce blood cells. Overall, bone marrow is essential for hematopoiesis, immune function, and as a diagnostic and therapeutic target in numerous blood-related illnesses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560530</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Retromolar Trigone">
      <data key="d0">Retromolar Trigone</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Small area behind the wisdom teeth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560530</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Oral Mucosa">
      <data key="d0">Oral Mucosa</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The mucous membrane lining the inside of the mouth, consisting of epithelium and lamina propria, which can be nonkeratinized or keratinized depending on the region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
