    <node id="Support System">
      <data key="d0">Support System</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Having friends, family, or peers supports emotional wellbeing and logistical needs during diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Having family, friends, or peers for emotional and logistical support is beneficial during FL diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A network of assistance involving friends, family, or peers providing transportation, meals, and emotional support during diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A support system includes friends, family, or peers who provide transportation, meals, and emotional support during diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Includes bringing someone for support, transportation, and post-treatment home care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560536</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer treatment encompasses a wide range of medical approaches aimed at curing, controlling, or managing various types of cancer. The primary methods include surgical intervention, radiation therapy, systemic therapies such as chemotherapy and immunotherapy, and participation in clinical trials exploring emerging treatments. These modalities are selected based on factors like tumor location, cancer stage, and specific characteristics of the cancer, with treatment strategies often tailored to individual patient circumstances.

For example, the treatment of mouth, head, and neck cancers depends heavily on the tumor's location and stage, commonly involving surgery, radiation, or chemotherapy. Similarly, approaches to treating cervical cancer incorporate surgery, chemotherapy, radiation therapy, and clinical trials to optimize efficacy. Primary central nervous system (CNS) lymphoma is managed with options such as chemotherapy, immunotherapy, and targeted therapy, demonstrating the diversity of treatment strategies available for different cancer types.

Cancer treatments can also impact reproductive health, particularly in testicular cancers, where therapies may cause damage to sperm and affect reproductive functions. These side effects highlight the importance of comprehensive patient management and counseling regarding potential reproductive implications. Overall, cancer treatment involves conventional methods like surgery, chemotherapy, and radiation, as well as emerging approaches that may combine standard and alternative therapies to enhance outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560538</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) is a malignant cancer originating from squamous cells, which are flat, thin cells lining various tissues and organs in the body. It is notably the most common type of skin cancer, often arising in the surface cells of the skin and mucous membranes. SCC frequently occurs in the mouth, where it develops in the thin, flat cells lining the mucous membranes, making it the most prevalent form of mouth cancer. Additionally, SCC is the most common histological type of nasopharyngeal cancer, originating in the surface lining cells of the nasopharynx, and it is also the leading type of cervical cancer, arising from squamous cells lining the ectocervix.

In the respiratory system, squamous cell carcinoma is a significant subtype of non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), forming from cells lining the bronchi—the large air passages in the lungs. It originates specifically in the flat cells lining the airways and is a major histological category of lung cancers associated with smoking and other risk factors. SCC can also develop in the bladder, though less commonly, arising from squamous cells within the lining of the bladder. Moreover, it is involved in cancers of the anal canal, where it originates in squamous cells lining the tissue surface of the anal canal, representing the most common type of anal cancer.

Overall, squamous cell carcinoma can occur in various organs and tissues where squamous cells are present, including the skin, mouth, nasopharynx, cervix, lungs, bladder, and anal canal. Its manifestations and treatment options vary depending on the site of origin, with therapies ranging from topical treatments and surgery in skin cases to more complex interventions for internal carcinomas. The diagnosis, staging, and management of SCC are affected by its location, histological characteristics, and the extent of malignant progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560538</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Documents">
      <data key="d0">Medical Documents</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical documents include insurance forms, medical records, and test results organized for access and communication with healthcare providers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
