    <node id="Primary Care Physician">
      <data key="d0">Primary Care Physician</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The primary care physician is a healthcare provider responsible for overseeing a patient's general health and treatment updates.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary care physician (PCP) is a healthcare provider involved in coordinating a patient's overall health care.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary care physician (PCP) is the main healthcare provider responsible for patient care coordination and updates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560538</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Portal">
      <data key="d0">Patient Portal</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">An online platform allowing patients to access their test results, medical records, and communicate with their healthcare providers.&lt;SEP&gt;Online system for viewing medical records, test results, and health information.&lt;SEP&gt;A patient portal is an online platform used for tracking medical appointments, communicating with the care team, and managing health records.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560538</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ureters">
      <data key="d0">Ureters</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Ureters are tubes that carry urine from the kidneys to the bladder.&lt;SEP&gt;Ureters are tubes that carry urine from the kidneys to the bladder, and are part of the urinary tract examined during ureteroscopy.&lt;SEP&gt;Ureters are tubes that carry urine from the kidneys to the bladder, visible during imaging tests like ultrasound and pyelogram.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560540</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary Tract">
      <data key="d0">Urinary Tract</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">The urinary tract includes the kidneys, ureters, bladder, and urethra, which work together to produce and eliminate urine.&lt;SEP&gt;The system of organs, including the urethra, bladder, ureters, and kidneys involved in urine production and elimination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560540</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Urothelium">
      <data key="d0">Urothelium</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The urothelium is the inner lining of the bladder that prevents urine from leaking back into the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Urothelium is the inner lining of the urinary tract, including the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560540</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Urothelial Cells">
      <data key="d0">Urothelial Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Urothelial cells line the inside of the bladder and other parts of the urinary tract; they are involved in most bladder cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;The inner lining of the bladder is made of urothelial cells, where most bladder cancers originate.&lt;SEP&gt;Cells that line the inner lining of the bladder, where most bladder cancers originate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560540</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bladder Wall">
      <data key="d0">Bladder Wall</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The bladder wall is composed of layers of tissue that can be invaded by cancer over time.&lt;SEP&gt;The structural layer of the bladder in which cancer can invade and which determines cancer stage and severity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560540</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancers Types">
      <data key="d0">Cancers Types</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Types of bladder cancer include urothelial (most common), squamous cell carcinoma, adenocarcinoma, and small cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560540</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary System">
      <data key="d0">Urinary System</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">The urinary system consists of organs such as the bladder, urethra, and prostate, involved in urine production and expulsion.&lt;SEP&gt;The urinary system includes organs such as the kidneys, ureters, bladder, and urethra, responsible for urine production and elimination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560541</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Errors">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Errors</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic errors or mutations are changes in DNA that can lead to uncontrolled cell growth, as seen in bladder cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560541</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Plasma">
      <data key="d0">Plasma</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Plasma is a clear, yellowish fluid component of blood, primarily made of water, that carriers blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560541</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Red Blood Cells">
      <data key="d0">Red Blood Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">Red blood cells (erythrocytes) are cells in blood responsible for carrying oxygen throughout the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Red blood cells carry oxygen throughout the body, and their reduction causes health issues in AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Red blood cells (RBCs) carry oxygen throughout the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560541</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Platelets">
      <data key="d0">Platelets</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">Platelets (thrombocytes) are cell fragments that help control bleeding.&lt;SEP&gt;Platelets (PLTs) are blood cells involved in clot formation to prevent or stop bleeding.&lt;SEP&gt;Platelets help control bleeding.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560541</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hematopoietic Stem Cells">
      <data key="d0">Hematopoietic Stem Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">Hematopoietic stem cells in bone marrow multiply and develop into blood cells through differentiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hematopoietic stem cells in bone marrow differentiate into various blood cell types, including red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets.&lt;SEP&gt;Hematopoietic stem cells are early blood-forming cells located in bone marrow that differentiate into all types of blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Hematopoietic stem cells are specific blood-forming stem cells that are used in stem cell rescue procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560541</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="AML (Acute Myeloid Leukemia)">
      <data key="d0">AML (Acute Myeloid Leukemia)</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">AML is a cancer originating from abnormal blood stem cells, involving immature myeloid cells called blasts.&lt;SEP&gt;AML is an aggressive blood cancer that may develop as a rare severe side effect of chemotherapy and PARP inhibitor treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;AML, or Acute Myeloid Leukemia, is a type of blood cancer diagnosed through bone marrow testing, characterized by abnormal leukemia cells.&lt;SEP&gt;AML is a subtype of leukemia diagnosed through tests on bone marrow samples, involving abnormal proliferation of myeloid cells.&lt;SEP&gt;AML is a blood cancer characterized by abnormal proliferation of myeloid cells, diagnosed via genetic and biomarker tests.&lt;SEP&gt;AML is a type of blood cancer characterized by the rapid growth of abnormal myeloid cells in the bone marrow and blood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560541</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Myeloblasts">
      <data key="d0">Myeloblasts</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">Myeloblasts are immature white blood cells involved in AML, which mature into granulocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloblasts are immature white blood cells that proliferate excessively in AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloblasts are immature white blood cells that become abnormal in AML, leading to buildup in the bone marrow and blood.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloblasts are immature white blood cells that can proliferate excessively in AML, crowding out other blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloblasts are immature cells that develop from myeloid progenitors and are involved in blood cell formation.&lt;SEP&gt;Immature white blood cells involved in AML that fail to mature properly.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloblasts are immature blood cells derived from myeloid progenitor cells, involved in blood formation and in CML pathology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560542</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blasts">
      <data key="d0">Blasts</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Blasts are immature blood cells, often referring to the most immature myeloid or lymphoid cells involved in CML progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Blasts are immature white blood cells involved in leukemia, characterized by uncontrolled proliferation in blood and bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;Blasts are immature leukemia cells in the blood, reported as a percentage, indicating possible leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Immature blood cells, with a threshold of 20% or more in marrow for AML diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560542</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Preservation">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Preservation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility preservation is a set of medical actions and strategies aimed at maintaining an individual's reproductive options and capacity before the initiation of cancer treatments that could cause infertility. It primarily involves measures taken prior to therapy, such as consulting fertility specialists and utilizing procedures like sperm banking, egg freezing, and ovarian tissue banking, to safeguard reproductive potential for future use.

These procedures are designed to enable patients, particularly those undergoing treatments like chemotherapy, radiotherapy, or surgery for cancers such as cervical cancer, to retain the possibility of future pregnancy. Fertility preservation can be implemented either before or during cancer treatment, with the goal of preserving reproductive function and options for later life. In the context of cervical cancer, special attention is sometimes given to approaches that allow for cancer treatment while still maintaining the possibility of future pregnancy.

Overall, fertility preservation emphasizes proactive measures to mitigate infertility caused by cancer therapies, ensuring that patients retain the ability to conceive after their treatment is completed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560542</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="AML Testing">
      <data key="d0">AML Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">AML testing involves genetic, biomarker, and blood cell analysis to diagnose and understand leukemia characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560543</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="AML Biomarker">
      <data key="d0">AML Biomarker</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">AML biomarkers are specific genetic or chromosomal mutations used to diagnose and classify AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560543</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Myeloid Progenitor Cells">
      <data key="d0">Myeloid Progenitor Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">Myeloid progenitor cells develop into myeloblasts and other non-lymphoid blood cells, involved in the production of various immune cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloid progenitor cells are blood cells that differentiate into myeloblasts and other non-lymphoid blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloid progenitor cells develop into red blood cells, granulocytes, and platelets affected in AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloid progenitor cells develop into myeloblasts and other non-lymphoid blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloid progenitor cells are precursor cells that develop into various myeloid blood cells, involved in CML development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560543</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic mutations are alterations in the DNA sequence that can influence cancer development and treatment response.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic mutations refer to specific changes in genes that are used as biomarkers to diagnose and classify AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic mutations such as BRAF, ALK, NTRK, RET, Mismatch Repair Deficiency, MSI-H, and TMB-H are associated with thyroid cancer and influence treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic mutations such as those in CDKN2A, FGFR3, PIK3CA, and ERBB can increase the inherited risk of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560543</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chromosomal Abnormalities">
      <data key="d0">Chromosomal Abnormalities</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromosomal abnormalities involve structural or numerical changes in chromosomes, used to identify specific AML subtypes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560543</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Urethra">
      <data key="d0">Urethra</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The urethra is a vital anatomical tube responsible for transporting urine from the bladder to outside the body. It serves as the final conduit in the urinary system, allowing for the excretion of urine produced by the kidneys. The urethra's primary function is to enable the expulsion of urine, passing through the pelvic region to open externally.

In males, the urethra is positioned beneath the prostate gland and surrounds it, passing through the prostate as it travels from the bladder to the exterior. This anatomical placement is significant, as conditions affecting the prostate can influence urethral function. The urethra's course in males is longer and more complex compared to females, partly due to its dual role in urinary and reproductive systems.

Throughout medical examinations, such as cystoscopy, the urethra is inspected for abnormalities including signs of cancer or other pathologies. Its structure and integrity are essential for normal urinary function, and any obstructions or damage can lead to difficulty urinating or other urinary issues. Overall, the urethra is a crucial component of both the urinary system and male reproductive health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560544</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Diseases">
      <data key="d0">Blood Diseases</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood diseases include various disorders affecting blood function, including AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560544</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Testing">
      <data key="d0">Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing is a comprehensive process encompassing a variety of diagnostic procedures used across multiple medical contexts to identify, evaluate, and develop treatment plans for different health conditions. These procedures include blood tests, imaging techniques, biopsies, genetic or biomarker testing, and general health assessments, each tailored to specific diseases and diagnostic needs.

In the context of adrenal tumors, testing involves blood analyses, imaging modalities, genetic testing, biopsy, and overall health evaluations to accurately diagnose the condition and inform treatment strategies. For skin cancers such as cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC), testing encompasses health history assessment, lesion examination, and skin biopsy to confirm diagnosis. Similarly, in mouth cancer, testing involves general health exams, imaging, biopsies, and biomarker assessments to establish a diagnosis and guide therapy.

Breast cancer diagnosis and staging incorporate blood tests, imaging, biopsies, and biomarker analyses to determine the extent of the disease and plan effective treatment approaches. Neurological assessments, including neurological exams, performance status evaluations, imaging scans, and biopsies, are employed to confirm diagnoses and determine disease progression. Nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC) testing comprises health evaluations, imaging, biopsies, and biomarker testing to inform treatment decisions.

Thyroid nodules are evaluated through procedures such as TSH tests, ultrasound imaging, and biopsies to assess for malignancy and establish a diagnosis. For conditions like CBCL (cutaneous B-cell lymphomas), testing involves blood analyses, imaging, biopsy, and immunophenotyping to confirm the diagnosis and plan appropriate treatment.

Additional testing after initial diagnosis, such as physical examinations and imaging, is often necessary to develop comprehensive treatment plans. Overall, the term "testing" in the medical domain refers to an array of diagnostic procedures integral to the accurate identification, staging, and management of diverse health conditions across various specialties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560544</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bladder">
      <data key="d0">Bladder</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The bladder is a vital hollow, muscular organ located in the pelvis, primarily responsible for storing urine until it is expelled from the body. It is situated above the prostate in males and within the pelvis in both sexes, functioning as a storage reservoir that contracts during urination to release urine. The bladder's structure includes four main layers: an inner lining called the urothelium, connective tissue, a muscular wall known as the detrusor muscle, and an outer fatty layer, all of which contribute to its ability to hold and expel urine effectively.

In medical imaging, the bladder can appear bright on PET scans, which is considered normal. It can also be visualized through MRI and other diagnostic tests like cystoscopy and urograms, which assess for conditions such as cancer invasion, tumors, or abnormalities. During cystoscopy, a procedure whereby a scope is inserted into the bladder, clinicians can detect tumors, inflammation, or other issues related to bladder health.

The bladder is also significant in the context of bladder cancer, a disease that affects this organ. Bladder cancer can involve various tumor types that are often treated surgically or via other therapies, including intravesical treatments. The condition of the bladder, such as the presence of tumors or invasion into surrounding tissues, is crucial for diagnosis, staging, and treatment planning.

Furthermore, the bladder’s function and structure can be impacted by medical interventions like radiation therapy. It is frequently examined in the context of bladder cancer diagnosis and management, and its normal appearance and function are essential considerations during various diagnostic procedures and treatments. Overall, the bladder plays a central role in urinary storage and elimination, as well as serving as a key focus in urological health and disease management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560544</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kidneys">
      <data key="d0">Kidneys</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A pair of organs located behind other abdominal organs, responsible for hormone production and blood filtration.&lt;SEP&gt;The kidneys can appear bright on PET scans, which is normal.&lt;SEP&gt;Kidneys are organs that may be damaged by Cisplatin; reduced kidney function may limit the use of Cisplatin.&lt;SEP&gt;Kidneys are vital organs in the urinary tract responsible for filtering blood and producing urine.&lt;SEP&gt;The kidneys are organs responsible for filtering waste products from blood, monitored through kidney function tests in blood analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;The kidneys are organs that filter blood to produce urine; they can be examined to assess cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Kidneys are vital organs responsible for filtering blood and producing urine. They are part of the urinary system and are examined during renal ultrasound and other diagnostic procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560544</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Urothelial Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Urothelial Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Urothelial carcinoma starts in the cells lining the renal pelvis and ureter, but is not classified as kidney tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Urothelial carcinoma is the most common type of bladder cancer that begins in the urothelium.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560546</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Small Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Small Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Small cell carcinoma is an uncommon, aggressive cancer of the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560546</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hematuria">
      <data key="d0">Hematuria</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Hematuria is the presence of blood in the urine, often an early symptom of bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560546</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Urine">
      <data key="d0">Urine</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Urine is the liquid waste excreted by the urinary system, potentially containing blood when bladder tumors shed blood.&lt;SEP&gt;Urine is a body fluid collected to perform diagnostic tests like urine cytology and tumor marker analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Urine analysis provides information about bodily functions and can help identify conditions like bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560546</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary Tract Infection">
      <data key="d0">Urinary Tract Infection</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Urinary tract infection (UTI) is a common infection that can cause blood in the urine, aside from bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560546</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Urinalysis">
      <data key="d0">Urinalysis</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Urinalysis is a diagnostic test analyzing urine samples to detect abnormalities such as blood or cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560547</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cytology">
      <data key="d0">Cytology</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Cytology is the microscopic examination of individual cells collected during biopsy procedures to determine cancer presence.&lt;SEP&gt;Cytology involves examining urine or tissue samples under a microscope to detect cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560547</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hereditary Mutation">
      <data key="d0">Hereditary Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">A gene mutation present in all cells of the body, inherited from parents and increasing cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Hereditary mutations are genetic alterations passed through families that increase bladder cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560547</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lynch Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Lynch Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A hereditary condition caused by gene mutations, increasing risk for ovarian, colon, and uterine cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is a hereditary condition associated with increased risk of ovarian and other cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is an inherited cancer syndrome associated with mismatch repair deficiency or high microsatellite instability in rectal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is an inherited condition that increases risk for various cancers, including pancreatic cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is an inherited genetic condition increasing the risk of colon and other cancers, associated with mismatch repair gene mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is an inherited disorder associated with a high risk of colon cancer due to genetic mutations affecting mismatch repair.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is an inherited condition that heightens the risk of cancers, including bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560547</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chemical Exposure">
      <data key="d0">Chemical Exposure</data>
      <data key="d1">riskfactor</data>
      <data key="d2">Exposure to certain chemicals used in dyes, rubber, leather, printing, textiles, and paint increases bladder cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560547</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Age">
      <data key="d0">Age</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk of basal cell skin cancer increases with age as cumulative sun exposure builds up over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Patient's age used to evaluate likelihood of hereditary ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The highest risk for prostate cancer is in men aged 65 and above, with risk increasing with age.&lt;SEP&gt;Age affects PSA levels and prostate size, influencing prostate health assessments.&lt;SEP&gt;Older age, particularly in the 60s or 70s, is a risk factor for primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;The risk of bladder cancer increases with age.&lt;SEP&gt;A demographic factor affecting treatment options and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
