    <node id="Recurrence">
      <data key="d0">Recurrence</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Recurrence, in the context of cancer, refers to the return or relapse of cancer after initial treatment and remission. It can manifest locally at the original site of the tumor, as well as in other parts of the body, such as lymph nodes or distant organs. For example, basal cell skin cancer may recur either at its original location or spread to other areas, including lymphatic or distant sites. Similarly, thyroid cancer and bladder cancer are known to frequently return after treatment, necessitating ongoing monitoring and potentially additional therapy to manage the disease.

Cancer recurrence is typically detected through clinical symptoms and confirmed via various testing methods, including imaging techniques. The concept of recurrence underscores the importance of continuous vigilance after treatment to identify and address any signs of cancer returning. Overall, recurrence is a significant concern across many cancer types, highlighting the need for careful follow-up and management strategies to improve patient outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560555</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
