    <node id="Radon">
      <data key="d0">Radon</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A radioactive gas linked to increased lung cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Radon is a radioactive gas that is classified as a carcinogen and contributes to the risk of developing SCLC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560556</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Immunotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Immunotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunotherapy is a systemic cancer treatment that stimulates, enhances, or manipulates the immune system to detect, recognize, and destroy cancer cells. It aims to re-activate the body's natural defenses against tumors, utilizing various approaches including immune checkpoint inhibitors, such as PD-1 inhibitors like cemiplimab and pembrolizumab, which are used specifically for cancers like squamous cell skin cancer (CSCC), advanced or recurrent cervical cancers, and certain biomarker-positive cancers such as dMMR/MSI-H tumors. Immunotherapy is applicable across a broad range of cancer types, including leukemia, AML, thyroid cancers with specific biomarkers, bladder cancer, anal cancer, and others.

A prominent aspect of immunotherapy involves the use of immune checkpoint inhibitors, which block regulatory pathways in immune cells to enhance anti-tumor immune responses. These agents are employed for locally progressive and metastatic cancers, such as anal cancer, where they may serve as a treatment option before surgery or when conventional treatments have failed. Immunotherapy can also employ other mechanisms, such as the use of monoclonal antibodies like rituximab, to target specific cancer markers. 

While generally effective, immunotherapy can be associated with side effects, including autoimmune reactions and fatigue. Overall, it represents a pivotal modality in modern oncology, harnessing the immune system's power to provide targeted and systemic cancer control and is especially valuable in cases involving specific genetic or biomarker profiles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560556</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Stage">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer stage is a clinical classification that describes the extent and severity of cancer at the time of diagnosis. It encompasses various criteria such as tumor size, the degree of spread to lymph nodes, and the presence or absence of metastasis, which collectively help determine the overall progression of the disease. The staging system commonly employs numbered stages from 0 or I through IV, with lower stages indicating localized disease and higher stages reflecting advanced spread.

Specifically, cancer staging provides crucial information for treatment planning, prognosis estimation, and treatment response monitoring. For example, in lung cancer, the stage describes the extent of spread within the body, categorized into stages I, II, III, and IV. Similarly, bladder cancer staging assesses how deeply the tumor has invaded the bladder wall and whether it has spread beyond the bladder, influencing therapeutic decisions. The classification considers factors like tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis to offer a comprehensive picture of disease progression.

While most descriptions focus on solid tumors like lung, ovarian, and bladder cancers, the concept of staging broadly applies across many cancer types, helping clinicians determine the best course of action. In cases of ovarian cancer, the stage describes the extent of spread within the pelvis and abdomen. Surgical staging may also be used to assess tumor growth and dissemination, especially in ovarian and other cancers. Overall, the cancer stage remains a vital metric guiding clinical management and prognosis across different cancer types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560556</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Asbestos">
      <data key="d0">Asbestos</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A mineral fiber associated with lung cancer when inhaled.&lt;SEP&gt;Asbestos is a mineral fiber known as a carcinogen, and exposure to it increases the risk of SCLC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560556</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thoracic radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Thoracic radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A thoracic radiologist is a medical specialist involved in diagnosing lung diseases through imaging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560556</data>
