    </node>
    <node id="Interventional radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Interventional radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An interventional radiologist is a medical specialist who performs minimally invasive procedures for lung disease diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560556</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Targeted Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Targeted Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Targeted therapy is a form of cancer treatment that employs medications designed to specifically attack certain molecular, genetic, or cellular features of cancer cells, thereby inhibiting their growth, survival, and spread. This approach involves identifying and targeting particular biomarkers, gene mutations, or receptors present on or within cancer cells, allowing for a more precise and often less toxic treatment compared to traditional chemotherapy.

In the context of various cancers, targeted therapies focus on specific molecules involved in tumor development and progression. For example, in skin cancer such as squamous cell carcinoma, epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) inhibitors like cetuximab are used to target overexpressed receptors. In leukemia, particularly acute myeloid leukemia (AML), drugs are designed to target unique genetic or molecular features of the cancer cells, sparing normal cells. Similarly, in cervical cancer and thyroid cancers, targeted treatments aim at biomarkers and mutations associated with tumor growth, especially in recurrent, metastatic, or treatment-resistant cases. Drugs such as lenvatinib, sorafenib, and cabozantinib are employed to treat advanced or metastatic thyroid cancers that are unresponsive to other therapies.

Targeted therapies can be used alone or in combination with chemotherapy and are distinguished by their specificity toward molecules involved in oncogenesis, cell proliferation, and metastasis. They often utilize kinase inhibitors or other agents to interfere with signaling pathways that promote tumor growth. Overall, targeted therapy represents a personalized approach to cancer treatment, emphasizing the disruption of specific genetic or molecular abnormalities that drive the disease, thus improving efficacy and reducing collateral damage to normal tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
