    <node id="Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Skin cancer is a disease characterized by abnormal skin cell growth, and several factors can increase the risk of developing it.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin cancer is a disease characterized by abnormal cell growth in the skin, often requiring regular skin examinations for monitoring and early detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560560</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CML">
      <data key="d0">CML</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) is caused by increased myeloid progenitor cells resulting from a specific genetic mutation involving the Philadelphia chromosome and BCR::ABL1.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) is a blood cancer caused by a specific genetic abnormality involving the Philadelphia chromosome and BCR::ABL1 gene.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) is a type of blood cancer characterized by abnormal proliferation of myeloid cells, often caused by a genetic abnormality.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic myeloid leukemia is a blood cancer caused by the BCR::ABL1 gene fusion involving translocation of chromosomes 9 and 22.&lt;SEP&gt;CML is caused by the BCR::ABL gene fusion, leading to uncontrolled blood cell growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560560</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Philadelphia (Ph) Chromosome">
      <data key="d0">Philadelphia (Ph) Chromosome</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">The Philadelphia chromosome is a shortened chromosome 22 resulting from a translocation involving chromosomes 9 and 22, associated with CML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560560</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The BCR::ABL1 gene is a fused gene created by the translocation involving chromosomes 9 and 22, characteristic of CML.&lt;SEP&gt;BCR::ABL1 is a gene producing a protein leading to uncontrolled blood cell growth in CML.&lt;SEP&gt;BCR::ABL1 is a fused gene resulting from the translocation of parts of chromosome 9 and 22, causing CML.&lt;SEP&gt;BCR::ABL1 is a fused gene resulting from a chromosomal translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22, associated with chronic myeloid leukemia (CML).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560560</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Cells">
      <data key="d0">Blood Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood cells include red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, essential for oxygen transport, immune defense, and blood clotting.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood cells are the cellular components of blood, including myeloid progenitor cells, myeloblasts, lymphoblasts, and others involved in blood formation.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood cells include red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, each with specific immune and transport functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood cells, including red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, are specialized cells within blood performing oxygen transport, immune defense, and clotting functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood cells include myeloid progenitors, lymphocytes, and other immune cells involved in blood formation and leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood cells are the components of blood produced in the bone marrow, vital for oxygen transport, immunity, and clotting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560561</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoid Progenitor Cells">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoid Progenitor Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphoid progenitor cells develop into lymphoblasts and then into lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoid progenitor cells develop from blood stem cells and mature into lymphocytes, including B cells, T cells, and natural killer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoid progenitor cells are precursors that develop into lymphocytes, involved in lymphocyte production.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoid progenitor cells develop into lymphoblasts and then into lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560561</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
