    <node id="Supportive Care">
      <data key="d0">Supportive Care</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Supportive care is an integral aspect of cancer management that focuses on alleviating symptoms and mitigating the side effects associated with cancer itself and its treatments. Primarily, supportive care includes medical interventions such as transfusions, growth factors, and pain management strategies designed to manage treatment-induced adverse effects and support patient health and comfort. It also encompasses a broad range of supportive strategies aimed at enhancing the patient's quality of life during treatment, including symptom management, emotional support, and patient education.

Beyond pharmacological treatments, supportive care involves multidisciplinary services such as counseling, nutritional advice, physical therapy, meditation, palliative care, and integrative medicine approaches. These interventions are applicable at any stage of cancer treatment to help patients cope with physical, emotional, and psychological challenges. Overall, supportive care seeks to improve overall well-being by relieving distressing symptoms and side effects, thereby enhancing comfort and the patient's ability to continue cancer treatment effectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560585</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hemorrhoids">
      <data key="d0">Hemorrhoids</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Common benign condition that symptoms resembling anal cancer can be attributed to.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560585</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Healthcare Providers">
      <data key="d0">Healthcare Providers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary care physician, gynecologist, and gastroenterologist are specialists involved in evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560585</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Colorectal Surgeons">
      <data key="d0">Colorectal Surgeons</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Specialists qualified to evaluate and treat anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560586</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="High-Resolution Anoscopy">
      <data key="d0">High-Resolution Anoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A procedure involving magnified visualization of the anal canal to detect precancerous or cancerous lesions.&lt;SEP&gt;High-resolution anoscopy is a diagnostic procedure used to examine the anal canal for cancer or precancerous lesions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560586</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Rectal Exam">
      <data key="d0">Digital Rectal Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A diagnostic procedure used to examine the prostate to detect abnormalities potentially indicating prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure where a doctor inserts a finger into the rectum to feel the prostate for signs of cancer or abnormal texture.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure where a doctor inserts a gloved finger into the rectum to feel the prostate for irregularities or signs of tumor development.&lt;SEP&gt;Physical exam where a gloved finger is inserted into the anus to detect abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Integral part of diagnosis, staging, and follow-up for anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A Digital Rectal Exam (DRE) is a physical examination used to assess the rectum, anus, and prostate, and is recommended 8 to 12 weeks after chemoradiation for anal cancer follow-up.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560586</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 0 Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 0 Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Early form of anal cancer that may be found during biopsy or removal of non-cancerous growths.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560586</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="HPV">
      <data key="d0">HPV</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Human papillomavirus (HPV) is a group of viruses comprising over 100 different strains that infect skin and mucous membranes. HPV is widely recognized as a highly prevalent sexually transmitted infection that can cause persistent cell changes and is the primary causal factor in the development of various cancers, notably cervical cancer, anal cancer, and certain throat cancers. 

In the context of throat cancers, HPV is associated with particular tumor types, especially p16-positive tumors, with p16 testing serving as an important biomarker for HPV-related throat malignancies. The virus's link to abnormal cell growth underpins its role in cancer development, making HPV a significant focus in cancer prevention, diagnosis, and management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560586</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="SISCCA">
      <data key="d0">SISCCA</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Superficially invasive squamous cell carcinoma, earliest form of anal cancer, often small and found incidentally.&lt;SEP&gt;Lesions smaller than 7 mm, not deep, and potentially treatable with localized removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560586</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-Up">
      <data key="d0">Follow-Up</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Monitoring after treatment, including HRA and testing to check for spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560586</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Colposcope">
      <data key="d0">Colposcope</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A magnifying instrument used during HRA to closely examine anal tissue for abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560586</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Acan">
      <data key="d0">Acan</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Anal intraepithelial neoplasia (AIN) is an abnormal neoplastic lesion associated with HPV.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560586</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="p16 (HPV) Testing">
      <data key="d0">p16 (HPV) Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">p16 (HPV) testing is used to detect human papillomavirus associated with throat cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560587</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Counseling">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Counseling</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility counseling is offered as part of the treatment planning process for patients with mouth cancer, especially when treatments might affect reproductive health.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility counseling provides information and guidance related to fertility preservation during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560587</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck Exam">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A physical examination focusing on the head and neck area to check for signs of mouth or head and neck cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;A complete head and neck clinical examination includes inspection with tools like mirrors and fiberoptic scopes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560587</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Support">
      <data key="d0">Patient Support</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Patient support involves emotional and logistical assistance during diagnosis and treatment, including partner or caregiver involvement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560587</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dental Exam">
      <data key="d0">Dental Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Dental exams and x-rays are recommended before certain treatments to assess oral health and prevent complications.&lt;SEP&gt;Dental examination is important for maintaining oral health during cancer treatment and involves regular checkups and cleanings.&lt;SEP&gt;Dental exams involve evaluation of teeth, gums, and oral health, especially concerning head and neck cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560587</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Anal cancer is a malignant tumor that primarily originates in the tissue lining the anal canal, most commonly arising from squamous cells. It can also involve the perianal region and adjacent tissues. This disease often presents as abnormal, malignant cell development in or near the anus and is associated with risk factors such as human papillomavirus (HPV) infection.

As a rare malignancy, anal cancer typically requires a comprehensive diagnostic process and involves various stages, commonly classified as stages I, II, and III, which influence treatment strategies. The disease is characterized by its potential to metastasize beyond the pelvic region, necessitating systemic treatment options. The primary treatment modalities include surgery, radiation therapy (including radiochemotherapy), and chemotherapy, with immunotherapy sometimes playing a role in specific cases. Procedures like local excision may be applicable, especially in early or localized stages.

The most common histological type of anal cancer is squamous cell carcinoma, although other malignant cell types can occur. Treatment planning is tailored based on the stage and extent of disease, with a focus on controlling local and metastatic disease, improving prognosis, and managing symptoms. Overall, anal cancer requires specialized diagnostic and therapeutic approaches, reflecting its unique pathology and progression patterns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560588</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients are comprehensive, evidence-based resources designed to help patients and caregivers understand cancer care and make informed decisions. These guidelines serve as trustworthy, accessible summaries of expert recommendations, providing current and reliable information on a wide range of cancer-related topics. They cover specific cancers such as squamous cell skin cancer, melanoma, prostate cancer, bone and soft tissue sarcomas, and encompass general aspects of cancer management.

The primary goal of the NCCN Guidelines for Patients is to educate individuals about various facets of cancer care, including testing, treatment options, assessment strategies, and supportive care. They are crafted to aid patients in understanding their diagnosis, potential treatment pathways, and managing side effects and distress associated with cancer therapies. The guidelines also assist patients in preparing questions for their healthcare team, fostering effective communication and shared decision-making.

Available online and via the NCCN app, these patient-friendly summaries distill complex clinical guidelines into accessible information, ensuring that patients and caregivers can navigate the intricacies of cancer care confidently. Published by the National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN), these guidelines aim to promote evidence-based, tailored care recommendations that enhance patient understanding, support informed choice, and improve overall care outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560588</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="HRA">
      <data key="d0">HRA</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">HRA likely refers to Human Rights Authority or possibly a health-related assessment; the context is insufficient for precise definition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560588</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Groin">
      <data key="d0">Groin</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The groin is an area where the thigh meets the abdomen, containing inguinal lymph nodes important in cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560588</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Examination">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Examination</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph node examination includes palpation, fine-needle aspiration (FNA), and excisional biopsy to assess for metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical procedure involving examination of lymph nodes to check for signs of cancer spread or recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560588</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Resonance Imaging">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Resonance Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) uses magnetic fields and radio waves to produce detailed images of the inside of the body. Open MRIs have a top and bottom with openings on each end, while closed MRIs are more common and may cause claustrophobia.&lt;SEP&gt;A non-invasive imaging technique that uses magnetic fields and radio waves to produce detailed images of internal organs and tissues, especially useful for prostate cancer detection.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI can be used with PET to visualize the extent of cervical cancer in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI uses strong magnetic fields and radio waves to produce detailed images of internal body structures without radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI uses magnetic fields and radio waves to create detailed images without radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560588</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Positron Emission Tomography">
      <data key="d0">Positron Emission Tomography</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A PET scan uses radioactive tracers to visualize cancer cells in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;PET is an imaging test that uses radioactive materials called radiotracers to visualize metabolic activity, especially in cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;PET involves radioactive glucose injection and detects cancer cells by their increased sugar uptake.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560588</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="HIV Testing">
      <data key="d0">HIV Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">HIV testing identifies HIV status to help guide cancer treatment and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;HIV testing is recommended after diagnosis of anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560589</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gynecologic Exam">
      <data key="d0">Gynecologic Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Includes cervical cancer screening and HPV testing to detect cervical abnormalities and assess cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560589</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Staging">
      <data key="d0">Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Staging determines the extent of cancer spread and guides subsequent treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;A process used to determine the extent of cancer growth and spread based on tumor size, invasion, and lymph node involvement.&lt;SEP&gt;Staging determines how far cancer has spread in the body, guiding treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Staging assesses the extent of cancer, aiding prognosis and treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Staging assesses the extent of cancer growth in the colon based on invasion depth and lymph node involvement.&lt;SEP&gt;Staging reflects the extent of cancer at diagnosis, guiding prognosis and treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Staging uses TNM system to assess tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis for treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560589</data>
      <data key="d6" />
