    <node id="Sperm Banking">
      <data key="d0">Sperm Banking</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Sperm banking, or semen cryopreservation, stores semen samples in liquid nitrogen for future use.&lt;SEP&gt;Sperm banking involves freezing semen in liquid nitrogen for potential future use.&lt;SEP&gt;Sperm banking is a fertility preservation technique involving freezing semen for future use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560591</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Egg Freezing">
      <data key="d0">Egg Freezing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Egg freezing, or oocyte cryopreservation, involves removing and freezing unfertilized eggs for future use.&lt;SEP&gt;Egg freezing entails removing and freezing unfertilized eggs for later use.&lt;SEP&gt;Egg freezing, or oocyte preservation, involves removing and storing unfertilized eggs for future use.&lt;SEP&gt;Egg freezing, or oocyte cryopreservation, involves freezing unfertilized eggs for later use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560591</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Tissue Banking">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Tissue Banking</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">This involves removing part or all of an ovary, freezing the tissue containing eggs, and reimplanting it later.&lt;SEP&gt;Removing and freezing part or all of an ovary to preserve eggs, which can be re-implanted later.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian tissue banking involves removing and freezing parts of the ovary containing eggs for future transplantation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560591</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Oophoropexy">
      <data key="d0">Oophoropexy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Oophoropexy, also known as ovarian transposition, is a surgical procedure that moves the ovaries out of the radiation field to preserve hormonal function during cervical cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Oophoropexy is a surgical procedure that moves ovaries and fallopian tubes out of radiation exposure range.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560591</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenal Gland Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Adrenal Gland Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Adrenal gland cancer, also known as adrenocortical carcinoma (ACC), is a rare type of cancer affecting the adrenal glands and may produce symptoms related to hormone overproduction or pressure on nearby organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560592</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical cancer is a malignant tumor that arises from the cells of the cervix, which is the lower part of the uterus. It primarily originates in the epithelial cells of the cervix and is classified into various types, including squamous cell carcinoma, adenocarcinoma, and adenosquamous carcinoma. The disease is most commonly associated with human papillomavirus (HPV) infection, which is regarded as a major causative factor. 

Cervical cancer is typically diagnosed through biopsy and imaging studies, with staging conducted using the FIGO system. Its stages range from 1A (early, confined to the cervix) to 4B (advanced with distant metastasis). The progression of the disease involves tumor growth and potential spread to other organs, especially in its advanced stages, which can complicate treatment and prognosis. It is considered a common gynecological malignancy and affects the female reproductive system, including the cervix, uterus, ovaries, and fallopian tubes.

Treatment strategies for cervical cancer depend on the cancer's stage and may include surgical intervention, radiotherapy, chemotherapy, or combined approaches such as radiation with systemic therapies. In early stages, surgery may be sufficient, whereas advanced stages often require more comprehensive treatment options. The disease can recur after treatment, and when metastasis occurs, management becomes more challenging. 

Screening methods like Pap tests and HPV/Pap co-tests are utilized for early detection and prevention, emphasizing the importance of regular screening in reducing disease incidence. Additionally, a history of cervical cancer or precancerous conditions is considered a risk factor for developing other cancers, such as anal cancer. Overall, cervical cancer remains a significant health concern that necessitates ongoing vigilance through screening, vaccination, and timely treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560592</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenal Glands">
      <data key="d0">Adrenal Glands</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Adrenal glands are small, yellow glands vital to the body's endocrine system, producing hormones that regulate various bodily functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560592</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenocortical Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Adrenocortical Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">Adrenocortical carcinoma is a type of adrenal cancer that starts in the adrenal cortex and can affect people of any age, with higher incidence in children under 5 and adults in their 40s and 50s.&lt;SEP&gt;Adrenocortical carcinoma (ACC) is a rare adrenal cancer beginning in the cortex, with potential hormone overproduction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560592</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Neuroblastoma">
      <data key="d0">Neuroblastoma</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">Neuroblastoma is a cancerous adrenal tumor affecting children aged 5 and under, often found in infants, capable of spreading to bones and other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Neuroblastoma is a rare, cancerous adrenal tumor primarily affecting children under 5, especially infants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560592</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pheochromocytoma">
      <data key="d0">Pheochromocytoma</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">Pheochromocytoma is a tumor originating in the adrenal medulla that causes symptoms like high blood pressure, headache, rapid heartbeat, and sweating, and is mostly benign but can be cancerous.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560592</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adenoma">
      <data key="d0">Adenoma</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenoma, or adrenocortical adenoma, is the most common noncancerous adrenal tumor which may or may not produce excess hormones and often does not cause symptoms.&lt;SEP&gt;An adenoma is a common type of non-cancerous growth (polyp) that forms on the inner lining of the colon and rectum and can potentially become invasive cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenomas are the most common type of polyps that can develop into invasive cancer over many years.&lt;SEP&gt;An adenoma is the most common type of polyp, which may develop into invasive colon cancer over many years.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenoma is the most common type of polyp, which can become malignant and is a precursor to adenocarcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A benign tumor that can be high risk; finding concerning or high-risk adenomas influences subsequent colonoscopy timing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560592</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenal Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Adrenal Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Adrenal tumor refers collectively to various tumors found in the adrenal glands, which can be benign or malignant, functioning or nonfunctioning.&lt;SEP&gt;Adrenal tumors develop in one or both adrenal glands, are often benign, and may produce hormones or be found incidentally.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560592</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hereditary Conditions">
      <data key="d0">Hereditary Conditions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Hereditary conditions are genetic mutations passed from parents that increase the risk of developing adrenal tumors, including syndromes like Li-Fraumeni syndrome, Multiple endocrine neoplasia types 1 and 2, and others.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560594</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Signs And Symptoms">
      <data key="d0">Signs And Symptoms</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms of adrenal cancer include weight gain, muscle weakness, skin changes, hormonal imbalances, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, fever, loss of appetite, and weight loss.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560594</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hormones">
      <data key="d0">Hormones</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemical substances produced by the adrenal glands that regulate various bodily functions and may be overproduced in functioning tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormones produced by adrenal glands influence metabolism, blood pressure, stress response, and physical characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Estrogen and progesterone are hormones produced mainly by the ovaries during premenopause.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormones such as ethinyl estradiol, fluoxymesterone, and megestrol acetate can be used at high doses to treat breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Substances such as estrogen affected by ovary removal, influencing menopause symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560594</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Signs">
      <data key="d0">Signs</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Signs include symptoms like weight gain, muscle weakness, skin changes, hormonal disruptions, nausea, vomiting, pain, fever, and weight loss related to adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560594</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenal Cortex">
      <data key="d0">Adrenal Cortex</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The adrenal cortex is the outer part of the adrenal gland that produces hormones like cortisol, aldosterone, and DHEA.&lt;SEP&gt;The adrenal cortex is the outer part of the adrenal gland where adrenocortical carcinoma begins.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560594</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenal Medulla">
      <data key="d0">Adrenal Medulla</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The adrenal medulla is the inner part of the adrenal gland that produces hormones such as epinephrine, norepinephrine, and dopamine.&lt;SEP&gt;The adrenal medulla is the inner part of the adrenal gland where pheochromocytoma starts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560594</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 1">
      <data key="d0">Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 1</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN 1) is a hereditary condition associated with increased risk of adrenal tumors along with other endocrine gland tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560594</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 2">
      <data key="d0">Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 2</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 (MEN 2) is a hereditary syndrome linked to adrenal tumors, particularly pheochromocytoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560594</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="VHL Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">VHL Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome (VHL) is a hereditary condition associated with increased risk of adrenal tumors among other tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560595</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancers and Pre-cancers">
      <data key="d0">Cancers and Pre-cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A history of certain other cancers or pre-cancerous conditions increases the risk of developing anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560595</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="DRE">
      <data key="d0">DRE</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Digital Rectal Exam (DRE) is a recommended test following diagnosis of anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560595</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Anoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Anoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Anoscopy is a recommended diagnostic procedure for anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A diagnostic procedure to examine the anal canal, recommended for surveillance after treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Anoscopy is an endoscopic procedure performed to examine the anal canal for signs of cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560595</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Cancer Screening">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Cancer Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Screening for cervical cancer is recommended post-diagnosis of anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560595</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stages I – III">
      <data key="d0">Stages I – III</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stages I, II, and III are non-metastatic, indicating cancer has not spread beyond the pelvis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560595</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage IV">
      <data key="d0">Stage IV</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage IV is metastatic cancer that has spread to distant sites.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage IV is the most advanced stage, often with distant metastasis or extensive spread within the head and neck.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage IV is metastatic, indicating cancer has spread to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560595</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">IMRT is an advanced form of EBRT that delivers radiation with many small beams of different strengths to limit damage to normal tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;IMRT is an advanced form of external beam radiation therapy that shapes radiation doses to the tumor, minimizing damage to surrounding tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560595</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Linear Accelerator">
      <data key="d0">Linear Accelerator</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A device used in EBRT to deliver high-energy radiation to tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560596</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Fractions">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Fractions</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Small doses of radiation given over 5-6 weeks, typically Monday through Friday.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560596</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Simulation">
      <data key="d0">Simulation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A preparatory step where the patient is positioned and imaging is used to plan radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Pre-treatment planning session involving imaging (CT scan) to plan radiation delivery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560596</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sexual Health">
      <data key="d0">Sexual Health</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A domain impacted by radiation and ovarian function.&lt;SEP&gt;Potential long-term effect of radiation therapy affecting sexual function.&lt;SEP&gt;Sexual health encompasses physical and emotional aspects of sexuality, which can be affected after anal cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560596</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bowel and Bladder Function">
      <data key="d0">Bowel and Bladder Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Potential long-term effects on bowel and bladder health due to radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560596</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Anal Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">A cancerous growth developing in the anal canal or perianal region, which may require treatments like chemoradiation or surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560596</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Microscopic Metastases">
      <data key="d0">Microscopic Metastases</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Microscopic metastases refer to cancer cells that have spread outside the ovaries or pelvis and can only be seen with a microscope.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560596</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Peritoneal Washing">
      <data key="d0">Peritoneal Washing</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Peritoneal washing involves rinsing the abdominal space with a special liquid to detect cancer cells when there is no fluid buildup.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560596</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="General Anesthesia">
      <data key="d0">General Anesthesia</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">General anesthesia is used during surgery to induce deep sleep and prevent pain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560596</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Premature Menopause">
      <data key="d0">Premature Menopause</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Premature menopause occurs when both ovaries are removed before menopause, causing a sudden drop in estrogen.&lt;SEP&gt;Early menopause resulting from radiation affecting the ovaries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560596</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Estrogen">
      <data key="d0">Estrogen</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Estrogen is a hormone that regulates reproductive functions, and its levels drop abruptly after ovarian removal causing menopause.&lt;SEP&gt;A hormone involved in the development of breast tissue and related to hormone receptor-positive breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Estrogen is a hormone made mainly by ovaries and other tissues like fat tissue, involved in breast cancer growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Estrogen is a hormone involved in breast cancer growth, potentially targeted by endocrine therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Estrogen is a hormone whose deficiency can lead to long-term health risks such as cardiovascular disease and osteoporosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560596</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone Replacement Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Hormone Replacement Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">HRT is used to manage symptoms of surgical menopause by restoring hormone levels.&lt;SEP&gt;HRT, now often called MHT, used to lessen menopausal symptoms post-radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560596</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Thinners">
      <data key="d0">Blood Thinners</data>
      <data key="d1">medication</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood thinners are drugs used post-surgery to prevent the formation of blood clots.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560596</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bevacizumab">
      <data key="d0">Bevacizumab</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Bevacizumab is a medication used in some ovarian cancer chemotherapy regimens to inhibit blood vessel growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Bevacizumab, also known as Avastin, is a medication used in chemotherapy to inhibit the growth of new blood vessels feeding tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Bevacizumab is an anti-angiogenic medication used in chemotherapy and as part of maintenance therapy for ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Bevacizumab (Avastin) is a biologic targeted therapy that may be added to chemotherapy regimens for cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560596</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Interval Cytoreductive Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Interval Cytoreductive Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Interval cytoreductive surgery (ICS) is performed after initial chemotherapy to remove remaining cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560596</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hyperthermic Intraperitoneal Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Hyperthermic Intraperitoneal Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">HIPEC involves circulating heated chemotherapy in the abdomen during surgery to treat stage 3 ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560596</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="American Joint Committee on Cancer">
      <data key="d0">American Joint Committee on Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The AJCC is responsible for developing a cancer staging system for ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) is responsible for creating the staging system used for cancer classification.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee on Cancer develops the tumor, node, metastasis (TNM) system used for staging colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560597</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics">
      <data key="d0">International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The FIGO is the organization that most often uses the staging system for ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560597</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="FIGO Stages">
      <data key="d0">FIGO Stages</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">FIGO stages categorize ovarian cancer based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and spread, numbered I to IV.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560597</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stages">
      <data key="d0">Stages</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer stages categorize the extent of disease, guiding prognosis and treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Stages categorize ovarian cancer from I to IV, with smaller subgroups to specify the extent of spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560597</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Substages">
      <data key="d0">Substages</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Substages provide more detailed classification within main cancer stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560597</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Advanced Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">More advanced ovarian cancers tend to have worse outcomes than early-stage cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560597</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Early-Stage Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Early-Stage Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Early-stage ovarian cancers generally have better prognosis than later stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560597</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mitomycin">
      <data key="d0">Mitomycin</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Mitomycin is a chemotherapy drug used in combination regimens for treating anal cancer, administered intravenously.&lt;SEP&gt;Mitomycin is a chemotherapy agent used with radiation therapy in treating perianal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Mitomycin is a chemotherapy drug used in combination with other agents like capecitabine or 5-FU to treat anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560597</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Capecitabine">
      <data key="d0">Capecitabine</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Capecitabine is an oral chemotherapy drug taken twice daily during radiation therapy for anal cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Capecitabine is a chemotherapeutic agent used with radiation therapy for perianal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Capecitabine is an oral chemotherapy medication used with radiation in anal cancer regimens.&lt;SEP&gt;Capecitabine is an oral chemotherapy drug used as an alternative to 5-FU during chemoradiation planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560598</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="5-FU">
      <data key="d0">5-FU</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">5-FU (fluorouracil) is a chemotherapy drug administered via continuous infusion to treat anal cancer, often alongside mitomycin or cisplatin.&lt;SEP&gt;5-Fluorouracil, a chemotherapy agent used in several regimens for anal cancer, including in conjunction with leucovorin, oxaliplatin, or capecitabine.&lt;SEP&gt;5-FU (5-fluorouracil) is a chemotherapy drug used with radiation therapy for treating perianal cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;5-Fluorouracil (5-FU) is a chemotherapy drug used with radiation in anal cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;5-fluorouracil (5-FU) is a chemotherapy drug used as part of the chemotherapy regimen when chemoradiation is planned.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560598</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage I, II, and III Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage I, II, and III Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Stages I, II, and III anal cancers are nonmetastatic and typically treated with chemoradiation, depending on cancer invasion and grade.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560598</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Perianal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Perianal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Perianal cancer refers to cancer located in the perianal region, stages I to III, often treated with chemoradiation or local excision.&lt;SEP&gt;Perianal cancer is a type of cancer occurring in the tissue surrounding the anus, with different grades indicating levels of differentiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560598</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Local Excision">
      <data key="d0">Local Excision</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical method involving removal of abnormal tissue from the colon, sometimes requiring additional treatment based on postoperative findings.&lt;SEP&gt;Local excision is a surgical procedure that removes only the tumor and surrounding tissue, considered for early-stage, low-risk, low-grade perianal cancers that do not invade the sphincter muscle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560598</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Margin">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Margin</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">The surgical margin is the normal tissue removed around a tumor during excision, with at least 1 cm recommended if free of cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;The surgical margin is the normal tissue surrounding the lesion removed during surgery, used to determine the completeness of excision.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560598</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Intravenous">
      <data key="d0">Intravenous</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Intravenous refers to delivering medicines directly into the bloodstream through a vein, a common method for chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560598</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Oral Administration">
      <data key="d0">Oral Administration</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Oral administration involves taking medicine by mouth, used as an alternative method for delivering chemotherapy drugs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560598</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stages I, II, and III Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stages I, II, and III Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Stages I to III anal cancers are nonmetastatic and involve specific treatment approaches such as chemoradiation and local excision.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560599</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Invasion">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Invasion</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The invasion of nearby structures, such as the sphincter muscle, determines eligibility for local excision.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560599</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="PICC Line">
      <data key="d0">PICC Line</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A peripherally inserted central catheter (PICC) line is a deep IV line inserted into the arm, used for administering chemotherapy over a period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560599</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Treating Healthy Cells">
      <data key="d0">Treating Healthy Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemotherapy can damage normal, healthy cells, leading to potential side effects such as nausea and low blood cell counts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560599</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hand-Foot Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Hand-Foot Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A side effect caused by capecitabine characterized by redness, swelling, and pain on the palms and soles, sometimes with blisters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560599</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Open MRI">
      <data key="d0">Open MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Open MRI features a magnetic top and bottom, allowing for openings on each end, which can help reduce claustrophobia in patients.&lt;SEP&gt;An open MRI has an opening at each end with a magnetic top and bottom, suitable for claustrophobic patients.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI design with openings at each end, allowing for easier access and reducing claustrophobia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560599</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Closed MRI">
      <data key="d0">Closed MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Closed MRI is a more common type of MRI with a tunnel-like structure, less open space, and potential for claustrophobia.&lt;SEP&gt;A closed MRI has a capsule-like design that surrounds the patient and is more common.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560599</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Panoramic Dental X-ray">
      <data key="d0">Panoramic Dental X-ray</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A radiographic imaging technique capturing the entire mouth structure in one image, used in dental planning.&lt;SEP&gt;A comprehensive dental image capturing jaws and teeth, used in dental treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;A panoramic dental X-ray captures the entire mouth including teeth, jaw, and surrounding tissues in one image using a small dose of radiation, typically performed by dentists.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560599</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Scope">
      <data key="d0">Scope</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A scope is a long, thin tube with a light and camera used to examine parts of the GI tract during procedures like colonoscopy and upper endoscopy.&lt;SEP&gt;A scope is a thin, tube-shaped tool inserted into the mouth or nose to examine internal structures and to capture images or guide biopsies, often called an endoscope.&lt;SEP&gt;A scope is a thin, tubeshaped tool inserted into the mouth or nose to examine the throat during diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560600</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Endoscope">
      <data key="d0">Endoscope</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An endoscope is a flexible instrument inserted into the body, guided through the mouth or nose to examine and take images of internal parts like the oropharynx and to assist in biopsies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560600</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fiberoptic Nasopharyngolaryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Fiberoptic Nasopharyngolaryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An exam using a fiberoptic scope to examine the larynx and nearby structures while the patient is awake with topical anesthesia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560600</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Laryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Laryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An examination of the back of the throat, vocal cords, and surrounding areas to detect tumors or abnormalities using a laryngoscope.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560600</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nasal Laryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Nasal Laryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of laryngoscopy where a flexible scope is passed through the nose to examine the throat and voice box.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560600</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Direct Laryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Direct Laryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A detailed exam performed under general anesthesia with a scope placed in the back of the throat to examine deeper structures and take biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;Direct laryngoscopy is a detailed examination performed under general anesthesia using a laryngoscope inserted into the back of the throat to see deeper and obtain tissue samples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560600</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hypopharynx">
      <data key="d0">Hypopharynx</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The lower part of the throat near the larynx, examined during laryngoscopy.&lt;SEP&gt;Lower part of the pharynx near the larynx involved in head and neck cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560600</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="G2">
      <data key="d0">G2</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">G2 indicates an intermediate-grade tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;G2 represents the moderately differentiated (intermediate grade) classification of perianal cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560600</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="G3">
      <data key="d0">G3</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">G3 is the highest grade for breast cancers, indicating highly abnormal and aggressive tumor cells.&lt;SEP&gt;G3 indicates poorly differentiated (high grade) tumors in the classification of perianal cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560601</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="G4">
      <data key="d0">G4</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">G4 refers to undifferentiated (high grade) tumors in the context of perianal cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560601</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="G1">
      <data key="d0">G1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">G1 indicates a low-grade tumor with less abnormal cells.&lt;SEP&gt;G1 is the classification for well differentiated (low grade) perianal cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560601</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Normal tissue">
      <data key="d0">Normal tissue</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Normal tissue is the tissue surrounding the lesion that, when removed with at least a 1 cm margin, may not contain cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560601</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Re-excision">
      <data key="d0">Re-excision</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Re-excision is a surgical procedure performed to remove additional tissue if the initial margin is less than 1 cm or contains cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560601</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage I">
      <data key="d0">Stage I</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer has grown into the second or third (muscle) layer of the rectal wall, typically requiring no further treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage I is early invasive cancer that has begun to spread beyond ducts or lobules.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage I is an early stage indicating limited tumor or lymph node involvement.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage I refers to early-stage, fast-growing (high-grade) perianal cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560601</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage II">
      <data key="d0">Stage II</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer has penetrated through the rectal wall and possibly into nearby tissues or organs.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage II is an intermediate invasive stage with potential lymph node involvement.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage II involves more extensive tumor growth or lymph node spread but no distant metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage II refers to more advanced perianal cancers, which may invade the sphincter or involve other tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560601</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage III">
      <data key="d0">Stage III</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes, often necessitating additional treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage III is a more advanced invasive cancer possibly involving extensive lymph node spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage III indicates locally advanced cancer with significant tumor or lymph node involvement.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage III indicates even more advanced perianal cancers with extensive invasion or spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560601</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Surveillance">
      <data key="d0">Surveillance</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Monitoring after treatment using sigmoidoscopy, ultrasound, or MRI to detect recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Surveillance involves monitoring a patient's condition without active treatment to observe disease progress or recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Surveillance involves monitoring and follow-up care to detect any recurrence or progression of disease after initial treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Post-treatment monitoring to detect recurrence, involving physical exams and imaging over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Surveillance involves ongoing testing to detect early signs of cancer recurrence after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560602</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-up physical exam">
      <data key="d0">Follow-up physical exam</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A follow-up physical exam includes digital rectal examination (DRE) and anoscopy to monitor for signs of cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560602</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Rectal Examination">
      <data key="d0">Digital Rectal Examination</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">DRE is a physical exam procedure used during surveillance to detect recurrent cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560602</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Complete remission">
      <data key="d0">Complete remission</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Complete remission indicates no signs of cancer in the body after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560602</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Persistent disease">
      <data key="d0">Persistent disease</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Persistent disease refers to cancer that remains in the body without growth or spread after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560602</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Progressive disease">
      <data key="d0">Progressive disease</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Progressive disease indicates that the cancer has grown or spread after initial treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560602</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Locally progressive">
      <data key="d0">Locally progressive</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Locally progressive indicates cancer growth that is limited to the anal area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560602</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic disease">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic disease</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic disease refers to cancer that has spread beyond the anal area to distant organs such as the liver or lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560602</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Abdominoperineal resection">
      <data key="d0">Abdominoperineal resection</data>
      <data key="d1">surgery</data>
      <data key="d2">Abdominoperineal resection (APR) is a surgical procedure to remove the anus in cases of recurrent or locally progressive anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560603</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Recurrence">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Recurrence</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The return of cancer after initial treatment, monitored through follow-up testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer recurrence is the return of cancer after a period of being cancer-free, which may occur locally, in lymph nodes, or distantly.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer recurrence refers to the return of cancer after initial treatment, often affecting treatment decisions and follow-up procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560603</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Distant areas">
      <data key="d0">Distant areas</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Distant areas such as the liver or lungs can be sites of metastatic recurrence of anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560603</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Inguinal Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Inguinal Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Inguinal lymph nodes are lymphatic structures near the groin area that are examined during follow-up for signs of metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560603</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Exam">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Examination of lymph nodes, performed regularly after treatment to detect recurrence in inguinal regions.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph node exams involve checking inguinal lymph nodes every 3 to 6 months for 5 years after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560603</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Surveillance Testing">
      <data key="d0">Surveillance Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Includes physical exams (DRE and anoscopy), imaging, and lymph node assessments to detect recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560603</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage II and III">
      <data key="d0">Stage II and III</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Specific stages of anal cancer that may require imaging during surveillance in complete remission.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560603</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Locally Progressive">
      <data key="d0">Locally Progressive</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Growth of the cancer that is limited to the anal area, indicating local recurrence or progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560603</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Distant Organ">
      <data key="d0">Distant Organ</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Distant organs like the liver and lungs can be sites where metastatic anal cancer spreads.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560604</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Abdominoperineal Resection">
      <data key="d0">Abdominoperineal Resection</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgery removing the anus, rectum, and surrounding tissue, resulting in a permanent colostomy, for lower rectal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Abdominoperineal resection (APR) is a surgical procedure involving removal of the anus, rectum, surrounding fatty tissue, and possibly lymph nodes in the groin, often used for anal and some rectal cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560604</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Muscle Flap Reconstruction">
      <data key="d0">Muscle Flap Reconstruction</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Muscle flap reconstruction uses muscle tissue, like rectus abdominus, to rebuild the vagina following pelvic exenteration.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgical technique using tissue, such as the rectus abdominis muscle, to promote wound healing after radiation or surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560604</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rectus Abdominis">
      <data key="d0">Rectus Abdominis</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A major abdominal muscle used in muscle flap reconstruction to aid healing after anal cancer surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560604</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Dissection">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Dissection</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph node dissection involves surgically removing lymph nodes near a tumor to check for spread of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgical removal of lymph nodes to evaluate the spread of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph node dissection or lymphadenectomy involves surgically removing lymph nodes to test for cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgical removal of lymph nodes, performed when lymph nodes are affected by cancer or as part of treatment for recurrent anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph node dissection is a surgical procedure to remove affected lymph nodes, especially in cases of recurrence or metastasis involving the groin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560604</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Leucovorin">
      <data key="d0">Leucovorin</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A drug used with 5-FU to enhance its effectiveness in chemotherapy regimens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560604</data>
      <data key="d6" />
