    <node id="Blast Phase CML">
      <data key="d0">Blast Phase CML</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Blast phase CML (BP-CML) is an advanced stage of CML involving increased lymphoid or myeloid progenitor cells, affecting treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Blast phase CML is an advanced form of chronic myeloid leukemia characterized by excess immature myeloid or lymphoid cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560615</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">BCR::ABL is a specific abnormal fusion gene that causes CML, resulting from a chromosome exchange between chromosomes 9 and 22.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560615</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Philadelphia Chromosome">
      <data key="d0">Philadelphia Chromosome</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Philadelphia chromosome (Ph) is an abnormal chromosome created by the exchange of chromosome parts, hallmark of CML.&lt;SEP&gt;The Philadelphia (Ph) chromosome is a shortened chromosome 22 resulting from the translocation in CML.&lt;SEP&gt;The Philadelphia (Ph) chromosome is a shortened chromosome 22 created by translocation, hallmark of CML, containing part of chromosome 9.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560615</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chromosomes 9 and 22">
      <data key="d0">Chromosomes 9 and 22</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromosomes 9 and 22 are involved in the genetic abnormality that causes CML by exchanging parts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560615</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Normal Blood Cells">
      <data key="d0">Normal Blood Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Normal blood cells do not contain the BCR::ABL gene and do not cause CML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560616</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Philadelphia Chromosome (Ph)">
      <data key="d0">Philadelphia Chromosome (Ph)</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Philadelphia chromosome is an abnormal chromosome containing part of chromosome 22 with the BCR::ABL1 gene, associated with CML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560616</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Care Centers">
      <data key="d0">Care Centers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Specialized care centers are recommended for treating CML to provide appropriate management.</data>
