      <data key="d0">vaginal stenosis</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A condition where the vagina becomes narrower and shorter, often due to radiation or surgery, causing discomfort.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560620</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hodgkin Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Hodgkin Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Hodgkin lymphoma is a highly treatable cancer originating in lymphatic system lymphocytes, characterized by Reed-Sternberg cells, commonly diagnosed in young adults.&lt;SEP&gt;Hodgkin lymphoma is a type of lymphatic cancer characterized by the presence of Reed-Sternberg cells in classical forms and popcorn-shaped cells in NLPHL, affecting the lymphatic system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560620</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Reed-Sternberg Cells">
      <data key="d0">Reed-Sternberg Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Reed-Sternberg cells are abnormal, oversized lymphocytes with multiple nuclei, characteristic of Hodgkin lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Reed-Sternberg cells are large lymphocytes indicative of classic Hodgkin lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560620</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hodgekin Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Hodgekin Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Hodgkin lymphoma is a specific type of lymphoma, a cancer of lymphatic system lymphocytes, distinguished by Reed-Sternberg cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560620</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN.org/patient-guidelines">
      <data key="d0">NCCN.org/patient-guidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The website NCCN.org/patient-guidelines provides access to NCCN's guidelines and resources for patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560620</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Basal Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Basal Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Basal cell skin cancer is a type of skin cancer that can recur or metastasize, requiring specific treatment approaches and diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Basal cell skin cancer, also known as basal cell carcinoma (BCC), is the most common type of skin cancer, characterized by abnormal growth of basal cells in the skin's epidermis, often caused by UV radiation exposure and treatable primarily through surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560621</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="United States">
      <data key="d0">United States</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The United States is a country where approximately 3 million cases of basal cell skin cancer are diagnosed annually.&lt;SEP&gt;The United States is a country where clinical trial information resources such as NCCN Cancer Centers and NLM are available.&lt;SEP&gt;The United States is a country where clinical trials are conducted and referenced.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560621</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sun">
      <data key="d0">Sun</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">The sun emits UV radiation that can cause DNA damage in basal cells, increasing skin cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560621</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tanning Beds">
      <data key="d0">Tanning Beds</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Artificial sources of UV radiation that increase the risk of basal cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560621</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Lesion">
      <data key="d0">Skin Lesion</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A skin lesion is often a visible manifestation of basal cell skin cancer, appearing as various types like shiny bumps, patches, or sores.&lt;SEP&gt;An abnormal area of skin, called a lesion, may indicate skin cancer and requires testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560621</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="UV Radiation">
      <data key="d0">UV Radiation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Ultraviolet radiation from the sun or tanning beds damages DNA inside basal cells, leading to cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560621</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="History of Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">History of Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Having a personal or family history of skin cancer increases the likelihood of developing basal cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560621</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fair Skin">
      <data key="d0">Fair Skin</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Fair skin, characterized by lighter skin, hair, or eyes, is a risk factor for basal cell skin cancer due to less melanin protection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560621</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Red or Blonde Hair">
      <data key="d0">Red or Blonde Hair</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Having red or blond hair is associated with higher risk of basal cell skin cancer due to lighter pigmentation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560622</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Light Eye Color">
      <data key="d0">Light Eye Color</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Light-colored eyes, such as blue or green, are a risk factor for basal cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560622</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Immune Suppression">
      <data key="d0">Immune Suppression</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Weakened immune system, due to organ transplants or autoimmune diseases, raises the risk of basal cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560622</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Early Detection">
      <data key="d0">Early Detection</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Detecting basal cell skin cancer early, especially when lesions are flat, shiny, or bleeding, greatly improves chances of cure.&lt;SEP&gt;Early detection aims to find lung cancer before symptoms appear for more successful treatment and prevention of death.&lt;SEP&gt;Early detection improves treatment success and reduces mortality in breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560622</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Exams">
      <data key="d0">Skin Exams</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Regular skin exams by a doctor are essential for monitoring and detecting recurrence of basal cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin exams are regular check-ups performed by healthcare providers to monitor for cancer recurrence or new skin cancers after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560622</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Melanoma">
      <data key="d0">Melanoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Melanoma is a serious form of skin cancer originating from melanocytes, distinct from basal cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Melanoma is a type of skin cancer that can be associated with HPV-related tissue changes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560622</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="PDL1">
      <data key="d0">PDL1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">PDL1 is an immune protein expressed in tumors, measured by combined positive score (CPS), and involved in immune checkpoint therapy decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560622</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Combined Positive Score">
      <data key="d0">Combined Positive Score</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The combined positive score (CPS) measures immune proteins in tumor and immune system to assess PDL1 expression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560622</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">This therapy involves drugs targeting proteins like PD-1 and PD-L1 to treat cancers with certain biomarkers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560622</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cell-Free DNA Testing">
      <data key="d0">Cell-Free DNA Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Cell-free DNA testing uses blood samples to detect cancer cells or DNA fragments from tumor cells, often for monitoring treatment response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560622</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Throat (Oropharyngeal) Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Throat (Oropharyngeal) Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Throat (oropharyngeal) cancer is a type of head and neck cancer diagnosed through biopsies and molecular testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560622</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="HPV Testing">
      <data key="d0">HPV Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV testing by p16 is recommended for all diagnosed with throat (oropharyngeal) cancer to gain more information about the cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560622</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Sample">
      <data key="d0">Blood Sample</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood samples are used in tumor mutation testing and cell-free DNA testing to analyze DNA from cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A sample of blood taken for liquid biopsy to detect cancer cells in the blood.&lt;SEP&gt;A blood sample, also called a liquid biopsy, is used to detect circulating cancer cells or DNA.&lt;SEP&gt;A small amount of blood collected for various tests like CBC, CMP, LDH, and HIV to evaluate health status and disease presence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560622</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dental Care">
      <data key="d0">Dental Care</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Regular dental checkups and cleanings are recommended for individuals with head and neck cancer to maintain oral health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560622</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor&lt;Cell">
      <data key="d0">Tumor&lt;Cell</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A sample of tumor tissue can be tested for DNA mutations, protein levels, or molecular features to inform treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560622</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="AJCC">
      <data key="d0">AJCC</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) is responsible for creating the cancer staging system.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) created the staging system used to determine the extent of throat cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) is an organization that created a staging system for various cancers, including NPC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560622</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Prognostic Stage">
      <data key="d0">Prognostic Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Prognostic stage combines anatomical staging, tumor grade, and molecular features to estimate likely outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560623</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Grade">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Grade</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor grade indicates how abnormal the cells appear and predicts growth rate and potential for spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor grade is a classification that predicts how quickly a tumor is expected to grow and spread, based on cellular appearance under a microscope.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor grade indicates how much cancer cells resemble normal cells, reflecting aggressiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor grade indicates the abnormality of cancer cells and their tendency to grow and spread, providing prognostic information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560623</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Syndromes">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Syndromes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic syndromes such as xeroderma pigmentosum and recessive dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa increase the risk of skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560623</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Weakened Immune System">
      <data key="d0">Weakened Immune System</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A weakened immune system reduces the body's ability to fight infections and increases the risk of squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560623</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Organ Transplant">
      <data key="d0">Organ Transplant</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Receiving an organ transplant and using immunosuppressants can weaken the immune system, raising the risk of skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Receiving an organ transplant involves immunosuppressive therapy, which raises the risk of cancer development and recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560623</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="HIV/AIDS">
      <data key="d0">HIV/AIDS</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">HIV/AIDS are health conditions that weaken the immune system, elevating the risk of squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) causes AIDS and is associated with increased risk of primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560623</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fiberoptic Laryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Fiberoptic Laryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Fiberoptic laryngoscopy (nasolaryngoscopy) is a procedure that uses a small flexible scope passed through the nose into the throat to examine the airway.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560623</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nasolaryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Nasolaryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Nasolaryngoscopy is another term for fiberoptic laryngoscopy, involving a flexible scope to view the nose and throat.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560623</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Laryngoscope">
      <data key="d0">Laryngoscope</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A laryngoscope is a device used during direct laryngoscopy to visualize the larynx and tissues of the throat.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560624</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Esophagoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Esophagoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Esophagoscopy involves guiding a device down the throat into the esophagus to examine for abnormal growths under anesthesia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560624</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bronchoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Bronchoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Bronchoscopy is a procedure where a device is inserted through the nose or mouth to examine the airway, including the trachea and bronchi, often for detecting cancer or treatments, under anesthesia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560624</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Airway">
      <data key="d0">Airway</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The airway includes the trachea and bronchi, examined during bronchoscopy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560624</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Panendoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Panendoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Panendoscopy involves passing different types of endoscopes through the mouth or nose to examine the mouth, oropharynx, larynx, esophagus, trachea, and bronchi, often under anesthesia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560624</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsy Sample">
      <data key="d0">Biopsy Sample</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Samples taken during biopsy are sent for analysis to determine the presence of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A tissue specimen obtained during a biopsy used for microscopic examination to assess cancer characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560624</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fine-Needle Aspiration">
      <data key="d0">Fine-Needle Aspiration</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Fine-needle aspiration (FNA) uses a thin needle to collect tumor or lymph node samples for analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Uses a thin needle to extract a small tissue sample from a tumor for diagnostic purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Fine-needle aspiration (FNA) is a method using a thin needle to remove tissue or fluid samples for analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;FNA is a minimally invasive biopsy technique used to sample thyroid nodules, providing suggestive rather than definitive diagnosis of follicular or oncocytic cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560624</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fine-Needle Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Fine-Needle Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A fine-needle biopsy (FNB) uses a small needle to extract tissue or fluid for analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Fine-needle biopsy (FNB) is a procedure similar to FNA but uses a larger needle for sampling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560624</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsy of Metastasis">
      <data key="d0">Biopsy of Metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Biopsy of metastasis involves sampling cancer spread sites like lymph nodes or lungs to confirm metastatic disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Biopsying metastatic sites helps confirm the spread of cancer and identify affected areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560625</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="MSI-H/dMMR">
      <data key="d0">MSI-H/dMMR</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">MSI-H/dMMR refers to microsatellite instability-high or deficient mismatch repair, indicators of genetic instability in cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsatellite instability-high (MSI-H) and deficient mismatch repair (dMMR) are genetic features associated with DNA errors in cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;MSI-H (microsatellite instability-high) and dMMR (deficient mismatch repair) are markers indicating DNA repair defects in cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560625</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="PD-L1">
      <data key="d0">PD-L1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Programmed death ligand 1 (PD-L1) is an immune protein expressed on cancer cells that can inhibit immune responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Programmed death-ligand 1 (PD-L1) is an immune protein expressed on cancer cells influencing immune response.&lt;SEP&gt;Programmed death-ligand 1 (PD-L1) is an immune protein on cancer cells that can suppress immune response.&lt;SEP&gt;Programmed death ligand 1 (PD-L1) is an immune protein expressed on cancer cells that can suppress immune responses, impacting immunotherapy effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;PD-L1 expression level is tested to determine eligibility for certain immunotherapies in recurrent or metastatic cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;PD-L1 is a biomarker that influences the choice of first-line systemic therapy for metastatic cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560625</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CPS">
      <data key="d0">CPS</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The combined positive score (CPS) measures PD-L1 expression on tumor and immune cells to guide immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Combined positive score (CPS) measures PD-L1 expression in tumor and immune cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560625</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="p16">
      <data key="d0">p16</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">p16 is a protein used as a surrogate marker for HPV infection in cancer cells, especially in throat cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;p16 is used as a biomarker to detect HPV-related cancer in tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;p16 status (positive or negative) helps determine the HPV-relatedness of throat cancer and influences staging and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;p16 testing is used to assess HPV status in cancers such as throat tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560625</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Histology">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Histology</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor histology refers to the classification of the tumor based on microscopic tissue analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560625</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chromosome">
      <data key="d0">Chromosome</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromosomes are structures within cells that organize genes and genetic information.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromosomes are structures within cells that contain DNA, determining genetic characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromosomes are structures within cells that contain the genetic material (DNA) and carry most of the genetic information in a cell.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromosomes are structures that contain most genetic information in a cell, including many genes.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromosomes are structures within cells that contain genetic material, and changes here are linked to leukemia subtypes.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromosomes are tightly packed structures of DNA that contain most of the genetic information inside cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560625</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Deoxyribonucleic Acid (DNA)">
      <data key="d0">Deoxyribonucleic Acid (DNA)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">DNA molecules store genetic information, tightly packaged into chromosomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560625</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Genomic Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genomic Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Genomic testing includes tumor sequencing, gene expression profiling, and other methods to identify genetic features of tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Genomic testing examines the DNA in tumor tissue to identify genetic mutations for targeted therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Microsatellite Instability (MSI)">
      <data key="d0">Microsatellite Instability (MSI)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">MSI is a condition where short repeated DNA sequences become unstable, often due to mismatch repair deficiencies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Deficient Mismatch Repair (dMMR)">
      <data key="d0">Deficient Mismatch Repair (dMMR)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">dMMR is a genetic defect leading to microsatellite instability-high (MSI-H) in some cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Immune checkpoint inhibitors are therapies that help restore immune response against tumors by targeting proteins like PD-L1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gastrointestinal (GI) Tract">
      <data key="d0">Gastrointestinal (GI) Tract</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The GI tract consists of organs through which food passes during digestion, including the stomach, small bowel, and large bowel, and can be examined via imaging tests such as scopes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Colonoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Colonoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Colonoscopy is an imaging test for examining the large bowel, involving inserting a scope into the anus and guiding it through the rectum and colon.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical procedure recommended every 3 to 5 years to examine the colon for signs of disease or abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;A colonoscopy is a procedure used to examine the colon’s inner lining and remove polyps through polypectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure involving the examination of the colon used for surveillance after treatment for stage 1 cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Upper Endoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Upper Endoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Upper endoscopy involves guiding a scope down the throat into the esophagus, stomach, and small bowel to examine these organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Chemistry Profile">
      <data key="d0">Blood Chemistry Profile</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">This profile measures chemical levels in the blood affected by organs like kidneys and bones, providing information on organ function and nutrient levels.&lt;SEP&gt;A blood chemistry profile measures levels of chemicals in the blood to assess organ function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Marker">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Marker</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor markers are substances in tissue or body fluids that can signal the presence of cancer, aiding in diagnosis and monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor markers such as CA-125 are used to monitor cancer status and potential relapse after treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Proteins or substances found in blood or tissue that may indicate the presence or characteristics of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CA-125">
      <data key="d0">CA-125</data>
      <data key="d1">biomarker</data>
      <data key="d2">CA-125 is a tumor marker commonly tested in blood to detect ovarian cancer; elevated levels may indicate ovarian or other cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;CA-125 is a tumor marker tested before each chemotherapy cycle to help evaluate ovarian cancer activity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Inhibin">
      <data key="d0">Inhibin</data>
      <data key="d1">biomarker</data>
      <data key="d2">Inhibin (A and B) levels can be elevated in ovarian cancers and are used as tumor markers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Beta-hCG">
      <data key="d0">Beta-hCG</data>
      <data key="d1">biomarker</data>
      <data key="d2">Beta-human chorionic gonadotropin (β-hCG) is a tumor marker that may be elevated in certain ovarian cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="AFP">
      <data key="d0">AFP</data>
      <data key="d1">biomarker</data>
      <data key="d2">Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) is a tumor marker that can be elevated in some ovarian cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CEA">
      <data key="d0">CEA</data>
      <data key="d1">biomarker</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) is a tumor marker that may be elevated in some ovarian cancer types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CA 19-9">
      <data key="d0">CA 19-9</data>
      <data key="d1">biomarker</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer antigen 19-9 (CA 19-9) is a tumor marker associated with certain ovarian cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="HE4">
      <data key="d0">HE4</data>
      <data key="d1">biomarker</data>
      <data key="d2">HE4 is a biomarker used alongside CA-125 to assist in diagnosing ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Homologous Recombination Deficiency">
      <data key="d0">Homologous Recombination Deficiency</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">HRD describes a state where DNA repair mechanisms are deficient, often linked to BRCA mutations, influencing treatment options in ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;HRD status is used to guide treatment, particularly in targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560628</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA Mutation">
      <data key="d0">BRCA Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">geneticmutation</data>
      <data key="d2">A mutation in BRCA genes affecting DNA repair pathways, significant for treatment planning in ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA mutation is a genetic alteration linked to ovarian cancer, making tumors more responsive to PARP inhibitors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560628</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="HRD-positive Tumor">
      <data key="d0">HRD-positive Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">A tumor that exhibits homologous recombination deficiency, which may be due to BRCA mutations or other alterations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560628</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Biomarkers">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Biomarkers</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic biomarkers are genetic features such as mutations that help guide treatment and prognosis in ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560628</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Molecular Testing">
      <data key="d0">Molecular Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing that analyzes tumor DNA for mutations and genetic features, also called tumor profiling or genomic testing.&lt;SEP&gt;A technique analyzing genetic mutations or protein levels in tumor samples to personalize treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular testing analyzes genetic and protein markers in tumors to support personalized treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560628</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="BRAF V600E">
      <data key="d0">BRAF V600E</data>
      <data key="d1">geneticmutation</data>
      <data key="d2">BRAF V600E is a mutation that may be present in ovarian tumors, impacting targeted therapy options.&lt;SEP&gt;BRAF V600E mutation can be a target for specific therapies in ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560628</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Key Points">
      <data key="d0">Key Points</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Key points summarize the importance of biopsy for diagnosis, imaging tests like ultrasound, and the role of various biomarker tests in ovarian cancer management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560628</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app provides accessible information and guidelines for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app offers accessible resources for patients seeking cancer care information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560628</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tobacco and Alcohol Use">
      <data key="d0">Tobacco and Alcohol Use</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">History of tobacco and alcohol use, which are major risk factors for developing head and neck cancers, affecting treatment outcomes and survival.&lt;SEP&gt;Use of tobacco and alcohol are risk factors for head and neck cancers and can influence treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560629</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Feeding Tube">
      <data key="d0">Feeding Tube</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A feeding tube is a medical device used to provide nutrition to patients unable to eat normally, often used during treatment for mouth cancer when swallowing is difficult.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560629</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sperm">
      <data key="d0">Sperm</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Male reproductive cells that can be damaged or impaired by cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Sperm are male reproductive cells that can be damaged by cancer treatment, impacting fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560629</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hormonal Birth Control">
      <data key="d0">Hormonal Birth Control</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormonal birth control methods are used to prevent pregnancy during cancer treatment, including options like IUDs and barriers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560629</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Intrauterine Devices (IUDs)">
      <data key="d0">Intrauterine Devices (IUDs)</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">IUDs are hormonal or non-hormonal devices inserted into the uterus to prevent pregnancy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560629</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Barrier Methods">
      <data key="d0">Barrier Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Barrier methods include condoms, diaphragms, cervical caps, and contraceptive sponges used to prevent pregnancy.&lt;SEP&gt;Condoms, diaphragms, cervical caps, and sponges used for contraception.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560629</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Menstruation">
      <data key="d0">Menstruation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Menstruation is the regular shedding of the uterine lining, which may stop during cancer treatment but often returns within 2 years in certain age groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Menstrual cycle, which may stop during treatment but often resumes after in women under 35.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560629</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Contraception">
      <data key="d0">Contraception</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A method such as condoms used to prevent pregnancy during and after cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Methods used to prevent pregnancy, including hormonal and barrier-based options.&lt;SEP&gt;Contraception includes methods like condoms used to prevent pregnancy during and after cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560629</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="HPV-related Cancer">
      <data key="d0">HPV-related Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer caused by HPV infection, particularly squamous cell carcinomas of the throat.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="p16 (HPV)-positive">
      <data key="d0">p16 (HPV)-positive</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer with overexpression of p16 indicating likely HPV involvement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chromosome 9">
      <data key="d0">Chromosome 9</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromosome 9 contains the gene that produces p16 protein.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromosome 9 is a human chromosome involved in the translocation process that creates the BCR::ABL1 gene.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromosome 9 is one of the pairs of chromosomes involved in genetic translocation leading to CML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Viral Warts">
      <data key="d0">Viral Warts</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Viral warts result from HPV infection and can cause abnormal tissue growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Psychological Distress">
      <data key="d0">Psychological Distress</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Psychological distress encompasses mental, social, physical, or spiritual suffering caused by cancer diagnosis and treatment, including feelings such as sadness, fear, helplessness, worry, anger, guilt, depression, anxiety, and sleep issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hearing Test">
      <data key="d0">Hearing Test</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A procedure to assess hearing ability and identify loss, severity, and causes potentially related to tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;A hearing test assesses hearing ability, detects hearing loss, and identifies its type, severity, and possible causes, especially affected by tumors or treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A hearing test assesses hearing ability; it can identify hearing loss caused or affected by head and neck tumors or their treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumors in Head and Neck">
      <data key="d0">Tumors in Head and Neck</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumors located in the head and neck can impact hearing directly or through their treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Function Tests">
      <data key="d0">Lung Function Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung function tests measure lung capacity, airflow, oxygen usage, and carbon dioxide output, indicating respiratory health, especially relevant before cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nutrition Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Nutrition Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A nutrition assessment involves consulting with a nutrition expert to ensure adequate nutrition before, during, and after cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;An assessment to evaluate nutritional needs before cancer treatment, involving consultation with a nutrition expert.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Smokers and Vapers">
      <data key="d0">Smokers and Vapers</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Individuals who smoke or vape nicotine have increased risks of head and neck or lung cancers, and their habits can affect treatment effectiveness, wound healing, and surgical outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nicotine">
      <data key="d0">Nicotine</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Nicotine, the chemical in tobacco products, increases the risk of cancer, complicates treatment, and causes withdrawal symptoms.&lt;SEP&gt;Nicotine is the chemical in tobacco that strongly encourages smoking and vaping, affecting health and treatment outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Nicotine is a chemical in tobacco that fosters addiction, complicates smoking cessation during cancer treatment, and contributes to worsening health outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Nicotine is a chemical found in tobacco that induces the desire to smoke and can cause withdrawal symptoms.&lt;SEP&gt;Nicotine is the addictive chemical in tobacco that sustains smoking behavior and withdrawal challenges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG)">
      <data key="d0">Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">ECOG is a collaboration that developed the PS scale, rating health from 0 (fully active) to 5 (deceased).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pre-anesthesia Exam">
      <data key="d0">Pre-anesthesia Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Pre-anesthesia assessment evaluates medical history, airway, and other health factors to prepare for anesthesia during cancer surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck Cancer Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck Cancer Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical treatment for head and neck cancers involves complex procedures, with considerations of health conditions, airway, swallowing, and tumor sites.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility and Reproductive Health">
      <data key="d0">Fertility and Reproductive Health</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility and reproductive health refer to the ability to conceive and carry children, potentially impacted by cancer treatments, with options for preservation discussed proactively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group">
      <data key="d0">Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) is an organization that developed the performance status (PS) scale used to assess a patient's general health and ability to perform daily activities.&lt;SEP&gt;The Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) score is a scale used to rate the general health status of patients, particularly in assessing treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560630</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T Stages">
      <data key="d0">T Stages</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">T stages describe the size and local extent of the tumor in throat cancer, ranging from Tis to T4b.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560631</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage0 to Stage4">
      <data key="d0">Stage0 to Stage4</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer stages range from Stage 0 to Stage 4, indicating increasing severity and spread of disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560631</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Resectable">
      <data key="d0">Resectable</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Resectable tumors can be completely removed with surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560631</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Unresectable">
      <data key="d0">Unresectable</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Unresectable tumors involve critical structures or extent making surgical removal unsafe or impossible.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560631</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic cancer has spread to distant parts of the body or distant lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560631</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="P16-negative">
      <data key="d0">P16-negative</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">P16-negative (p16-) refers to throat cancer not caused by HPV and with specific staging criteria.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560631</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tis">
      <data key="d0">Tis</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinoma in situ, local early-stage tumor with no invasion.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor stage representing carcinoma in situ, where cancerous cells are limited to the squamous cell layer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560631</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T1">
      <data key="d0">T1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor is 2 cm or smaller in size.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor stage where cancer is confined to the nasopharynx or has grown into the oropharynx or nasal cavity, but not into the parapharyngeal space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560632</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T2">
      <data key="d0">T2</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor is between 2 and 4 cm in size.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor stage indicating growth into the parapharyngeal space and/or nearby soft tissues such as muscles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560632</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T3">
      <data key="d0">T3</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor is larger than 4 cm or has invaded the epiglottis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560632</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T4a">
      <data key="d0">T4a</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Moderately advanced local disease involving invasion of larynx, extrinsic tongue muscles, palate, or mandible.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560632</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T4b">
      <data key="d0">T4b</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Very advanced local disease involving invasion of lateral pterygoid, skull base, or carotid artery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560632</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinoma in situ">
      <data key="d0">Carcinoma in situ</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinoma in situ (Tis) is a very early form of cancer confined to the epithelium with no invasion into deeper tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560632</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing blood cancer originating in lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell of the immune system, leading to overproduction of immature lymphocytes called lymphoblasts which can crowd out normal blood cell production.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing blood cancer originating in lymphocytes, characterized by overproduction of lymphoblasts, which can impair normal blood function.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing blood cancer characterized by overproduction of immature lymphocytes called lymphoblasts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560632</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="B-cell Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">B-cell Lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">B-cell lymphocytes are immune cells that produce antibodies, involved in the development of B-cell ALL, and can give rise to mature B-cell ALL or Burkitt leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560633</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T-cell Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">T-cell Lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">T-cell lymphocytes are immune cells that fight infections and tumor cells, involved in T-cell ALL, which may cause thymus enlargement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560633</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoblastic Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoblastic Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphoblastic lymphoma is a condition where lymphoblasts grow mainly in lymph nodes or thymus instead of bone marrow, related to B-ALL or T-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560633</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-Hodgkin lymphoma encompasses lymphatic cancers, including Burkitt lymphoma, which is related to mature B-cell ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560633</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN)">
      <data key="d0">National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN provides guidelines for cancer care, including for Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia, and offers resources for patients and providers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560633</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pediatric">
      <data key="d0">Pediatric</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Pediatric refers to ALL conditions found in infants, children, and young adults aged 18 years or under, including adolescents and young adults (AYAs).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560633</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adolescents and Young Adults (AYAs)">
      <data key="d0">Adolescents and Young Adults (AYAs)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">AYAs are individuals aged 15 to 39 years at cancer diagnosis, treated in pediatric or adult centers depending on cancer type.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560633</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Burkitt Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Burkitt Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Burkitt lymphoma is a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that is essentially the same as mature B-cell ALL, but treated differently, and associated with the subtype called mature B-cell ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Burkitt lymphoma is a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that is essentially the same as mature B-cell ALL but is treated differently.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560634</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="ALL">
      <data key="d0">ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a blood cancer affecting lymphoid progenitor cells, which are involved in blood cell formation. It develops from abnormal lymphoid progenitor cells that can become lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560634</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoid progenitor cells">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoid progenitor cells</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphoid progenitor cells arise from blood stem cells and develop into lymphocytes, which are affected by ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoid progenitor cells develop into lymphoblasts that mature into lymphocytes, involved in immune response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560634</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="National Comprehensive Cancer Network® (NCCN®)">
      <data key="d0">National Comprehensive Cancer Network® (NCCN®)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN provides guidelines and information on cancer testing, treatment, and supportive care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560634</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Book on ALL">
      <data key="d0">Book on ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A comprehensive resource organized into chapters covering testing, treatments, supportive care, and resources for ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560634</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Testing for ALL">
      <data key="d0">Testing for ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Includes blood tests, bone marrow tests, biomarker and genetic testing, and imaging to diagnose and determine treatment for ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Testing for ALL involves various diagnostic methods such as blood tests, bone marrow tests, imaging tests, and genetic testing to diagnose and determine treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560634</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Tests">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone marrow testing is crucial for diagnosing ALL, with at least 20% lymphoblasts generally necessary.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow testing involves sampling marrow to diagnose leukemia, especially AML, before treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow tests are used to determine the presence of lymphoblasts for diagnosing ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560634</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarker and Genetic Testing">
      <data key="d0">Biomarker and Genetic Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Tests identifying genetic or biomarker mutations relevant for risk assessment and treatment planning in ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560635</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Types of Treatements for ALL">
      <data key="d0">Types of Treatements for ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Includes steroids, multiagent chemotherapy, systemic therapies, and clinical trials tailored to specific ALL subtypes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560635</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Steroids">
      <data key="d0">Steroids</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Steroids are used as part of systemic therapy to treat ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560635</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Multiagent Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Multiagent Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A standard systemic therapy involving multiple chemotherapeutic agents used to treat ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560635</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Subtypes of B-cell ALL; clinical trial is often the preferred treatment option.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560635</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Subtype of ALL characterized by activity of the BCR::ABL protein, requiring intensive systemic therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560635</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T-ALL">
      <data key="d0">T-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, a less common type of leukemia starting in T-lymphocytes, treatable with clinical trials or chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;T-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (T-ALL) is a leukemia subtype originating in T-cell lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;T-ALL, also called Mature B-cell ALL or Burkitt leukemia, is a rare subtype of lymphoblastic leukemia affecting B-cell lymphoblasts and T-cell lymphoblasts, with distinct treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560635</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Infant ALL">
      <data key="d0">Infant ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Subtype of ALL affecting infants under 12 months, with specific treatment considerations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560635</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Patient advocacy resources">
      <data key="d0">Patient advocacy resources</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Includes organizations and materials to assist patients and caregivers seeking information and support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560635</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Advocate">
      <data key="d0">Advocate</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Encourages active involvement in the child's care, making shared decisions with the medical team.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560635</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic and Biomarker Mutation Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic and Biomarker Mutation Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Identifies genetic alterations that influence risk stratification and personalized treatment decisions in ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560635</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Results and Diagnosis">
      <data key="d0">Results and Diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnosis depends on detecting at least 20% lymphoblasts in bone marrow; tests in blood, marrow, and organs are used to inform treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560635</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Cell Formation">
      <data key="d0">Blood Cell Formation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood cell formation is the biological process where blood stem cells differentiate into various types of blood cells, including red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560635</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bone marrow">
      <data key="d0">Bone marrow</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone marrow is the tissue inside bones where blood cell formation occurs and is a primary site for detecting lymphoblasts in ALL diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the tissue inside bones where blood cells, including lymphoblasts, are produced from stem cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow involvement is more common in non-mediastinal gray zone lymphomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560635</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CNS (Central Nervous System)">
      <data key="d0">CNS (Central Nervous System)</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The CNS includes the brain and spinal cord, which can be affected in ALL, with detection of leukemia cells in cerebrospinal fluid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560636</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Polyps are non-cancerous growths in the colon and rectum, including hyperplastic and inflammatory types, which rarely turn into cancer but can be precursors to rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyps are non-cancerous growths in the inner lining of the colon and rectum that can develop into invasive colon cancer if not removed.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyps are abnormal tissue growths that can develop in the colon, including hyperplastic and inflammatory types, and can potentially lead to colon cancer if not removed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560636</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Polypectomy">
      <data key="d0">Polypectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Polypectomy is a minor surgical procedure performed during colonoscopy to remove polyps and prevent cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A minor surgical procedure using colonoscopy to remove polyps.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypectomy is a minimally invasive surgical procedure performed during colonoscopy to remove polyps from the colon and rectum.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypectomy is a minor surgical procedure performed during a colonoscopy to remove polyps and prevent cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypectomy is a minor surgical procedure performed during colonoscopy to remove polyps from the colon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560636</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Family health history">
      <data key="d0">Family health history</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Family health history involves information about inherited risks for rectal cancer, including gene mutations like Lynch syndrome and familial adenomatous polyposis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560636</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch repair (MMR) genes">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch repair (MMR) genes</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Genes responsible for fixing damaged DNA; mutations in these genes are involved in some rectal cancers and are analyzed via testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560636</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lynch syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Lynch syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Lynch syndrome is an inherited genetic condition that increases the risk of colorectal, endometrial, and ovarian cancers caused by mutations in mismatch repair genes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560636</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP)">
      <data key="d0">Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">FAP is an inherited syndrome characterized by hundreds to thousands of polyps in the colon and rectum that may become invasive cancer over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560636</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic counselor">
      <data key="d0">Genetic counselor</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A genetic counselor is a healthcare professional who advises patients and families about genetic testing and inherited cancer syndromes related to rectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560637</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood tests">
      <data key="d0">Blood tests</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood tests used include complete blood count (CBC), chemistry profile, carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA), and circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) testing for diagnosis, monitoring, and detection of residual disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood tests include CBC, BUN, creatinine, and liver function tests to monitor health and detect recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560637</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair (MMR) Genes">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair (MMR) Genes</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Genes responsible for fixing damaged DNA; mutations in these genes are involved in some rectal cancers and are analyzed via testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560637</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Microsatellite Instability-High (MSI-H)">
      <data key="d0">Microsatellite Instability-High (MSI-H)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A biomarker indicating deficiencies in mismatch repair, used in rectal cancer diagnostics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560637</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Microsatellite Stable (MSS)">
      <data key="d0">Microsatellite Stable (MSS)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A term for tumors without mismatch repair deficiencies, indicating microsatellite stability.&lt;SEP&gt;MSS is a subtype of stable genetic condition relevant to rectal cancer screening and research.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560637</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Inherited Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Inherited Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic mutations passed through families that increase cancer risk, such as in Lynch syndrome or FAP.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560637</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer App">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer App</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app offers accessible guidance and information for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560637</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoblasts">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoblasts</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphoblasts are immature lymphoid cells that develop from lymphoid progenitors and are involved in the early stages of lymphocyte development. These cells play a crucial role in the formation of mature lymphocytes, which are essential components of the immune system. Under normal conditions, lymphoblasts are present in the bone marrow as part of the normal hematopoietic process, but their levels are tightly regulated.

In the context of disease, lymphoblasts are typically associated with acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), where they proliferate excessively and abnormally. In ALL, lymphoblasts proliferate massively, crowding out and disrupting the production of normal blood cells, leading to symptoms related to anemia, infection, and bleeding. The percentage of lymphoblasts in the bone marrow is an important diagnostic criterion for ALL, with a threshold of less than 20% often used; however, in certain cases, even lower levels can be indicative of the disease. Their abnormal proliferation can occur not only in bone marrow but also in the blood, impairing normal hematopoiesis.

Additionally, lymphoblasts may also proliferate in blast phase chronic myeloid leukemia (CML), which involves a sudden increase in immature lymphoid cells. Overall, the presence and levels of lymphoblasts in blood or bone marrow are key diagnostic markers in hematologic malignancies, particularly ALL, reflecting their critical role in disease development and progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560638</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">T Lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">T lymphocytes, also called T cells, develop in the thymus from lymphocytes originating in the bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;T lymphocytes help fight infections, cancer, and regulate immune responses.&lt;SEP&gt;T lymphocytes are immune cells involved in fighting infections, killing tumor cells, and immune regulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560638</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Classic Hodgkin Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Classic Hodgkin Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">Most people with Hodgkin lymphoma have classic Hodgkin lymphoma (CHL), characterized by Reed-Sternberg cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560638</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nodular Lymphocyte-Predominant Hodgkin Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Nodular Lymphocyte-Predominant Hodgkin Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">NLPHL is a rare form of Hodgkin lymphoma characterized by popcorn-shaped cells, which can grow slowly or become aggressive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560638</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Popcorn-Shaped Cells">
      <data key="d0">Popcorn-Shaped Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">Popcorn-shaped cells are characteristic of NLPHL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560638</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gallbladder and Bile Ducts">
      <data key="d0">Gallbladder and Bile Ducts</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Gallbladder and bile ducts are covered by a thin, moist serous membrane called serosa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560638</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Muscle">
      <data key="d0">Muscle</data>
      <data key="d1">tissue</data>
      <data key="d2">Muscles are soft tissues in the layers of the bile duct wall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560638</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Perimuscular Fibrous Tissue">
      <data key="d0">Perimuscular Fibrous Tissue</data>
      <data key="d1">connectivetissue</data>
      <data key="d2">Perimuscular fibrous tissue surrounds muscle layers in the bile duct.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560638</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bile Duct">
      <data key="d0">Bile Duct</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The bile duct is composed of multiple layers, including membranes, muscle, and connective tissue, and is involved in bile transport and staging in cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The bile duct is composed of multiple layers, including membranes, muscle, and connective tissue, and transports bile.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560639</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Endorectal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Endorectal Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An ultrasound technique that uses sound waves to produce images of the pelvis, especially for assessing rectal cancer extent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560639</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Transposition">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Transposition</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian transposition, or oophoropexy, is a surgery moving ovaries out of the radiation range to preserve hormonal function and fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgical procedure, also called oophoropexy, moves ovaries out of radiation range to preserve hormonal function.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery moving ovaries out of the radiation field to preserve ovarian function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560639</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair Deficiency">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair Deficiency</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mismatch repair deficiency (dMMR) indicates a genetic defect in DNA repair mechanisms, associated with certain cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Mismatch repair deficiency (dMMR) is a biomarker indicating genetic features of some colon cancers, guiding testing and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Mismatch repair deficiency (dMMR) indicates failure in DNA repair mechanisms, associated with Lynch syndrome and cancer development.&lt;SEP&gt;Mismatch repair deficiency (dMMR) is a genetic feature affecting some thyroid cancers and their response to immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560639</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sedative">
      <data key="d0">Sedative</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A medication given to help patients relax during MRI procedures, especially if they experience nervousness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560639</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic MRI">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnetic resonance imaging of the pelvis used to assess local tumor extent and lymph node involvement in rectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560639</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="POI">
      <data key="d0">POI</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Premature ovarian insufficiency, a condition caused by radiation damage to ovaries, resulting in early menopause and infertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560639</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Reproductive">
      <data key="d0">Reproductive</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Relates to the ability to conceive and have children, affected by cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560639</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rectal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Rectal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Rectal cancer is a malignant growth originating from the lining of the rectum, which can involve polyps, lesions, and invasion into surrounding tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560640</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Family Health History">
      <data key="d0">Family Health History</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Information about genetic background that may influence lung cancer risk, important for risk assessment and screening decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Family health history involves information about health conditions among relatives, important for assessing cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560640</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="MRI (Magnetic Resonance Imaging)">
      <data key="d0">MRI (Magnetic Resonance Imaging)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">MRI of the pelvis is used to determine the extent of rectal cancer and involve staging techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;Uses radio waves and magnets to produce detailed images of internal organs, helping to locate tumors and determine cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI is an imaging technique used to visualize the brain's interior and identify potential primary CNS lymphoma, often with contrast agent.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI uses radio waves and magnets to create detailed images of the brain and spinal cord for diagnosing primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560640</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CT (Computed Tomography)">
      <data key="d0">CT (Computed Tomography)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">CT of the chest and abdomen helps identify if rectal cancer has spread to other organs like the liver or lungs.&lt;SEP&gt;CT scans use X-rays to produce 3D images of the brain when MRI is unavailable or unsuitable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560640</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Polyp">
      <data key="d0">Polyp</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A polyp is an overgrowth of cells on the inner lining of the rectal wall, which may contain cancerous or benign tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;A polyp is an overgrowth of cells on the inner lining of the colon wall, which can be shaped like mushrooms (pedunculated) or flat (sessile).&lt;SEP&gt;A polyp is an overgrowth of cells on the colon wall's inner lining, which can develop into invasive cancer over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560640</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pedunculated Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Pedunculated Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Shape of polyps with a stalk, resembling mushrooms, protruding from the colon wall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560640</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sessile Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Sessile Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Flat, stalkless polyps that are more likely to recur after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560640</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Endoscopic Submucosal Dissection (ESD)">
      <data key="d0">Endoscopic Submucosal Dissection (ESD)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A minimally invasive procedure to remove small rectal cancers in one piece, often curative.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure to remove T1 tumors that haven't invaded beyond the second layer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560640</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Endoscopic Mucosal Resection (EMR)">
      <data key="d0">Endoscopic Mucosal Resection (EMR)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Alternative name for ESD, used for removing small tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560641</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T1 Tumor">
      <data key="d0">T1 Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor confined to the first (muscle) layer of the rectal wall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560641</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Transanal Local Excision">
      <data key="d0">Transanal Local Excision</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical technique for removing tumors located at the end of the rectum through the anus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560641</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Transanal Endoscopic Microsurgery (TEM)">
      <data key="d0">Transanal Endoscopic Microsurgery (TEM)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A technique for removing higher rectal tumors minimally invasively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560641</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Transanal Minimally Invasive Surgery (TAMIS)">
      <data key="d0">Transanal Minimally Invasive Surgery (TAMIS)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Another minimally invasive method for removing rectal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560641</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Removal">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Removal</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">General term for resection or excision of rectal tissue containing cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560641</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Polyps with Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Polyps with Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Polyps with cancer are growths on the rectal lining that contain malignant cells, capable of developing into invasive cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyps with cancer are overgrowths that have developed into invasive cancer within the colon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560641</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Magnetic Resonance Imaging">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Magnetic Resonance Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic MRI is an imaging technique used to determine the extent of rectal cancer and assess invasion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560641</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Post-Treatment Surveillance">
      <data key="d0">Post-Treatment Surveillance</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Post-treatment surveillance involves regular sigmoidoscopies, colonoscopies, and imaging to monitor for cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560641</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Transanal Endoscopic Microsurgery">
      <data key="d0">Transanal Endoscopic Microsurgery</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">TEM is a minimally invasive technique for removing higher rectal tumors through the anus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560641</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Transanal Minimally Invasive Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Transanal Minimally Invasive Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">TAMIS is an advanced minimally invasive surgical technique for excising rectal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560641</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="High-Risk Features">
      <data key="d0">High-Risk Features</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Indicators identified by testing removed tissue that suggest a higher likelihood of cancer recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;High-risk features refer to clinical or histological findings in removed tissue indicating higher likelihood of recurrence, prompting additional treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;High-risk features include tumor size, invasion, metastasis, or unfavorable pathology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560641</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Transabdominal Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Transabdominal Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical approach involving incision into the abdomen to remove rectal tumors and surrounding tissues, including lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560641</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Low Anterior Resection">
      <data key="d0">Low Anterior Resection</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of transabdominal surgery that removes tumors in the mid to upper rectum along with part of the sigmoid colon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560641</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Colorectal Anastomosis">
      <data key="d0">Colorectal Anastomosis</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical connection of the colon to the remaining rectum after resection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560641</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Coloanal Anastomosis">
      <data key="d0">Coloanal Anastomosis</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Connecting the remaining colon directly to the anus if the rectal connection isn't possible.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560641</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Irinotecan">
      <data key="d0">Irinotecan</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A chemotherapy drug with “IRI” in its name, associated with side effects such as abdominal cramping, nausea, diarrhea, and hair loss.&lt;SEP&gt;Irinotecan is a chemotherapy drug contained in regimens that cause side effects like abdominal cramping, nausea, diarrhea, and hair loss.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560642</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Kidney Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of cancer originating in the kidneys, with various subtypes and mechanisms of spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Kidney cancer, also known as renal cell carcinoma, starts in the kidney tissues and is a common type of cancer in the organ.&lt;SEP&gt;Kidney cancer starts in the kidney tissues and is primarily composed of carcinomas, especially renal cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560642</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="RCC (Renal Cell Carcinoma)">
      <data key="d0">RCC (Renal Cell Carcinoma)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The most common subtype of kidney cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560642</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney Function">
      <data key="d0">Kidney Function</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The kidneys filter blood, remove waste, and regulate hormones related to blood pressure and red blood cell production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560642</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rectal Wall">
      <data key="d0">Rectal Wall</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The rectal wall is a part of the rectum involved in tumor staging, with invasion indicating a T2 tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560642</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney">
      <data key="d0">Kidney</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The kidneys are a pair of organs located behind other organs in the abdomen, responsible for filtering blood and producing hormones.&lt;SEP&gt;Each kidney works independently and many people live healthy lives with only one functioning kidney.&lt;SEP&gt;Most individuals have two kidneys, but functioning with only one is possible for full health.&lt;SEP&gt;The kidney is an organ responsible for filtering blood, removing waste, and regulating fluid and electrolyte balance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560642</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Renal Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">subtype</data>
      <data key="d2">Renal cell carcinoma is the most common subtype of kidney cancer, originating from the cells lining the renal tubules.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560642</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Wilms Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Wilms Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Wilms tumor is a kidney cancer that typically occurs in young children.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560642</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Sarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Renal Sarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">subtype</data>
      <data key="d2">Renal sarcoma begins in the blood vessels or connective tissue of the kidney.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney and Adrenal Gland">
      <data key="d0">Kidney and Adrenal Gland</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The kidney and adrenal gland are located behind other organs in the abdomen, surrounded by fat and fibrous tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gerotas Fascia">
      <data key="d0">Gerotas Fascia</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Gerota’s fascia is an outer fibrous layer covering the fat surrounding the kidney and adrenal gland; its invasion indicates advanced kidney cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vena Cava">
      <data key="d0">Vena Cava</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The vena cava is a large vein that carries filtered, clean blood from the renal vein back to the heart.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Artery">
      <data key="d0">Renal Artery</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The renal artery supplies blood to the kidney, carrying waste-laden blood into the organ.&lt;SEP&gt;The renal artery supplies blood to the kidney.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Vein">
      <data key="d0">Renal Vein</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The renal vein carries filtered blood away from the kidney to the vena cava.&lt;SEP&gt;The renal vein carries filtered blood away from the kidney.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Tubules">
      <data key="d0">Renal Tubules</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Renal tubules are tiny filtering structures within the kidney that remove waste and excess substances from blood to produce urine.&lt;SEP&gt;Renal tubules are small tubes in the kidney where waste is removed from blood to produce urine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Windpipe">
      <data key="d0">Windpipe</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The windpipe (trachea) is a main airway that splits into bronchi, facilitating airflow into the lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Oxygen">
      <data key="d0">Oxygen</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Oxygen is a gas transferred from air into the blood in the alveoli, essential for respiration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">NSCLC is a type of lung cancer originating from specific cell types, forming from the lining cells of alveoli, bronchi, or large cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Most common lung cancer type, including subtypes such as adenocarcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma, and large cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Large Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Large Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Large cell carcinoma is a type of NSCLC arising from large cells found throughout the airways.&lt;SEP&gt;Subtype of NSCLC characterized by large cells that can grow anywhere in the lungs.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of non-small cell lung cancer starting from large undifferentiated cells that can grow anywhere in the lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation therapy">
      <data key="d0">Radiation therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation therapy uses high-energy rays to destroy cancer cells, sometimes used when surgery isn't suitable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical trials">
      <data key="d0">Clinical trials</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical trials are research studies testing new treatments or approaches for lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Supportive care">
      <data key="d0">Supportive care</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Supportive care addresses symptoms and improves quality of life for lung cancer patients, extending survival.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care encompasses services like pain management, emotional, and spiritual support for metastatic cervical cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nodule">
      <data key="d0">Nodule</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A nodule is a small mass of tissue in the lung that can be benign or malignant and is often detected incidentally.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Nodule Experts">
      <data key="d0">Lung Nodule Experts</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Experts involved in evaluating lung nodules include pulmonologists, thoracic radiologists, and thoracic surgeons.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thoracic Radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Thoracic Radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A thoracic radiologist specializes in chest imaging to help distinguish benign from malignant nodules.&lt;SEP&gt;A doctor specializing in imaging of the chest to identify potential abnormalities such as nodules.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thoracic Surgeon">
      <data key="d0">Thoracic Surgeon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A thoracic surgeon performs operations within the chest, including procedures to remove or biopsy lung nodules.&lt;SEP&gt;A doctor expert in performing surgical procedures within the chest cavity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Clear Cell Renal Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Clear Cell Renal Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Clear cell RCC (ccRCC) is the most common subtype of renal cell carcinoma, characterized by pale or clear appearing cells under a microscope.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Clear Cell Renal Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Non-Clear Cell Renal Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-clear cell RCC (nccRCC) includes multiple subtypes such as papillary RCC, chromophobe RCC, translocation RCC, and unclassified RCC, each with distinct features.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Papillary Renal Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Papillary Renal Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Papillary RCC (PRCC) is a subtype of nccRCC, often appearing as long, thin finger-like growths, and accounts for about 1 out of 10 RCC cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chromophobe Renal Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Chromophobe Renal Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromophobe RCC cells are larger than clear cells and have distinctive pale appearance, representing a subtype of nccRCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Translocation RCC">
      <data key="d0">Translocation RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Translocation RCC occurs at all ages but is more commonly diagnosed in children or young adults, characterized by gene fusions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Unclassified RCC">
      <data key="d0">Unclassified RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Unclassified RCC describes cancer cells that do not resemble any specific subtype or involve multiple subtypes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560644</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Collecting Duct RCC">
      <data key="d0">Collecting Duct RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Collecting duct RCC (cdRCC) develops in the cells of kidney collecting ducts or tubules.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Medullary Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Renal Medullary Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Renal medullary carcinoma (RMC) primarily affects young people of African descent and is a rare RCC type.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Clear Cell Papillary Renal Cell Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Clear Cell Papillary Renal Cell Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">A recently defined RCC subtype characterized by clear cells with papillary features.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fumarate Hydratase-Deficient RCC">
      <data key="d0">Fumarate Hydratase-Deficient RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Fumarate hydratase-deficient RCC is a rare RCC type with specific metabolic enzyme deficiency.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="ELOC (TCEB1)-Mutated RCC">
      <data key="d0">ELOC (TCEB1)-Mutated RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">ELOC (formerly TCEB1)-mutated RCC involves specific genetic mutations impacting tumor development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sarcomatoid Renal Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Sarcomatoid Renal Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Sarcomatoid RCC can occur in any RCC type, characterized by sarcoma-like cellular features, and tends to grow and spread faster.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Local Spread">
      <data key="d0">Local Spread</data>
      <data key="d1">process</data>
      <data key="d2">Local spread refers to kidney cancer invading nearby tissues such as fat, blood vessels, or lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Risk Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Risk Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic testing assesses hereditary factors influencing esophageal cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic risk testing identifies inherited mutations that may predispose individuals to RCC.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic risk testing assesses inherited gene mutations that may increase cancer susceptibility, guiding treatment and family risk evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560645</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Renal VenaCava">
      <data key="d0">Renal VenaCava</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The renal vena cava is a large vein that carries filtered blood away from the kidney.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560646</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gerota's Fascia">
      <data key="d0">Gerota's Fascia</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Gerota's fascia is a fibrous tissue layer surrounding the kidney, acting as an anatomical boundary.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560646</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Friends">
      <data key="d0">Friends</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Friends can offer companionship, assistance with transportation, meals, and emotional support to individuals undergoing medical diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560646</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Peers">
      <data key="d0">Peers</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Peers can assist with transportation, emotional support, and help navigate healthcare processes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560646</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="B Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">B Lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">B lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell that produces antibodies and help fight infections and cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;B lymphocytes are white blood cells responsible for antibody production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560646</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Natural Killer Cells">
      <data key="d0">Natural Killer Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">Natural killer (NK) cells are immune cells capable of killing tumor or virus-infected cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Natural killer cells are immune cells capable of killing tumor or virus-infected cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560646</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphatic Tissue">
      <data key="d0">Lymphatic Tissue</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphatic tissue includes lymph vessels and lymph nodes that support lymphocyte function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560646</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphocyte">
      <data key="d0">Lymphocyte</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell involved in the immune response, and their type (B cell or T cell) within leukemia cells influences the immunophenotype of the leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;A lymphocyte is a type of white blood cell involved in immune defense, including B cells, T cells, and natural killer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560646</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Progenitor Cells">
      <data key="d0">Progenitor Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">Progenitor cells are immature cells derived from blood stem cells that develop into specific blood cell lineages.&lt;SEP&gt;Progenitor cells are intermediate cells that give rise to specific blood cell types such as red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets.&lt;SEP&gt;Progenitor cells, including lymphoid and myeloid progenitors, are intermediate cells that develop into specific blood cells like lymphocytes and other non-lymphoid cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560646</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Teeth">
      <data key="d0">Teeth</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Teeth include the upper and lower jaws, along with surrounding structures and tissues involved in oral health and dental procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560646</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dentists">
      <data key="d0">Dentists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Dentists are healthcare professionals who perform dental procedures such as planning treatments for dentures, braces, extractions, and implants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560646</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Oral Surgeons">
      <data key="d0">Oral Surgeons</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Oral surgeons are specialists in surgical procedures involving the mouth, jaws, and facial structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560646</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vaping">
      <data key="d0">Vaping</data>
      <data key="d1">behavior</data>
      <data key="d2">The act of inhaling vaporized nicotine or other substances, which increases cancer risk and affects wound healing.&lt;SEP&gt;Vaping is inhaling vaporized nicotine or cannabis using electronic devices, which can impact cancer risk and treatment efficacy.&lt;SEP&gt;Vaping, or using electronic cigarettes, can similarly impair treatment results and increase health risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Vaping is a concern similar to smoking, as it can affect treatment outcomes and overall health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560646</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cannabis">
      <data key="d0">Cannabis</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Cannabis is a psychoactive substance that might influence anesthesia needs during surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560646</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Genes">
      <data key="d0">Genes</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Genes are coded instructions within DNA for producing proteins; mutations can affect cancer development and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Genes are units of hereditary information that can be analyzed for mutations affecting their function.&lt;SEP&gt;Genes are segments of DNA that contain instructions for making proteins, and they can undergo mutations affecting cell functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Genes are instructions within chromosomes that encode proteins; mutations can affect cell behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Genes are units of heredity coded within DNA that can be tested for mutations, such as BRCA1 and BRCA2.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560646</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Profiling">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Profiling</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor profiling includes genetic and molecular testing of tumor tissue to identify treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor profiling is the process of analyzing genetic and molecular features of leukemia cells to inform prognosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor profiling involves analyzing tumor characteristics such as gene expression, mutations, and protein levels to guide treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560646</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsy-Guided Imaging">
      <data key="d0">Biopsy-Guided Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Ultrasound or CT scans can guide biopsies of lymph nodes or metastases for precise sampling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560647</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Leukemia is a type of cancer affecting blood and bone marrow, involving abnormal white blood cell proliferation.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia is a type of blood cancer originating in the bone marrow, affecting blood cell production.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia is a blood cancer that can spread to surrounding fluids and tissues, affecting organs such as the spine and brain.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia is a type of blood cancer that can spread outside the bloodstream to organs like the lymph nodes, liver, and spleen.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia is a type of blood cancer characterized by the overproduction of abnormal lymphocytes in the bone marrow and blood.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia is a blood cancer that originates in the bone marrow, affecting blood cell production.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia is a blood cancer that can spread to the spinal fluid or brain, causing symptoms like headaches and neck pain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560647</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">B-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (B-ALL) is a subtype of leukemia originating in B-cell lymphocytes, more common than T-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560647</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="B-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">B-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma (B-LL or B-LBL) occurs when B-cell lymphoblasts grow mainly in lymph nodes instead of in the bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560647</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">T-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma (T-LL or T-LBL) occurs when T-cell lymphoblasts grow mainly in the thymus instead of in the bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560647</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adolescent and Young Adult">
      <data key="d0">Adolescent and Young Adult</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An AYA is a person aged 15 to 39 years at the time of initial cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560647</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adult Cancer Center">
      <data key="d0">Adult Cancer Center</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">An adult cancer center is a medical facility specializing in treating adults with cancers like ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560647</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Tests">
      <data key="d0">Medical Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical tests include various blood tests and imaging procedures used to diagnose and monitor patient health, particularly in relation to leukemia and other conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical tests include blood tests, tissue tests, genetic risk testing, imaging, lumbar puncture, and heart tests used to diagnose and plan treatment for ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical tests such as general health tests, imaging, biopsy, and biomarker testing are used in the diagnosis and treatment planning of NPC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560647</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="AML Subtypes">
      <data key="d0">AML Subtypes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">AML has many subtypes categorized by gene mutations, abnormal chromosomes, and other molecular factors, influencing treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560649</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="PML::RARA">
      <data key="d0">PML::RARA</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">The PML::RARA fusion gene is associated with acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL), a subtype of AML.&lt;SEP&gt;PML::RARA is a specific gene rearrangement involved in certain types of leukemia, detectable through gene testing methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560649</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Testing">
      <data key="d0">Blood Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood tests are diagnostic procedures used to analyze blood components and genetic or biomarker mutations related to AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560649</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Cell Differentiation">
      <data key="d0">Blood Cell Differentiation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Differentiation is the process by which stem cells develop into mature blood cells such as red blood cells, white blood cells, or platelets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560649</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="AML Specialist">
      <data key="d0">AML Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An expert in the diagnosis and treatment of acute myeloid leukemia, providing specialized advice on AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560649</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Care">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Care</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The overall process involved in diagnosis, treatment, and management of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560649</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Myeloid Blasts">
      <data key="d0">Myeloid Blasts</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Immature blood cells in the bone marrow, essential for diagnosing AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560649</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Abnormal Chromosomes">
      <data key="d0">Abnormal Chromosomes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromosomal changes that are associated with AML and its classification.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromosomal anomalies associated with AML prognosis and classification.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560649</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lumbar Puncture">
      <data key="d0">Lumbar Puncture</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A procedure to remove spinal fluid by inserting a needle into the lower back, also called a spinal tap.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure to collect cerebrospinal fluid for testing, especially if CNS involvement is suspected.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure to remove cerebrospinal fluid using a needle in the lower back, also called a spinal tap.&lt;SEP&gt;A lumbar puncture is a procedure to remove cerebrospinal fluid for testing, to diagnose or determine the spread of lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure that extracts cerebrospinal fluid to test for cancer cells related to central nervous system lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560649</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Clotting Tests">
      <data key="d0">Blood Clotting Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Tests that assess the blood's ability to clot, important in patients with leukemia or on certain treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Clotting tests assess the blood's ability to form clots, involving proteins called coagulation factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood tests to analyze clotting ability, relevant in leukemia diagnostics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560649</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="WBCs">
      <data key="d0">WBCs</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">White blood cells involved in immune response; abnormal counts suggest AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560649</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="HLA">
      <data key="d0">HLA</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">HLA (Human Leukocyte Antigen) is a protein on cell surfaces involved in immune response, with HLA typing used to determine compatibility for transplants.&lt;SEP&gt;Human leukocyte antigen (HLA) typing is a test used to identify compatibility for transplantation and may be relevant in certain leukemia assessments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560649</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hemoglobin">
      <data key="d0">Hemoglobin</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Hemoglobin (Hgb) is a blood measurement indicating oxygen-carrying capacity and potential need for transfusion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560649</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Absolute Neutrophil Count">
      <data key="d0">Absolute Neutrophil Count</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The absolute neutrophil count (ANC) measures cells that fight bacteria and infections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560649</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Creatinine">
      <data key="d0">Creatinine</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A waste product measured to assess kidney function.&lt;SEP&gt;Creatinine is a waste product from muscles, used to assess kidney function based on its blood level.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560649</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Electrolytes">
      <data key="d0">Electrolytes</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Ions in blood that are essential for nerve, muscle, heart, and brain function.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrolytes are ions essential for nerve, muscle, heart, and brain function, moving nutrients and waste.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560649</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Function Tests">
      <data key="d0">Liver Function Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">LFTs assess liver health through chemical levels, indicating liver function or duct blockages.&lt;SEP&gt;Liver function tests (LFTs) measure chemicals processed by the liver to assess liver health or cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Liver function tests evaluate enzyme levels to check for liver damage or cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560650</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vitamin B12">
      <data key="d0">Vitamin B12</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Vitamin B12 is a nutrient that helps the body make new proteins and is essential for red blood cell and white blood cell formation.&lt;SEP&gt;A vitamin essential for blood cell formation, monitored in blood tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Vitamin B12 helps the body produce new proteins and is important for RBC and WBC formation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560650</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Folic Acid">
      <data key="d0">Folic Acid</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Folic acid, also known as folate, helps the body make new proteins and is necessary for normal blood cell formation.&lt;SEP&gt;A vitamin involved in cell growth and blood formation, measured in blood tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Folic acid aids in protein production and is monitored to support healthy blood cell creation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560650</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Iron">
      <data key="d0">Iron</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Iron is a mineral important for producing hemoglobin, which carries oxygen in the blood, and for maintaining body functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Iron is vital for hemoglobin production and oxygen transport; monitored for deficiency or overload.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560650</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="HLA Typing">
      <data key="d0">HLA Typing</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">HLA typing is a blood test that detects an individual's HLA type to find suitable donors for cell transplants.&lt;SEP&gt;HLA typing identifies human leukocyte antigen proteins, essential for immune response and donor matching in transplants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560650</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Echocardiogram">
      <data key="d0">Echocardiogram</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">An echocardiogram uses sound waves to create images of the heart, tracking its heartbeat in real-time.&lt;SEP&gt;An echocardiogram (or echo) uses sound waves to create images of the heart, showing its structure and function, including ejection fraction and heart chamber details.&lt;SEP&gt;An ultrasound test that uses sound waves with patches and a wand to visualize the structure and function of the heart.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560650</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hearts">
      <data key="d0">Hearts</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The heart is a vital muscular organ that pumps blood throughout the body, with its function measurable by ejection fraction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560650</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="QTc">
      <data key="d0">QTc</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">QTc is a measurement indicating the heart's electrical activity; a prolonged QTc can lead to dangerous heart rhythms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560650</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Spinal Fluid">
      <data key="d0">Spinal Fluid</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Spinal fluid surrounds the spine and brain; its analysis can detect leukemia cells in cases of leukemia spread.&lt;SEP&gt;The fluid surrounding the spine and brain, which may contain leukemia cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560651</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarker Tests">
      <data key="d0">Biomarker Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Tests used to identify specific genetic or molecular features of AML to help determine treatment and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Tests analyzing specific proteins or gene mutations to assess AML subtype and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker tests profile specific features of pancreatic cancer to guide targeted therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Additional blood or urine tests, such as 4Kscore, PHI, IsoPSA, ExoDx, PCA3, among others, used to help determine the need for further prostate cancer testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560651</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Center">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A specialized medical facility providing treatment and diagnosis for cancer types, including AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560651</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hospital">
      <data key="d0">Hospital</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical institution where AML diagnosis, treatment, and testing are performed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560651</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hematologist">
      <data key="d0">Hematologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical doctor specialized in blood diseases and cancers, including AML.&lt;SEP&gt;A hematologist is a medical expert specialized in blood diseases and blood cancers, providing diagnosis and treatment for leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560651</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hematologic Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Hematologic Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A specialist in cancerous blood disorders that treats AML using various therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;A hematologic oncologist is a cancer specialist focused on blood cancers, involved in treating AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560651</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hematopoietic Cell Transplant">
      <data key="d0">Hematopoietic Cell Transplant</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A procedure to replace diseased bone marrow with healthy stem cells, used in AML treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure replacing diseased blood-forming cells with healthy ones to treat AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560651</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Remission">
      <data key="d0">Remission</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Remission is the phase where leukemia cells are no longer detectable in the blood or bone marrow after treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The state where AML is undetectable after treatment, representing a treatment goal.&lt;SEP&gt;The goal of AML therapy, indicating absence of detectable disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Remission means the absence of symptoms or signs of cancer and can last months or years.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560651</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Karnofsky Performance Status">
      <data key="d0">Karnofsky Performance Status</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS) is a scale used to evaluate the functional status of patients, influencing treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560652</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Immunohistochemistry">
      <data key="d0">Immunohistochemistry</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunohistochemistry is a staining process that uses chemical markers and microscopes to study cell immunophenotype from tissue samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunohistochemistry (IHC) is a staining process that uses chemicals to detect the immunophenotype of cells in biopsy or tissue samples.&lt;SEP&gt;A staining process used to determine receptor status (including HER2) in tissue samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunohistochemistry (IHC) involves staining cells with chemical markers for microscopic examination to analyze cancer or immune cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560652</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="FLT Genes">
      <data key="d0">FLT Genes</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">FLT genes are specific genes involved in AML, and changes in these genes can influence disease and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560652</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Changes">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Changes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic changes in AML include mutations, deletions, insertions, inversions, amplifications, translocations, and point mutations, which inform treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic changes in leukemia cells reflect mutations that develop over time and are used for prognosis and treatment guidance.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic changes in AML include deletions, insertions, inversions, amplifications, translocations, and point mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic changes refer to alterations in DNA that can be detected through blood or tissue testing to assess leukemia risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560652</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gene Rearrangement">
      <data key="d0">Gene Rearrangement</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Gene rearrangement refers to the switching or reorganization of genes, exemplified by RUNX1::RUNX1T.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560652</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Leukemia Predisposition Syndromes">
      <data key="d0">Leukemia Predisposition Syndromes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Hereditary syndromes that increase the risk of leukemia, possibly detected through familial or genetic testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia predisposition syndromes are inherited genetic conditions that increase the risk of developing leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560652</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hematopathology">
      <data key="d0">Hematopathology</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Hematopathology is a specialized branch of pathology focusing on blood-related diseases, including AML diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560652</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rearrangement">
      <data key="d0">Rearrangement</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Gene rearrangement involves switching parts of genes, often seen in translocations, impacting leukemia diagnosis and classification.&lt;SEP&gt;Gene rearrangement involves structural changes in genes, such as RUNX1::RUNX1T.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560652</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hereditary Cancer Syndromes">
      <data key="d0">Hereditary Cancer Syndromes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Hereditary syndromes are genetic conditions passed down that increase leukemia risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560653</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="RUNX1">
      <data key="d0">RUNX1</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">RUNX1 is a gene involved in leukemia predisposition syndromes and genetic alterations associated with AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560653</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="RUNX1T">
      <data key="d0">RUNX1T</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">RUNX1T is a gene partner in leukemia predisposition syndromes, often involved in genetic anomalies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560653</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="AML Mutation Testing">
      <data key="d0">AML Mutation Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">AML mutation testing includes techniques like karyotype, FISH, NGS, and PCR to detect genetic abnormalities in AML cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560653</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cytogenetics">
      <data key="d0">Cytogenetics</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Cytogenetics, including karyotyping, assesses chromosomal abnormalities in leukemia cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Cytogenetics involves analyzing chromosomes for abnormalities using methods such as karyotyping.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560653</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="FISH (Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization)">
      <data key="d0">FISH (Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">FISH uses fluorescent probes to detect specific DNA abnormalities in cells, aiding in leukemia diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560653</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Next-Generation Sequencing">
      <data key="d0">Next-Generation Sequencing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">NGS determines detailed DNA sequences to find mutations that might influence leukemia treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560653</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Polymerase Chain Reaction">
      <data key="d0">Polymerase Chain Reaction</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">PCR amplifies specific DNA or RNA sequences to detect genetic mutations or gene rearrangements in leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560653</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="AML Mutations">
      <data key="d0">AML Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">geneticabnormality</data>
      <data key="d2">Mutations in AML are specific genetic changes that can influence diagnosis, prognosis, and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560654</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gene Rearrangements">
      <data key="d0">Gene Rearrangements</data>
      <data key="d1">geneticabnormality</data>
      <data key="d2">Gene rearrangements like PML::RARA are genetic alterations relevant to leukemia diagnosis and response monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560654</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Leukemia Spread">
      <data key="d0">Leukemia Spread</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Leukemia can spread outside the bone marrow to areas such as lymph nodes, liver, spleen, and skin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560654</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Brain CT Scan">
      <data key="d0">Brain CT Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">A brain CT scan uses X-rays to quickly produce detailed images of the brain, used to detect bleeding or abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;A brain CT scan uses X-rays to examine the brain for bleeding or other abnormalities related to leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560654</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Brain MRI Scan">
      <data key="d0">Brain MRI Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">An MRI of the brain uses magnets and radio waves to produce images, revealing swelling or leukemic meningitis.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan employs magnetic fields to visualize the brain, detecting swelling or leukemic meningitis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560654</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Leukemic Meningitis">
      <data key="d0">Leukemic Meningitis</data>
      <data key="d1">condition</data>
      <data key="d2">Leukemic meningitis is an infection of the membranes covering the brain and spinal cord caused by leukemia cells infiltrating the central nervous system.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemic meningitis is swelling of the brain's outer layer caused by leukemia infiltration, detectable via MRI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560654</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 4">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 4</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 4 discusses treatment by risk and recurrence, emphasizing the importance of understanding cancer recurrence and management.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 4 describes different types of treatment and the treatment team involved.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560654</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 3">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 3</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 3 explains the various types of cancer treatments available.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 3 explains how bone cancer is staged and the locations of bone tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560654</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Risk">
      <data key="d0">Risk</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk refers to the possibility of cancer recurrence or metastasis, influencing treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560654</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenal Gland">
      <data key="d0">Adrenal Gland</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Adrenal glands are small, yellow, triangle-shaped glands located on top of each kidney.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560654</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Benign Adrenal Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Benign Adrenal Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Benign tumors do not spread and are usually nonfunctional but can sometimes produce hormones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560654</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Functional Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Functional Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Functional adrenal tumors can produce excess hormones and may cause symptoms or be detected through hormone tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560654</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Malignant Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Malignant Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Malignant adrenal tumors are cancerous, rare, and may grow or spread aggressively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560654</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Aldosteronoma">
      <data key="d0">Aldosteronoma</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Aldosteronoma is an adrenal tumor that produces excess aldosterone, affecting hormone balance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560654</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cushing Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Cushing Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Cushing syndrome results from excessive cortisol produced by adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560654</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Technologist">
      <data key="d0">Technologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A technologist operates MRI machines and must be informed about any metal in the patient's body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560654</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Metal">
      <data key="d0">Metal</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Metal refers to any metallic objects inside the body that could interfere with MRI scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560655</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Claustrophobia">
      <data key="d0">Claustrophobia</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Claustrophobia is the fear of enclosed spaces, which may affect patients undergoing MRI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560655</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bright Spots">
      <data key="d0">Bright Spots</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Bright spots on PET scans indicate areas of high metabolic activity, possibly cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560655</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Inflammation">
      <data key="d0">Inflammation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Inflammation or infection can also produce bright spots on PET scans.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammation can show up as a bright spot on PET scans, indicating a possible abnormality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560655</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG)">
      <data key="d0">Fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">FDG is a radiotracer used in FDG-PET/CT scans to detect cancer activity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560655</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Heart Tests">
      <data key="d0">Heart Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Tests used to assess heart function and monitor treatment effects in patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Tests used to evaluate heart function and monitor treatment effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560655</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cardiologist">
      <data key="d0">Cardiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A specialist in heart health who might be consulted for heart tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560655</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Electrocardiogram (ECG or EKG)">
      <data key="d0">Electrocardiogram (ECG or EKG)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A test that records electrical activity of the heart, showing rate and rhythm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560655</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Prolonged QT Interval">
      <data key="d0">Prolonged QT Interval</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A lengthened QT interval can cause dangerous heart rhythms and may be caused by certain treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560656</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ejection Fraction">
      <data key="d0">Ejection Fraction</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The percentage of blood pumped out of the left ventricle with each heartbeat, indicating heart function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560656</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)">
      <data key="d0">Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">AML is a type of leukemia characterized by the accumulation of immature white blood cells called myeloblasts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560656</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blast">
      <data key="d0">Blast</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">Immature white blood cell involved in AML, with a diagnostic threshold of at least 20% in marrow or blood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560656</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="AML Subtype">
      <data key="d0">AML Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A classification of AML based on genetic and molecular features.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560656</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Multidisciplinary Team">
      <data key="d0">Multidisciplinary Team</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of healthcare providers collaborating for AML treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560656</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="MRI Machine">
      <data key="d0">MRI Machine</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">An MRI machine is a device that uses powerful magnets and radio waves to create detailed images of the body's interior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560656</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fever">
      <data key="d0">Fever</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Fever is a possible symptom related to infections or inflammation that might be visualized or investigated via imaging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560656</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Speed">
      <data key="d0">Speed</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">MRI scans take longer to perform compared to CT scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560656</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Radioisotope">
      <data key="d0">Radioisotope</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Radioisotopes like FDG are used in PET scans to detect cancer activity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560656</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Supply">
      <data key="d0">Blood Supply</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood supply is crucial in cancer detection as tumors often have increased blood flow detectable in imaging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560656</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Heart Rate">
      <data key="d0">Heart Rate</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Heart rate is a measurement assessed during heart tests like ECG or echocardiogram.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560656</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Heart Rhythm">
      <data key="d0">Heart Rhythm</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Heart rhythm is monitored and recorded during heart function tests such as ECG.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560656</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Pumping">
      <data key="d0">Blood Pumping</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood pumping refers to the process of blood ejection from the heart, measured by ejection fraction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560656</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gene Mutation">
      <data key="d0">Gene Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Gene mutations are genetic alterations important for AML classification and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560656</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Strategy">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Strategy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A treatment plan for AML is formulated based on diagnostic testing and patient factors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560656</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Prognostic Factors">
      <data key="d0">Prognostic Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Factors that influence the expected course and outcome of AML include genetic and biomarker results.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560657</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Aspirate and Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Aspirate and Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">These procedures evaluate bone marrow involvement in FL.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure extracting bone marrow tissue for biomarker and genetic testing to diagnose leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy are procedures used to examine marrow cells to diagnose and determine the extent of leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560657</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Port">
      <data key="d0">Port</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical port is a device used for administering treatments, customized according to patient requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;A port is a small, round disc placed under the skin in the upper chest during minor surgery to deliver chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;A port is a small disc implanted in the chest for administration of treatments and blood sampling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560657</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Antimetabolites">
      <data key="d0">Antimetabolites</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Antimetabolites are drugs that inhibit DNA synthesis by preventing the use of building blocks of DNA.&lt;SEP&gt;Antimetabolites are chemotherapy agents that inhibit DNA building blocks, such as Cladribine, Cytarabine, and Methotrexate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560657</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Anthracyclines">
      <data key="d0">Anthracyclines</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Anthracyclines are drugs that damage DNA, causing cell death, with potential heart-related side effects, used in AML therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Anthracyclines damage DNA to kill leukemia cells but may cause heart issues; examples include daunorubicin, idarubicin, mitoxantrone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560657</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cytarabine">
      <data key="d0">Cytarabine</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Cytarabine (Ara-C) is an antimetabolite used in AML treatment, administered in various doses and in specific settings like intrathecal chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Cytarabine, also called Ara-C, is used in AML treatment alone or in combination, given as a single dose or several cycles, possibly intrathecally.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560657</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hypomethylating Agents">
      <data key="d0">Hypomethylating Agents</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Hypomethylating agents (HMAs) are drugs that inhibit DNA methylation, reactivating silenced genes and promoting maturation of leukemia cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Hypomethylating agents are molecules that block methyl groups from binding to DNA, thus reactivating silenced genes in leukemia cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Hypomethylating agents are drugs like azacitidine that reactivate silenced genes in leukemia cells by blocking methyl groups.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560657</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="AML">
      <data key="d0">AML</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">AML (Acute Myeloid Leukemia) is a form of leukemia diagnosed via bone marrow tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a cancer of blood and marrow with specific genetic mutations and markers that influence diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is a type of blood cancer that is treated primarily with chemotherapy, involving cycles of drug administration.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by rapid growth of abnormal myeloid cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560657</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Intrathecal Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intrathecal Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Intrathecal chemotherapy is a method where medication is injected into the spinal fluid to treat conditions like leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;A form of chemotherapy administered directly into the spinal fluid to treat leukemia involving the central nervous system.&lt;SEP&gt;Intrathecal chemotherapy involves infusing drugs directly into the cerebrospinal fluid to target cancer cells in the cerebrospinal fluid or spine.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy administered directly into cerebrospinal fluid to treat cancer within the central nervous system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Leukemia Cells">
      <data key="d0">Leukemia Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">Leukemia cells are abnormal blood cells that often have excess methyl groups blocking gene activity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Azacitidine">
      <data key="d0">Azacitidine</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Azacitidine (Vidaza) is a hypomethylating agent used to treat AML by reversing methylation-related gene silencing.&lt;SEP&gt;Azacitidine (Vidaza) is a hypomethylating agent used to treat leukemia by reactivating silenced genes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Decitabine">
      <data key="d0">Decitabine</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Decitabine (Dacogen) is a hypomethylating agent used in AML therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Decitabine (Dacogen) is a hypomethylating agent used to treat leukemia by converting silenced genes into active ones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin">
      <data key="d0">Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Gemtuzumab ozogamicin (GO) is a targeted therapy linked to chemotherapy that attaches to the CD33 protein on leukemia cells to deliver cytotoxic agents.&lt;SEP&gt;Gemtuzumab ozogamicin (GO) is a targeted therapy linked to chemotherapy, attaching to CD33 proteins on leukemia cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CD33">
      <data key="d0">CD33</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">CD33 is a cell surface protein expressed on many leukemic blasts, targeted by specific therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;CD33 is a cell surface protein found on many leukemic blasts but not on mature blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Core Binding Factor">
      <data key="d0">Core Binding Factor</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Core binding factor (CBF) refers to genetic abnormalities in AML that affect blood cell maturation, potentially targeted by therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Core binding factor (CBF) influences the creation of mature blood cells and can be involved in leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="FLT3">
      <data key="d0">FLT3</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">FLT3 is a gene that, when mutated, can be targeted by specific drugs to treat AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gilteritinib">
      <data key="d0">Gilteritinib</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Gilteritinib is a targeted therapy used to treat AML with FLT3 mutations, such as FLT3-ITD.&lt;SEP&gt;Gilteritinib is a targeted therapy used to treat AML with FLT3 mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560659</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Quizartinib">
      <data key="d0">Quizartinib</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Quizartinib is used for AML with specific FLT3 mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Quizartinib is a targeted therapy used to treat AML with FLT3 mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560659</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Midostaurin">
      <data key="d0">Midostaurin</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Midostaurin is a targeted therapy for AML with FLT3 mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Midostaurin is a drug used to treat AML with FLT3 mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560659</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sorafenib">
      <data key="d0">Sorafenib</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Sorafenib treats AML with FLT3 mutations, such as FLT3-ITD.&lt;SEP&gt;Sorafenib is a targeted therapy used to treat AML with FLT3 mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Sorafenib (Nexavar) is a targeted treatment option for advanced thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560659</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Myeloid Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Myeloid Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) is a blood cancer caused by a specific abnormal gene involving chromosome translocation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560659</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Differentiation">
      <data key="d0">Differentiation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Differentiation is the process by which blood stem cells develop into various mature blood cell types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560659</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chromosome 22">
      <data key="d0">Chromosome 22</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromosome 22 is a human chromosome involved in the translocation process that creates the Philadelphia chromosome.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromosome 22 is involved in the translocation with chromosome 9, resulting in the Philadelphia chromosome in CML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560659</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Online Portals">
      <data key="d0">Online Portals</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Online portals are platforms that provide access to test results and facilitate communication with healthcare providers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560659</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Healthcare Provider">
      <data key="d0">Healthcare Provider</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A healthcare provider is a medical professional or team that advises patients about their health, tests, and treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dental">
      <data key="d0">Dental</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Dental refers to teeth and gums, emphasizing the importance of regular dental checkups for patients with head and neck cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="DNA Mutations">
      <data key="d0">DNA Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">DNA mutations are genetic alterations in tumor cells that may be targeted with specific therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Proteins">
      <data key="d0">Proteins</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Proteins are molecular products whose levels can be measured to inform cancer treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Proteins are biological molecules like BRCA, HER2, and hormone receptors that are analyzed in biomarker testing to assess tumor characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Molecular Features">
      <data key="d0">Molecular Features</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Molecular features include specific genetic or protein markers that characterize tumors and guide targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Feedback">
      <data key="d0">Feedback</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Feedback refers to user responses, aimed at improving the quality and clarity of provided cancer information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Survey">
      <data key="d0">Survey</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The survey is an instrument designed to gather opinions on helpfulness and clarity of cancer-related information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN.org/patients/feedback">
      <data key="d0">NCCN.org/patients/feedback</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN (National Comprehensive Cancer Network) website hosts a feedback page for patient input.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="TNM Scores">
      <data key="d0">TNM Scores</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">TNM scores are numerical values assigned to tumor, nodes, and metastasis to determine cancer stage.&lt;SEP&gt;TNM scores quantify tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis for staging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Burkitt lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Burkitt lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Burkitt lymphoma is a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that is essentially the same as T-ALL but distinguished by its treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="B-cell lymphoblasts">
      <data key="d0">B-cell lymphoblasts</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">B-cell lymphoblasts are immature B cells that grow mostly in lymph nodes, and can lead to B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma when they grow outside bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T-cell lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">T-cell lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">T-cell lymphocytes are immune cells that can develop into T-ALL and can cause an enlarged thymus or lead to T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma when they grow outside bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph nodes">
      <data key="d0">Lymph nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph nodes are organs where B-cell lymphoblasts mainly grow, involved in immune responses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood stem cells">
      <data key="d0">Blood stem cells</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood stem cells are early blood-forming cells that differentiate into various blood cell types, including lymphoid and myeloid progenitors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Myeloid progenitor cells">
      <data key="d0">Myeloid progenitor cells</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">Myeloid progenitor cells develop into other non-lymphoid blood cells, including red blood cells and certain white blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 2: Testing for ALL">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 2: Testing for ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 2 of a book provides an overview of tests related to ALL, including general health tests, biomarker and genetic testing, and questions to ask regarding diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Children">
      <data key="d0">Children</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Children are the patients diagnosed with ALL, for whom various tests and treatments are discussed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pancreatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Pancreatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Pancreatic cancer is a disease discussed in a separate chapter, including its causes, types, and treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic cancer is a disease characterized by malignant growth in the pancreas, with various stages ranging from Stage 0 to Stage 4, and associated risk factors including lifestyle, genetic, and environmental factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic cancer is a type of cancer affecting the pancreas, requiring a series of diagnostic tests to identify and plan treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Pancreatic adenocarcinoma, a type of pancreatic cancer, forms from exocrine cells lining pancreatic ducts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pancreatic Neuroendocrine Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Pancreatic Neuroendocrine Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A less common pancreatic cancer originating from endocrine cells that produce hormones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chemicals">
      <data key="d0">Chemicals</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer-causing chemicals like pesticides can increase pancreatic cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hereditary">
      <data key="d0">Hereditary</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A proportion of pancreatic cancer cases are hereditary, inherited through family genetics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Test for ALL">
      <data key="d0">Test for ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Test for ALL encompasses diagnostic tests such as blood tests, bone marrow tests, imaging, and genetic analysis to diagnose and assess treatment options for acute lymphoblastic leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CNS">
      <data key="d0">CNS</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">CNS stands for central nervous system, including the brain and spinal cord, which can be affected by leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;The central nervous system includes the brain and spinal cord and can be affected by leukemia infiltration.&lt;SEP&gt;CNS (central nervous system) comprises the brain and spinal cord, affected by lymphoma leading to neurological symptoms.&lt;SEP&gt;CNS refers to the central nervous system, which includes the brain and spinal cord, affected in lymphoma and other neurological conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pancreatic cancer">
      <data key="d0">Pancreatic cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Pancreatic cancer involves abnormal growth of cells in the pancreas, affecting its function.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic cancer is a disease involving the uncontrolled growth of cells in the pancreas, requiring various diagnostic tests for identification and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic cancer is a disease characterized by malignant growth in the pancreas, with various stages ranging from Stage 0 to Stage 4, and associated risk factors including lifestyle, genetic, and environmental factors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560662</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pesticides">
      <data key="d0">Pesticides</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Pesticides are chemicals that can increase the risk of pancreatic cancer upon exposure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560662</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma, also called pancreatic adenocarcinoma, is the most common type of pancreatic cancer arising from exocrine cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560662</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Neuroendocrine Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Neuroendocrine Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor develops from endocrine cells and produces hormones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560662</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hereditary Pancreatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Hereditary Pancreatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Hereditary pancreatic cancer is a form of the disease passed down genetically, involving higher risk due to inherited genes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560662</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hereditary Breast-Ovarian Cancer Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Hereditary Breast-Ovarian Cancer Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Hereditary breast-ovarian cancer syndrome is an inherited condition that increases the risk of pancreatic, breast, and ovarian cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560662</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Peutz-Jeghers Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Peutz-Jeghers Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is an inherited disorder associated with increased pancreatic and other cancer risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560662</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Li-Fraumeni Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Li-Fraumeni Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Li-Fraumeni syndrome is an inherited disorder linked to a higher risk of multiple cancers, including pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560662</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="FAMMM Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">FAMMM Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Family malignant melanoma syndrome, also known as FAMMM, involves inherited risks increasing the likelihood of pancreatic and melanoma cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560663</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stages of Pancreatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stages of Pancreatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The stages of pancreatic cancer range from 0 to IV and are based on tumor size and spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560663</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Surveillance and Clinical Trials">
      <data key="d0">Surveillance and Clinical Trials</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Research and clinical trials are ongoing efforts to improve treatments and prognosis for pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560663</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tests for Pancreatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Tests for Pancreatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A range of diagnostic tests including imaging, endoscopic tests, biopsy, blood tests, genetic tests, and biomarker tests are used to identify pancreatic cancer and inform treatment plans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560663</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Multidisciplinary Consult">
      <data key="d0">Multidisciplinary Consult</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A multidisciplinary consultation involves various healthcare providers reviewing tests, symptoms, and signs to determine diagnosis and treatment options for pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560663</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Health Care Providers">
      <data key="d0">Health Care Providers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A team of multidisciplinary healthcare providers, including radiologists, pathologists, gastroenterologists, oncologists, surgeons, genetic counselors, and others, collaborate to diagnose and treat pancreatic cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers include breast specialists, diagnostic radiologists, gynecologists, and lactation consultants involved in breast health and cancer screening.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers are professionals and institutions involved in diagnosing and treating bone cancer, often with specialized experience.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560663</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Scan">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging scans are used to visualize the pancreas and surrounding tissues for signs of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging scans, including MRI and CT, are used to detect, locate, and assess the extent of primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560663</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Team Meeting">
      <data key="d0">Team Meeting</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A team meeting where healthcare providers discuss the patient’s case, review tests, and plan treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560663</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Signs of Pancreatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Signs of Pancreatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Signs include jaundice, abnormal x-ray findings, and symptoms such as jaundice or pain, though early stages often lack clear signs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560664</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate Cancer Symptoms">
      <data key="d0">Prostate Cancer Symptoms</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms may include blood in urine or semen, difficulty urinating, weight loss, bone pain, and erectile dysfunction, often more apparent when cancer is advanced.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560664</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia">
      <data key="d0">Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) is a common condition that enlarges the prostate but is different from prostate cancer; it can cause similar urinary symptoms.&lt;SEP&gt;Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) is a common condition where the prostate enlarges, causing urinary issues similar to prostate cancer symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560664</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Jaundice">
      <data key="d0">Jaundice</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Jaundice, characterized by yellowing of the skin and eyes, is a potential symptom of pancreatic cancer caused by bile duct obstruction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560664</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Health care providers">
      <data key="d0">Health care providers</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Health care providers include a multidisciplinary team of various specialists involved in diagnosing and treating pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560664</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="High-volume center">
      <data key="d0">High-volume center</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">A high-volume center is a healthcare facility experienced in managing pancreatic cancer, which is recommended for optimal care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560664</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Medical history">
      <data key="d0">Medical history</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical history involves reviewing past and current health information during consultation to aid diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560664</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Treating pancreatic cancer">
      <data key="d0">Treating pancreatic cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Treating pancreatic cancer involves diagnostic testing, staging, and determining suitability for surgery and therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560664</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sign">
      <data key="d0">Sign</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Signs are observable or detectable indicators of disease, such as jaundice or abnormal imaging findings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560664</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Signs and symptoms">
      <data key="d0">Signs and symptoms</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Signs and symptoms help guide suspicion and diagnostic testing for pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560664</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Health Organization Classification (WHO5)">
      <data key="d0">Health Organization Classification (WHO5)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The WHO5 classification system categorizes grades of follicular lymphoma and defines terms for its subtypes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560664</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Lymphoma (FL)">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Lymphoma (FL)</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular lymphoma is a slow-growing lymphoma characterized by abnormal B lymphocytes forming clusters in lymph nodes and other tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560664</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="WHO5">
      <data key="d0">WHO5</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">WHO5 is an organization that provides classification standards for follicular lymphoma and its subtypes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560664</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="International Consensus Classification (ICC)">
      <data key="d0">International Consensus Classification (ICC)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">ICC groups grades 1 and 2 of follicular lymphoma as classic FL and is a classification system used as a standard in this context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560664</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 1">
      <data key="d0">Grade 1</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade 1 is a classification of follicular lymphoma indicating a low-grade, less aggressive form.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560665</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 2">
      <data key="d0">Grade 2</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade 2 is a classification of follicular lymphoma indicating a slightly more advanced but still indolent form.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560665</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 3A">
      <data key="d0">Grade 3A</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade 3A refers to a subtype of follicular lymphoma that may be treated as either classic FL or DLBCL depending on testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560665</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 3B (FL3B)">
      <data key="d0">Grade 3B (FL3B)</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">FL3B, called follicular large B cell lymphoma in WHO5, is a rare form treated as DLBCL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560665</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chromosome Translocation">
      <data key="d0">Chromosome Translocation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A chromosome translocation involves the switching of parts between chromosomes within lymphoma cells, associated with FL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560665</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="BCL2 Protein">
      <data key="d0">BCL2 Protein</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The BCL2 protein is an abnormal protein often overexpressed in FL tumors, associated with cell survival.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560665</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Uncommon FL (uFL)">
      <data key="d0">Uncommon FL (uFL)</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Uncommon FL describes FL with unusual pathological features or negative t(14;18) translocation, treated as classic FL in this context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560665</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pediatric-type FL (PTFL)">
      <data key="d0">Pediatric-type FL (PTFL)</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">PTFL usually occurs in children, typically presenting with a single enlarged lymph node in the head or neck without symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560666</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Transformed FL">
      <data key="d0">Transformed FL</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Transformed FL refers to FL that progresses into a more aggressive lymphoma called DLBCL, either before, during, or after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560666</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma (DLBCL)">
      <data key="d0">Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma (DLBCL)</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">DLBCL is an aggressive type of lymphoma that FL can transform into, affecting various tissues including lymph nodes, spleen, liver, and bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;DLBCL is a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma, with extra-mediastinal disease diagnosed as DLBCL rather than mediastinal gray zone lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560666</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma (NHL)">
      <data key="d0">Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma (NHL)</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">NHL is a cancer originating from lymphocytes, including follicular lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560666</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="t(14;18) Translocation">
      <data key="d0">t(14;18) Translocation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">t(14;18) translocation is a genetic rearrangement commonly found in follicular lymphoma, involving the swapping of chromosome parts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560666</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Semen">
      <data key="d0">Semen</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A fluid composed of liquids from the prostate and seminal vesicles along with sperm from the testicles, released during ejaculation.&lt;SEP&gt;Semen is a fluid composed of secretions from the prostate and seminal vesicles, along with sperm from the testicles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560666</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate Specific Antigen">
      <data key="d0">Prostate Specific Antigen</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A protein produced by the prostate that nourishes and helps transmit semen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560666</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Factors">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Inherited genetic mutations, such as in BRCA2 gene, that increase the risk of prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic factors involve inherited genetic changes that influence the risk of prostate cancer, such as mutations in specific genes like BRCA2.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560666</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Race">
      <data key="d0">Race</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Biological and social factors associated with higher prostate cancer incidence and severity in Black males compared to other racial groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Race influences PSA levels, with higher normal levels observed in Black males compared to white males.&lt;SEP&gt;Race influences prostate cancer risk, with Black males in the United States being more likely to develop, at an earlier age, and have more aggressive forms, compared to white, Hispanic, and Asian males.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560666</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Aggressive Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Aggressive Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer that grows or spreads more rapidly than average or requires stronger, more intense therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Aggressive prostate cancer describes a form that grows or spreads rapidly and may require stronger or more intensive therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560666</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Early-Stage Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Early-Stage Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer confined within the prostate that grows slowly; also known as localized prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Early-stage prostate cancer is confined within the prostate gland and is highly treatable and often curable if diagnosed early.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560666</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced-Stage Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Advanced-Stage Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer that has spread outside the prostate to other parts of the body, including lymph nodes, bones, liver, and lungs; also called metastatic prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Advanced-stage prostate cancer has spread beyond the prostate gland, is usually a lifelong disease, but treatments can slow growth and reduce symptoms.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer that has spread outside the prostate to other areas in the body, often requiring personalized treatment approaches and considered a life-long condition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560666</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Localized Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Localized Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer contained entirely within the prostate, growing slowly without spreading beyond the organ.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560666</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer that has spread beyond the prostate to other organs or tissues in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic prostate cancer is an advanced stage where the cancer has spread outside the prostate to other parts of the body, such as lymph nodes, bones, liver, or lungs.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic prostate cancer is an advanced stage where the cancer spreads beyond the prostate to other parts of the body like lymph nodes, bones, liver, or lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560666</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate Gland">
      <data key="d0">Prostate Gland</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The prostate gland is a male reproductive organ where prostate cancer develops.&lt;SEP&gt;The prostate is an organ in the male reproductive system that produces fluids to nourish and help transmit sperm.&lt;SEP&gt;The prostate gland is a male reproductive organ involved in secreting fluids that are a part of semen, and is the origin site for prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The prostate gland is a male reproductive organ involved in the production of seminal fluid and is the primary site affected in prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560667</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate-specific Antigen (PSA)">
      <data key="d0">Prostate-specific Antigen (PSA)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">PSA is a protein produced inside the prostate gland, used as a marker in blood tests to detect prostate cancer or prostate health issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560667</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Active Surveillance">
      <data key="d0">Active Surveillance</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Active surveillance is a management strategy for early-stage prostate cancer involving regular tests to monitor cancer progression without immediate treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A management approach involving close monitoring of very-low-risk thyroid cancers without immediate surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Active surveillance involves monitoring small cancers without immediate surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560667</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Hormone Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormone therapy is a treatment option for prostate cancer that aims to reduce or block the body's natural hormones to slow cancer growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone therapy is used to manipulate hormone levels, particularly testosterone, to control prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone therapy uses hormones or hormonal agents to treat breast cancer, possibly involving high doses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560667</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bones">
      <data key="d0">Bones</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Bones are common sites where prostate cancer metastasizes.&lt;SEP&gt;Bones, including the skull and vertebrae, shield the brain and spinal cord in the CNS.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560667</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Regional Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Regional Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Regional prostate cancer refers to cancer that has spread to nearby regions such as the pelvis but not beyond.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560667</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate-specific Antigen">
      <data key="d0">Prostate-specific Antigen</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">PSA is a protein made inside the prostate gland, with higher levels potentially indicating prostate cancer or other prostate issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560667</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Urine Test">
      <data key="d0">Urine Test</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A diagnostic procedure involving analyzing urine samples for biomarkers related to prostate cancer detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560668</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="PSA Level">
      <data key="d0">PSA Level</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The PSA level measures the amount of prostate-specific antigen in the blood, which can indicate prostate health issues or cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560668</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood and Urine Tests">
      <data key="d0">Blood and Urine Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Procedures that analyze blood and urine samples to detect biomarkers indicating prostate cancer or other prostate abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560668</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="PSMA/PET">
      <data key="d0">PSMA/PET</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A specialized PET imaging technique targeting prostate-specific membrane antigen (PSMA) on prostate cancer cells to detect metastasis and recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560668</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Prostate Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A diagnostic procedure involving tissue sampling from the prostate to confirm or rule out cancer, often performed after abnormal imaging or tests.&lt;SEP&gt;A prostate biopsy is a procedure in which tissue from the prostate is sampled, often guided by ultrasound or MRI-TRUS fusion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560668</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bloodstream">
      <data key="d0">Bloodstream</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The circulatory system component responsible for transporting contrast agents, radioactive tracers, and other substances used in imaging procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560668</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Radioactive Tracer">
      <data key="d0">Radioactive Tracer</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A radioactive substance injected during PET or bone scans to highlight cancerous cells or areas of metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;A radioactive tracer is injected into the bloodstream and accumulates in bones or areas of damage to produce images for detecting abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Radioactive tracers such as sodium fluoride or FDG (fluorodeoxyglucose) are used in PET-CT scans to visualize metabolic activity and infections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560668</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Prostate cancer is a common and potentially life-threatening malignancy that originates in the prostate gland, a key component of the male reproductive system. It is characterized by the uncontrolled growth of abnormal prostate cells, which can remain localized within the gland or metastasize to other parts of the body, such as bones or lymph nodes. The disease often develops silently in its early stages, presenting few or no symptoms, making early detection through screening vital.

Risk factors for prostate cancer include advancing age (typically 65 years and older), a family history of the disease, genetic mutations, and race, with higher prevalence observed among Black males. Lifestyle factors may also contribute to the risk profile. Diagnostic procedures for detecting prostate cancer involve a combination of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) testing, digital rectal examinations (DRE), imaging scans, and tissue biopsies. Tumor grading, often using Gleason scores, and staging with tools like the TNM system, help determine the extent and severity of the disease.

Prostate cancer can be diagnosed through genetic testing and tumor profiling, which assist in understanding the nature of the tumor and guiding treatment strategies. Treatment options vary depending on the stage and aggressiveness of the cancer and may include surgical removal of the prostate, hormone therapy with drugs such as LHRH analogs, radiotherapy, chemotherapy, and active surveillance approaches. The disease's potential for progression to advanced stages underscores the importance of early detection and ongoing monitoring.

Overall, prostate cancer is a disease with a broad spectrum, from indolent forms that require minimal intervention to aggressive tumors necessitating comprehensive treatment. Advances in diagnostic techniques and therapies have improved the management of the disease, enabling many patients to live longer and healthier lives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560669</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Probe">
      <data key="d0">Probe</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A probe is a medical instrument used to visualize or access internal organs during procedures like biopsies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560669</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Core Sample">
      <data key="d0">Core Sample</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A core sample is a small piece of tissue, usually 1 to 20 millimeters long, collected during biopsy to test for cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A small tissue sample taken during a prostate biopsy for pathological examination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560669</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Inherited Mutation">
      <data key="d0">Inherited Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Inherited mutations are genetic changes passed from parents to offspring, present in all cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560669</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Germline Testing">
      <data key="d0">Germline Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Germline testing is a genetic test performed on blood, urine, or saliva to detect inherited mutations across all cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Germline testing checks for inherited genetic mutations to assess cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560669</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Molecular Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d0">Molecular Biomarker Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">This genetic test evaluates specific genetic markers that may indicate cancer risk or presence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560669</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA1">
      <data key="d0">BRCA1</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">A gene involved in DNA repair; mutations in BRCA1 increase the risk of ovarian and breast cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA1 is a gene mutation that can influence treatment options for ovarian cancer, especially in targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA1 is a gene associated with increased risk of certain cancers, including prostate cancer, when mutated.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA1 is a gene associated with increased risk of breast, ovarian, pancreatic, and skin cancers when mutated in the germline.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA1 is a gene mutation that makes PARP inhibitors effective against breast cancers with the mutation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560669</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA2">
      <data key="d0">BRCA2</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">A gene involved in DNA repair; mutations in BRCA2 are linked to higher risk of ovarian and breast cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA2 is a gene mutation that helps guide treatment decisions in ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA2 is a gene linked to hereditary prostate and other cancers when it carries mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA2 is a gene associated with increased risk of breast, ovarian, pancreatic, and skin cancers when mutated in the germline.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA2 is a gene mutation that makes PARP inhibitors effective against breast cancers with the mutation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560669</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="ATM">
      <data key="d0">ATM</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">ATM is a gene involved in DNA repair, with mutations increasing cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;ATM is a gene involved in DNA repair; germline mutations may increase cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560670</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CHEK2">
      <data key="d0">CHEK2</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">CHEK2 is a gene that, when mutated, can increase susceptibility to various types of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;CHEK2 is a gene linked to higher risk of multiple cancers when mutated germlinely.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560670</data>
