      <data key="d5">1764560684</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic breast cancer (MBC) is a stage of breast cancer that has spread beyond the breast and lymph nodes into other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic breast cancer (MBC) is when cancer has spread outside the breast and lymph nodes, possibly without involvement of axillary lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic breast cancer (Stage 4) has spread to distant organs or tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560684</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Her2 Status">
      <data key="d0">Her2 Status</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Her2 status refers to the level of human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 expression in breast cancer, important for treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560684</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="HER2 Status">
      <data key="d0">HER2 Status</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2 status indicates whether breast cancer cells overexpress the HER2 protein, guiding targeted therapy decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2 status testing examines the human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 expression in cancer cells, relevant for targeted therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2 status testing evaluates HER2 protein expression, guiding targeted therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2 status indicates the overexpression or amplification of the HER2 gene in breast cancer, affecting treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560684</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Testing">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility testing assesses reproductive health and is relevant for all genders diagnosed with breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560684</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="The Breast">
      <data key="d0">The Breast</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The breast is an organ on the chest containing structures like milk ducts, lobules, fat, nerves, and vessels, involved in breastfeeding.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560684</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Primary Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary tumor is the initial mass of cancer cells formed by uncontrolled growth at the site of origin.&lt;SEP&gt;A mass called a primary tumor forms over time from cancer cells originating in the breast.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560684</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Invasive Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Invasive Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Invasive cancer refers to cancer cells invading into surrounding tissues beyond their point of origin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560684</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Spread">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Spread</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer spread refers to the process where cancer cells invade surrounding tissues or metastasize to distant organs via lymph or blood.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer spread refers to the dissemination of cancer cells from the primary tumor to other parts of the body, often via lymph nodes or blood.&lt;SEP&gt;Refers to the dissemination of cancer cells from the primary site to surrounding tissues and lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560684</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Human Cells">
      <data key="d0">Human Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Human cells contain 23 pairs of chromosomes, totaling 46 chromosomes, which carry genetic information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560684</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Next-generation Sequencing">
      <data key="d0">Next-generation Sequencing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Next-generation sequencing (NGS) determines DNA sequence variations, mutations, and gene alterations in leukemia cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Next-generation sequencing (NGS) is a DNA analysis method that provides detailed information about gene sequences, identifying mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560684</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="PCR">
      <data key="d0">PCR</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) is used to find specific genetic mutations or changes in leukemia cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) detects known genetic mutations in leukemia cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) is a technique to amplify millions or billions of copies of DNA or RNA for analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) amplifies DNA sequences, enabling detection of genetic abnormalities in small samples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560685</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="PCR Products">
      <data key="d0">PCR Products</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">PCR products are the amplified DNA or RNA copies used for further testing or sequencing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560685</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Real-time PCR">
      <data key="d0">Real-time PCR</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Real-time PCR or reverse transcriptase (RT) PCR is used to detect gene rearrangements like PML::RARA, aiding diagnosis and therapy monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560685</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Oral Contrast">
      <data key="d0">Oral Contrast</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Oral contrast is ingested and passes through the intestines, not absorbed, aiding in imaging the digestive tract.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560685</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="IV Contrast">
      <data key="d0">IV Contrast</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">IV contrast is injected into a vein, exiting the body through urine shortly after imaging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560685</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Electrocardiogram">
      <data key="d0">Electrocardiogram</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An electrocardiogram (ECG or EKG) measures electrical activity in the heart to assess rhythm and rate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560685</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Prolonged QTc">
      <data key="d0">Prolonged QTc</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A prolonged QTc is a lengthened heart muscle recharge time, which can cause dangerous rhythms, often related to treatment effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560685</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ductal">
      <data key="d0">Ductal</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Ductal breast cancer refers to cancer originating in the milk ducts, which are involved in transporting milk from lobules to the nipple.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560685</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lobular">
      <data key="d0">Lobular</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Lobular breast cancer originates in the lobules, the structures that produce breast milk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560686</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone Receptors">
      <data key="d0">Hormone Receptors</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormone receptor testing involves analyzing tissue for estrogen and progesterone receptor presence to guide treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Testing for estrogen and progesterone receptors helps determine breast cancer type and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone receptors such as estrogen receptor (ER) and progesterone receptor (PR) are proteins that may influence the growth of breast cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560686</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ductal Carcinoma in Situ (DCIS)">
      <data key="d0">Ductal Carcinoma in Situ (DCIS)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A noninvasive breast cancer found in cells lining the milk ducts, stage 0, treated to prevent invasive breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560686</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone Receptor Status">
      <data key="d0">Hormone Receptor Status</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing to determine the presence of hormone receptors like estrogen in breast cancer cells, influencing treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;The classification indicating whether breast cancer cells have hormone receptors, guiding treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560686</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mammogram">
      <data key="d0">Mammogram</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">X-ray imaging of the breast used for screening or diagnostic purposes to detect abnormalities or signs of breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A mammogram is an imaging test that uses low-dose x-rays to examine breast tissue, available as 2D or 3D (tomosynthesis).&lt;SEP&gt;A diagnostic imaging test that uses X-rays to examine the breast tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;A mammogram is an imaging technique using x-rays to produce detailed pictures of breast tissue for tumor detection and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560686</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Screening Mammogram">
      <data key="d0">Screening Mammogram</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A mammogram performed regularly when no symptoms are present to detect early signs of breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A routine mammogram performed without symptoms, using low-dose x-rays for early detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560686</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Mammogram">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Mammogram</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A mammogram used to examine specific symptoms, abnormal findings, or changes in breast shape or size.&lt;SEP&gt;A targeted mammogram for patients with symptoms like lumps or changes, often with additional views.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560686</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Stages">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Stages</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Esophageal cancer is classified into stages based on its spread: early-stage, locoregional or locally advanced, and metastatic.&lt;SEP&gt;Classification of breast cancer based on its size, spread, and invasion, guiding treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Stages 0 through IV represent progression levels of colon cancer, from carcinoma in situ to widespread metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Different levels of cancer progression, from early (Stage 1A) to metastatic (Stage 4B), with specific criteria for each stage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560686</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fallopian Tubes">
      <data key="d0">Fallopian Tubes</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Fallopian tubes are long, thin tubes that surround the ovaries and transport eggs to the uterus.&lt;SEP&gt;Connect the ovaries to the upper part of the uterus, involved in reproductive processes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560687</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vagina">
      <data key="d0">Vagina</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Vagina is part of the female reproductive system, serving as the canal leading from the cervix to the external body.&lt;SEP&gt;The vagina is examined using ultrasound to assess the size, shape, and location of the cervix.&lt;SEP&gt;The vagina is a part of the female reproductive anatomy that MRI can show in detail.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560687</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Epithelial Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Epithelial Ovarian Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Most common type of ovarian cancer, originating in the outer surface of the ovaries called the epithelium.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560687</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="High-Grade Serous Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">High-Grade Serous Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">A common and aggressive form of epithelial ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560687</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="High-Grade Endometrioid Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">High-Grade Endometrioid Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">A frequently occurring aggressive ovarian cancer subtype.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560687</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Less Common Ovarian Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Less Common Ovarian Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Includes various less frequent types such as low-grade serous, low-grade endometrioid, carcinosarcoma, clear cell, and mucinous carcinomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560687</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Malignant Sex-Cord Stromal Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Malignant Sex-Cord Stromal Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Type of non-epithelial ovarian cancer originating in ovarian stromal tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560687</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Malignant Germ Cell Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Malignant Germ Cell Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Type of non-epithelial ovarian cancer originating in reproductive (egg) cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560687</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Peritoneal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Primary Peritoneal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer forming in the tissue lining the abdominal wall and covering abdominal organs, similar in treatment to ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560688</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gynecologic Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Gynecologic Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Specialist who performs surgery and chemotherapy for gynecologic cancers, including ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560688</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Recommendations">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Recommendations</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Guidelines provided for managing common and rare ovarian cancers, including personalized treatment plans and clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560688</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="DCIS Test">
      <data key="d0">DCIS Test</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for Ductal Carcinoma In Situ (DCIS) involves specific assessments for early breast cancer detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560688</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="General Health Tests">
      <data key="d0">General Health Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">General health tests include physical exams, imaging, biopsies, and estrogen receptor status testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560688</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Estrogen Receptor Status">
      <data key="d0">Estrogen Receptor Status</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for estrogen receptor status helps determine hormone responsiveness of breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560688</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Cancer Stages">
      <data key="d0">Breast Cancer Stages</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stages describe the extent and progression of breast cancer, guiding treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560688</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Transgender Individuals">
      <data key="d0">Transgender Individuals</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Transgender individuals should consult healthcare providers for screening guidance due to lack of specific evidence-based recommendations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560688</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Assigned Male at Birth">
      <data key="d0">Assigned Male at Birth</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Individuals assigned male at birth generally do not undergo routine mammograms but should seek medical advice if symptoms appear.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560688</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Guidelines">
      <data key="d0">Guidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Guidelines such as NCCN inform breast cancer screening, diagnosis, and treatment protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560688</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Health Record">
      <data key="d0">Health Record</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A health record includes patients' medical history, treatment details, and test results to aid ongoing care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560688</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Health Care Provider">
      <data key="d0">Health Care Provider</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Health care providers are professionals involved in health checkups and breast cancer screening, including specialists, radiologists, technologists, and practitioners.&lt;SEP&gt;A health care provider is a medical professional or organization involved in diagnosing, treating, and supporting patients with health concerns, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A health care provider may use a mirror or fiberoptic scope to examine the oropharynx and related areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560688</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Specialist">
      <data key="d0">Breast Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A healthcare professional who is an expert in breast health and disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560688</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Diagnostic Radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Breast Diagnostic Radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A specialist who interprets imaging tests such as mammograms, MRIs, and ultrasound and performs needle biopsies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560688</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gynecologist">
      <data key="d0">Gynecologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A doctor who diagnoses and treats diseases of female reproductive organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560688</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lactation Consultant">
      <data key="d0">Lactation Consultant</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A specialist in breastfeeding support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
