    <node id="Myths and Misconceptions">
      <data key="d0">Myths and Misconceptions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Common but inaccurate beliefs about clinical trials and cancer treatments, often leading to misunderstandings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560709</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Placebos">
      <data key="d0">Placebos</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Inactive medications rarely used alone in cancer trials; placebo use is limited and controlled.&lt;SEP&gt;Placebos are inactive substances that may be used in clinical trials as control treatments, rarely used alone in cancer studies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560709</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gold Futures">
      <data key="d0">Gold Futures</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Gold futures are standardized contracts traded on markets, reflecting a 1.5% rise during the period described.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560709</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Supply Constraints">
      <data key="d0">Supply Constraints</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Supply constraints refer to limitations in the availability of crude oil, contributing to rising prices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560709</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Primary CNS Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary central nervous system (CNS) lymphoma is a rare and aggressive form of cancer that originates within the central nervous system, which includes the brain, spinal cord, cerebrospinal fluid, and sometimes the eyes. It is characterized by the presence of malignant lymphocytes within these tissues. As a subtype of lymphoma, primary CNS lymphoma develops deep within brain tissue, making surgical removal challenging and often risky, thus requiring diagnosis through tissue biopsy and testing.

This cancer primarily affects the lymphatic tissue in various parts of the central nervous system, leading to symptoms associated with neurological impairment. Because of its unique location and nature, primary CNS lymphoma necessitates specific treatment approaches. Common therapies include chemotherapy, radiation therapy, and targeted therapies, with treatment plans tailored to eradicate cancer cells and prevent recurrence.

Overall, primary CNS lymphoma is distinguished by its origin within the central nervous system and its aggressive clinical course, demanding precise diagnostic confirmation and specialized treatment strategies to effectively manage the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560710</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Approach">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Approach</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment approach refers to the method used in clinical management, including standard treatments and experimental clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560710</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Investigational Drug">
      <data key="d0">Investigational Drug</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">An investigational drug is a new medication tested in clinical trials to determine safety, dosing, and effectiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560710</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Long-term Side Effects">
      <data key="d0">Long-term Side Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Long-term side effects of systemic therapy include other cancers, heart disease, and infertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560710</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Memory Problems">
      <data key="d0">Memory Problems</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Memory problems refer to difficulties with recalling or retaining information, often associated with neurological issues and can involve slowed thinking and trouble speaking.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560710</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Eyes">
      <data key="d0">Eyes</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The eyes can be targeted by radiation therapy to treat primary CNS lymphoma, sometimes involving whole eye treatment at a lower dose.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560710</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cataracts">
      <data key="d0">Cataracts</data>
      <data key="d1">medicalcondition</data>
      <data key="d2">Cataracts are clouding of the lens in the eye, which can be caused by radiation therapy and treatable with routine surgery by an eye surgeon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560710</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood-Forming Stem Cells">
      <data key="d0">Blood-Forming Stem Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood-forming stem cells are responsible for producing blood cells in the bone marrow, crucial in stem cell transplant procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood-forming stem cells, also called hematopoietic stem cells, are cells that develop into different blood cells and are generated in the bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560710</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rituximab (Rituxan)">
      <data key="d0">Rituximab (Rituxan)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Rituximab, marketed as Rituxan, is a monoclonal antibody used in immunotherapy to treat primary CNS lymphoma by targeting CD20 proteins on cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560711</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stem Cell">
      <data key="d0">Stem Cell</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Stem cells are unspecialized cells capable of developing into different cell types; blood-forming stem cells are a subset involved in blood production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560711</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="High-Dose Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">High-Dose Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">High-dose chemotherapy is an intensive treatment to kill lymphoma and cancer cells, followed by stem cell rescue to recover blood cell production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560711</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Clotting Cells">
      <data key="d0">Blood Clotting Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood clotting cells, or platelets, help in blood clot formation and are affected by certain cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560711</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stem Cells">
      <data key="d0">Stem Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Stem cells are specialized cells that can develop into different cell types. In the context of stem cell rescue, they are harvested, concentrated, frozen, and later transfused to help grow new blood-forming cells in the bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560711</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Multidisciplinary Care">
      <data key="d0">Multidisciplinary Care</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Multidisciplinary care involves a team of various healthcare providers working together to plan and manage cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560711</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rituximab">
      <data key="d0">Rituximab</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Rituximab is a main immunotherapy drug used to treat primary CNS lymphoma by attacking specific parts of cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Rituximab, marketed as Rituxan, is an immunotherapy drug used in combination with high-dose methotrexate to treat primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Rituximab might be added to chemotherapy (chemoimmunotherapy) if tumor cells express specific markers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560711</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Whole-brain Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Whole-brain Radiation Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Whole-brain radiation therapy (WBRT) is a common radiation technique used for primary CNS lymphoma to target cancer in the brain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560711</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Transfusion">
      <data key="d0">Blood Transfusion</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood transfusion involves the transfer of blood stem cells into a patient to regenerate healthy blood cells after chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560712</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Connective Tissue">
      <data key="d0">Connective Tissue</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Connective tissue in tumors indicates the presence of fibrous or connective tissue, associated with high-risk or very high-risk cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560712</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphovascular Invasion">
      <data key="d0">Lymphovascular Invasion</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphovascular invasion involves cancer cells in blood or lymph vessels outside the main tumor, increasing the risk of metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560712</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Corticosteroid">
      <data key="d0">Corticosteroid</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Corticosteroids are long-term medications that can weaken the immune system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560712</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="AIDS">
      <data key="d0">AIDS</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">AIDS (Acquired Immunodeficiency Syndrome) is a condition caused by HIV, compromising the immune system and increasing cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560712</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Standard Surgical Excision">
      <data key="d0">Standard Surgical Excision</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard surgical excision involves cutting out the tumor with a margin of healthy tissue, suitable for low-risk and some high-risk cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560712</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="PDEMA">
      <data key="d0">PDEMA</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">PDEMA (Progressive Destructive Electrochemical Melting and Ablation) is an alternative surgical technique for CSCC.&lt;SEP&gt;PDEMA (Primary Disease Excision with Margin Assessment) is a surgical approach used for high-risk or very high-risk CSCC to ensure complete removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560712</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Risk of Spreading">
      <data key="d0">Risk of Spreading</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The risk of cancer spreading depends on factors like tumor depth, connective tissue presence, and invasion of lymph or blood vessels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560712</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Counselor">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Counselor</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A treatment counselor is a healthcare professional who can advise patients on appropriate treatment options and next steps.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560712</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Quit Support">
      <data key="d0">Quit Support</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Support methods like counseling and medications can help individuals stop smoking or vaping.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560712</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="High-risk or Very High-risk CSCC">
      <data key="d0">High-risk or Very High-risk CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">High-risk and very high-risk cutaneous squamous cell carcinomas are more aggressive forms requiring extensive treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560712</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Support Websites">
      <data key="d0">Support Websites</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Online resources like SmokeFree.gov and CDC.gov/tobacco provide support and counseling to help quit smoking or vaping.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560712</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Induction Phase">
      <data key="d0">Induction Phase</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The induction phase is the initial step of treatment focused on destroying lymphoma cells, often involving chemotherapy such as high-dose methotrexate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560712</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Consolidation Phase">
      <data key="d0">Consolidation Phase</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The consolidation phase follows induction therapy, aiming to eliminate remaining cancer cells and prevent lymphoma recurrence, potentially involving additional chemotherapy or therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560712</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="High-Dose Methotrexate">
      <data key="d0">High-Dose Methotrexate</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">High-dose methotrexate is the most effective chemotherapy drug for primary CNS lymphoma, working by blocking folate usage in lymphoma cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560712</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood-Brain Barrier">
      <data key="d0">Blood-Brain Barrier</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The blood-brain barrier is a protective layer around the brain that restricts many substances, including some chemotherapy drugs like methotrexate, from entering the brain tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560712</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tunica Adventitia">
      <data key="d0">Tunica Adventitia</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The tunica adventitia is the outermost layer of the esophagus, made of connective tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560713</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Upper Esophagus">
      <data key="d0">Upper Esophagus</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The upper part of the esophagus is made of striated muscle and is under voluntary control.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560713</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Middle Esophagus">
      <data key="d0">Middle Esophagus</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The middle part of the esophagus contains a mixture of striated and smooth muscle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560713</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lower Esophagus">
      <data key="d0">Lower Esophagus</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The lower part of the esophagus consists only of smooth muscle and is involuntary.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560713</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Esophageal Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Esophageal Squamous Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of esophageal cancer that begins in the thin, flat cells in the inner lining of the esophagus, common in upper and middle esophagus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560713</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Esophageal Adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Esophageal Adenocarcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of esophageal cancer that begins in mucus-producing cells and is often found in the lower esophagus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560713</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Barrett Esophagus">
      <data key="d0">Barrett Esophagus</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Barrett esophagus involves abnormal changes in the squamous cells lining the lower esophagus due to chronic reflux, increasing the risk of adenocarcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560713</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Early-Stage Esophageal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Early-Stage Esophageal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer confined within the esophagus, often small and without lymph node involvement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560713</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Locoregional or Locally Advanced Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Locoregional or Locally Advanced Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer that has invaded the esophageal wall and possibly spread to nearby lymph nodes or organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560714</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Endoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Endoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Endoscopy procedures allow direct visualization and biopsy of esophageal tissue for diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560714</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Distant Metastases">
      <data key="d0">Distant Metastases</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Distant metastases refer to spread of esophageal cancer to organs like the liver, lung, brain, or distant lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560714</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Esophageal wall">
      <data key="d0">Esophageal wall</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The esophageal wall is composed of four main layers: mucosa, submucosa, muscularis propria, and tunica adventitia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560714</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Siewert types">
      <data key="d0">Siewert types</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Siewert types are used for cancer staging to describe the tumor location relative to the esophagus and stomach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560714</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Distant lymph nodes">
      <data key="d0">Distant lymph nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Distant lymph nodes are lymph nodes located far from the esophagus, such as in the neck, associated with metastatic esophageal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560714</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Castle">
      <data key="d0">Castle</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The name 'Castle' appears in context of references, but as no context provided, it is potentially a relevant proper noun to recognize as a person.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560714</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="North America">
      <data key="d0">North America</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">North America is a continent where esophageal adenocarcinoma is more prevalent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560714</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Western Europe">
      <data key="d0">Western Europe</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Western Europe is a region where esophageal adenocarcinoma is common.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560714</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Eastern Europe">
      <data key="d0">Eastern Europe</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Eastern Europe is a region where squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus is more common.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560714</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Asia">
      <data key="d0">Asia</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Asia is a continent with a higher prevalence of squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560714</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Africa">
      <data key="d0">Africa</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Africa is a region where squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus occurs more frequently.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560714</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphatic system">
      <data key="d0">Lymphatic system</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">The lymphatic system is involved in the spread of esophageal cancer to lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560714</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Colon Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Colon cancer often originates from polyps and can be prevented by removing polyps through procedures like polypectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Colon cancer is a malignant tumor originating in the lining of the colon, often developing from polyps, and can spread if untreated.&lt;SEP&gt;A malignant tumor originating in the colon, staged using the TNM system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560714</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Colorectal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Colorectal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Colorectal cancer refers to malignant growths in the colon or rectum, often originating from polyps.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560714</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="FAP (Familial Adenomatous Polyposis)">
      <data key="d0">FAP (Familial Adenomatous Polyposis)</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">FAP is an inherited disorder causing numerous polyps in the colon and rectum, which can become malignant over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560714</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CEA Test">
      <data key="d0">CEA Test</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) blood test monitors levels that may be elevated in colon cancer, especially with metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560715</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="dMMR (Mismatch Repair Deficiency)">
      <data key="d0">dMMR (Mismatch Repair Deficiency)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">dMMR is a biomarker indicating genetic features of some colon cancers, guiding testing and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560715</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="MSI-H (Microsatellite Instability-High)">
      <data key="d0">MSI-H (Microsatellite Instability-High)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">MSI-H indicates the presence of mismatch repair deficiency in tumors, used as a biomarker to guide therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560715</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="MSS (Microsatellite Stable)">
      <data key="d0">MSS (Microsatellite Stable)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">MSS indicates the absence of mismatch repair deficiency in tumors, relevant for treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560715</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular lymphoma (FL) is a slow-growing non-Hodgkin lymphoma originating from B lymphocytes, characterized by clusters of abnormal B cells in lymph nodes and other tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560715</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Germinal Centers">
      <data key="d0">Germinal Centers</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Germinal centers are temporary structures in lymph nodes formed during immune responses, where B cells proliferate and can develop into follicular lymphoma if growth is uncontrolled.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560715</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Germinal Center Zones">
      <data key="d0">Germinal Center Zones</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The germinal centers have two zones: light zone with centrocytes and dark zone with centroblasts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560715</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Centrocytes">
      <data key="d0">Centrocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">Centrocytes are smaller B cells found in the light zone of germinal centers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560715</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Centroblasts">
      <data key="d0">Centroblasts</data>
      <data key="d1">cell</data>
      <data key="d2">Centroblasts are larger B cells located in the dark zone of germinal centers, involved in proliferation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560716</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="FL Grades">
      <data key="d0">FL Grades</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular lymphoma has grades 1, 2, 3A, and 3B, determined by cell types and proportions, influencing treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560716</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="World Health Organization">
      <data key="d0">World Health Organization</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The WHO classification (2022) categorizes FL grades 1, 2, and 3A as classic FL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560716</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="International Consensus Classification">
      <data key="d0">International Consensus Classification</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">ICC categorizes grades 1 and 2 as classic FL, with systems influencing treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560716</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphatic Vessel">
      <data key="d0">Lymphatic Vessel</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphatic vessels are thin tubes that carry lymph fluid and white blood cells throughout the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560716</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Tissue">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Tissue</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph tissue includes lymph vessels, lymph nodes, spleen, and thymus, involved in immune functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560716</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Extranodal Disease">
      <data key="d0">Extranodal Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Extranodal disease describes tumor formation in parts of the body outside lymph nodes, such as bone marrow, in FL.&lt;SEP&gt;Extranodal disease refers to lymphoma involving tissues outside lymph nodes and is more common in older persons.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560716</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dismission">
      <data key="d0">Dismission</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Dismission refers to the act of being discharged or terminated from a position or role, experienced by the narrator for over a year for various reasons.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560716</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Self-Care">
      <data key="d0">Self-Care</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Self-care involves taking actions to maintain and improve one's health and well-being, emphasized as important before any diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560716</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="ctDNA">
      <data key="d0">ctDNA</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) testing, also called liquid biopsy, detects tumor DNA in blood to assess microscopic disease and cancer recurrence risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560716</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair Testing">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Mismatch repair testing analyzes tumor tissue for deficiency (dMMR) or high microsatellite instability (MSI-H) to inform prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560716</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Familial Adenomatous Polyposis">
      <data key="d0">Familial Adenomatous Polyposis</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Familial adenomatous polyposis is a hereditary disorder characterized by the development of numerous polyps in the colon, increasing cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560716</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Colectomy">
      <data key="d0">Colectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Colectomy is a surgery that removes the part of the colon containing cancer, often followed by anastomosis or colostomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560716</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bowel Blockage">
      <data key="d0">Bowel Blockage</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Bowel blockage is a condition where a tumor or other factors block stool passage, sometimes requiring surgical intervention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560716</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mesh Metal Tube">
      <data key="d0">Mesh Metal Tube</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A stent is a mesh metal tube that can be placed to keep the colon open in cases of bowel blockage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560716</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="American Joint Committee">
      <data key="d0">American Joint Committee</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The American Joint Committee provides cancer staging and classification standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560716</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor, Node, Metastasis System">
      <data key="d0">Tumor, Node, Metastasis System</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The TNM system used to stage colon cancer based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560717</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Colectomy">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Colectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical procedure to remove part of the colon containing cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560717</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Deposits">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Deposits</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Small tumors in the fat around colon, indicating spread in stage 3.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560717</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Surveillance Follow-up Testing">
      <data key="d0">Surveillance Follow-up Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing conducted after successful cancer treatment to detect early signs of cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560717</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1 Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1 Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Early stage of cancer where tumors are small and have limited spread, with recommended follow-up procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560717</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2 and 3 Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2 and 3 Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced stages of colon cancer requiring comprehensive surveillance strategies including physical exams, blood tests, and imaging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560717</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinoembryonic Antigen (CEA)">
      <data key="d0">Carcinoembryonic Antigen (CEA)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A blood marker used in surveillance for stages 2 and 3 colon cancer to monitor disease recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560717</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CT Scans">
      <data key="d0">CT Scans</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging procedure utilized in surveillance of stages 2 and 3 colon cancer to detect spread or recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560717</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nasopharynx">
      <data key="d0">Nasopharynx</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The upper part of the pharynx that connects the back of the nose to the oropharynx and is the primary site affected by nasopharyngeal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The nasopharynx connects the back of the nose (nasal cavity) to the back of the mouth (pharynx) and is affected by NPC.&lt;SEP&gt;The nasopharynx is the upper part of the throat behind the nose where carcinoma can originate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560718</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">A cancer affecting the nasopharynx, most common type being squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560718</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Oropharyngeal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Oropharyngeal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer affecting the middle part of the throat near the mouth, also a type of head and neck cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560718</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Health Issues">
      <data key="d0">Health Issues</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Health issues include illnesses, injuries, and medical conditions experienced over a lifetime.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560718</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Medical or Health History">
      <data key="d0">Medical or Health History</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical or health history is a record documenting past health conditions, treatments, and symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560718</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NPC">
      <data key="d0">NPC</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">NPC is a type of cancer affecting the nasopharynx, which is a malignant disease involving the mucosal lining of the nasopharynx.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560718</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC)">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">SCC is the most common type of nasopharyngeal cancer, forming in the flat, thin cells of the mucus membranes of the nasopharynx.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560718</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chest CT with Contrast">
      <data key="d0">Chest CT with Contrast</data>
      <data key="d1">imaging</data>
      <data key="d2">Chest CT with contrast is an imaging procedure used to examine the lungs and chest area, often performed to assess metastasis in NPC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560718</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV) DNA Testing">
      <data key="d0">Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV) DNA Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">EBV DNA testing measures the levels of Epstein-Barr Virus DNA in the blood, which may be associated with NPC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560718</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hearing Test (Audiogram)">
      <data key="d0">Hearing Test (Audiogram)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A hearing test or audiogram is conducted to evaluate hearing function, which can be affected by head and neck cancers or their treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560718</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Eye Evaluation">
      <data key="d0">Eye Evaluation</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Eye evaluation assesses visual health and function, as head and neck cancers can impact ocular structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560718</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Endocrine Evaluation">
      <data key="d0">Endocrine Evaluation</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Endocrine evaluation assesses hormone-related functions, which may be needed due to tumor location or treatment effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560718</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dental Work">
      <data key="d0">Dental Work</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Dental work refers to procedures performed on teeth, gums, or oral tissues, which may be recommended before cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560718</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stress">
      <data key="d0">Stress</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stress involves mental, physical, social, or spiritual strain caused by cancer diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560718</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Feelings">
      <data key="d0">Feelings</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Feelings related to cancer include sadness, fear, helplessness, worry, anger, and guilt.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560718</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Depression">
      <data key="d0">Depression</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Depression is a mental health condition that may occur in cancer patients experiencing distress.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560718</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Anxiety">
      <data key="d0">Anxiety</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Anxiety involves feelings of worry or unease that can affect cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560719</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sleep Issues">
      <data key="d0">Sleep Issues</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Sleep issues are common in individuals experiencing distress related to cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560719</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nasopharyngeal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Nasopharyngeal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Nasopharyngeal cancer is a type of head and neck cancer that can influence the eyes.&lt;SEP&gt;Nasopharyngeal cancer (NPC) is a type of cancer originating in the nasopharynx, with stages based on tumor size, lymph node spread, and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Nasopharyngeal cancer is a type of head and neck cancer commonly associated with Epstein-Barr virus infection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560719</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Endocrine Tests">
      <data key="d0">Endocrine Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood tests used to evaluate hormone levels, such as thyroid and pituitary glands, before and after head and neck radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560719</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Glands">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Glands</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The thyroid glands are endocrine glands in the head and neck that regulate hormones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560719</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pituitary Glands">
      <data key="d0">Pituitary Glands</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The pituitary glands are endocrine glands located in the head involved in hormone production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560719</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hearing Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Hearing Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Hearing assessments determine the presence, type, and severity of hearing loss.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560719</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Dietary Guidance">
      <data key="d0">Dietary Guidance</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Recommendations on foods and fluids to maintain adequate nutrition for patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560719</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Speech and Swallowing Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Speech and Swallowing Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Evaluation by speech or swallowing therapists of a patient's ability to speak and swallow before and after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560719</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Speech Therapist">
      <data key="d0">Speech Therapist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A therapist who evaluates and helps improve speech and swallowing functions in cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560719</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Swallowing">
      <data key="d0">Swallowing</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Swallowing ability is assessed to determine functionality before and after cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560719</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Reproductive Options">
      <data key="d0">Reproductive Options</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Options to preserve fertility, including consulting specialists and using specific procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560719</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adolescents and Young Adults">
      <data key="d0">Adolescents and Young Adults</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to age groups for whom fertility preservation resources are available.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560719</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="IUD">
      <data key="d0">IUD</data>
      <data key="d1">device</data>
      <data key="d2">Intrauterine device used as a form of birth control to prevent pregnancy during treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560719</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Contraceptive Sponge">
      <data key="d0">Contraceptive Sponge</data>
      <data key="d1">device</data>
      <data key="d2">A barrier method of contraception that covers the cervix.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560719</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Impact">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Impact</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer treatment may affect menstruation, fertility, and pregnancy capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560719</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Report">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Report</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The report from imaging tests detailing tumor location and spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560720</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Allergic Reactions">
      <data key="d0">Allergic Reactions</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Adverse responses to contrast agents that should be reported to the care team.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560720</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Panoramic Dental X-Ray">
      <data key="d0">Panoramic Dental X-Ray</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A panoramic dental x-ray captures an image of the entire mouth, including teeth, jaw, and surrounding structures, using a small dose of radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560720</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Syringe">
      <data key="d0">Syringe</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A syringe is a medical instrument used for injecting or withdrawing fluids, commonly used during biopsies and tracer injections in scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560720</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Squamous Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic squamous cell skin cancer is a form of skin cancer that has spread through blood or lymph to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560720</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical treatment involves physically removing the tumor and affected lymph nodes to treat skin cancer that has spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560720</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Distant Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Distant Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">otherobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Distant tumors are secondary growths of cancer that may cause symptoms such as pain or other side effects, and can be treated with radiation and/or surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560720</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="High-Risk Patients">
      <data key="d0">High-Risk Patients</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">High-risk patients are individuals with a history of skin cancer who require more frequent monitoring for recurrence or new cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560720</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Screening Scans">
      <data key="d0">Screening Scans</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Scans of the upper body, stomach, and pelvis are used to detect metastasis in other areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560721</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-up Exams">
      <data key="d0">Follow-up Exams</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Follow-up examinations include skin inspection and imaging to monitor for cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560721</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Epstein-Barr Virus">
      <data key="d0">Epstein-Barr Virus</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) is a common virus associated with infectious mononucleosis and certain EBV-related cancers, including nasopharyngeal cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV) is a virus associated with nasopharyngeal carcinoma, detectable in blood and used for prognostic assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;Epstein-Barr Virus is associated with positive neck lymph nodes in carcinoma patients, especially in nasopharyngeal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560721</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Information">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Information</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic information involves DNA molecules in cells, encoded within chromosomes, containing instructions for protein synthesis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560721</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="EPV (Epstein-Barr Virus)">
      <data key="d0">EPV (Epstein-Barr Virus)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">EPV levels in the blood are used as part of prognostic assessment in cancer staging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560721</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor (T)">
      <data key="d0">Tumor (T)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The T component measures the size and extent of the main tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560721</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node (N)">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node (N)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The N component indicates whether cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes, including how many and where.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560721</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Distant Metastasis (M)">
      <data key="d0">Distant Metastasis (M)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The M component indicates if cancer has spread to distant organs such as lungs or liver.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560721</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="TNM Staging">
      <data key="d0">TNM Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The TNM staging system classifies tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis for nasopharyngeal carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560721</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Head and neck tumors">
      <data key="d0">Head and neck tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumors in this category are measured in centimeters, with examples provided for size comparison.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560721</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Parapharyngeal Space">
      <data key="d0">Parapharyngeal Space</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A triangular area near the pharynx that can be invaded by tumor growth in advanced cancer stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560721</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ovary">
      <data key="d0">Ovary</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovaries are organs on each side of the upper uterus that produce eggs and hormones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560721</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Dysplasia">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Dysplasia</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical dysplasia refers to abnormal, microscopic cell changes on the surface of the cervix that may progress to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Abnormal, microscopic cell abnormalities on the cervix surface that can develop into cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical dysplasia refers to areas of abnormal cells on the lining of the cervix, ranging from mild to severe forms (CIN 1-3), and includes HSIL as a pre-cancerous condition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560721</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia (CIN)">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia (CIN)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">CIN represents graded abnormal cell changes in cervical tissue, with grades 1, 2, or 3 indicating severity.&lt;SEP&gt;CIN refers to abnormal cell growth on the surface of the cervix, with grades CIN 1-3 indicating severity levels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560721</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CIN 1">
      <data key="d0">CIN 1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">CIN 1 corresponds to mild dysplasia, also called Low-grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion (LSIL).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560721</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CIN 2 and 3">
      <data key="d0">CIN 2 and 3</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">CIN 2 and 3 refer to moderate or severe dysplasia, collectively called High-grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion (HSIL).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560721</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Transformation Zone">
      <data key="d0">Transformation Zone</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The transformation zone is the area on the cervix where most cervical cancers and pre-cancers, including CIN, start.&lt;SEP&gt;The junction where ectocervix and endocervix meet, common site for cervical dysplasia and cancer initiation.&lt;SEP&gt;The transformation zone is the area where the ectocervix and endocervix meet, and is the most common origin point for cervical cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560722</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ectocervix">
      <data key="d0">Ectocervix</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Outer part of the cervix visible during examination, lined with squamous cells.&lt;SEP&gt;The outer part of the cervix that extends into the vagina, where most cervical cancers and pre-cancers originate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560722</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Endocervix">
      <data key="d0">Endocervix</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Inner part of the cervix forming a canal, lined with glandular cells that produce mucus.&lt;SEP&gt;The canal within the cervix lined with glandular cells, where some cervical cancers develop.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560722</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="External Os">
      <data key="d0">External Os</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The narrow opening in the ectocervix that opens during menstruation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560722</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Inner Os">
      <data key="d0">Inner Os</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Upper part of the endocervix, serving as the opening between the uterus and cervix.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560722</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Stroma">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Stroma</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Thick muscular tissue beneath the cervical lining.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560722</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Parametrium">
      <data key="d0">Parametrium</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Fat and connective tissue surrounding the uterus and cervix, connecting to the pelvis.&lt;SEP&gt;The parametrium is tissue surrounding the cervix, evaluated for cancer spread via MRI.&lt;SEP&gt;The parametrium is the tissue surrounding the uterus, involved in advanced cervical cancers requiring interstitial brachytherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560722</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Changes">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Changes</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Abnormal cell changes in the cervix, starting as cervical dysplasia and potentially progressing to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical changes refer to abnormal cells or tissue alterations on the cervix, indicating potential precancerous conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560722</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CIN (Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia)">
      <data key="d0">CIN (Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Graded abnormal cell changes in the cervical epithelium, with grades 1, 2, or 3 indicating severity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560723</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T0">
      <data key="d0">T0</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor stage indicating no tumor is found but neck lymph nodes test positive for Epstein-Barr virus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560723</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Anterior">
      <data key="d0">Anterior</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The front part of the cervix within the transformation zone involved in cervical pathology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560723</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Posterior">
      <data key="d0">Posterior</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The back part of the cervix within the transformation zone involved in cervical pathology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560723</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Clotting">
      <data key="d0">Blood Clotting</data>
      <data key="d1">process</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood clotting is a series of events involving coagulation factors that stop bleeding by forming a blood clot, and it involves proteins made by the liver.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560723</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="HLA (Human Leukocyte Antigen)">
      <data key="d0">HLA (Human Leukocyte Antigen)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">HLA is a protein on cell surfaces playing a critical role in immune response, and HLA typing is a blood test to determine an individual's HLA type for transplant compatibility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560723</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="DL">
      <data key="d0">DL</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC) is a blood clotting disorder involving abnormal clot formation and bleeding.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560723</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Coagulopathy">
      <data key="d0">Coagulopathy</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Coagulopathy is a condition where blood clotting is impaired, often observed in leukemia patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560723</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Types of Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Types of Cervical Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Different classifications of cervical cancer include squamous cell carcinoma, adenocarcinoma, adenosquamous carcinoma, and neuroendocrine carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560724</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adenosquamous Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Adenosquamous Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">A rare form of cervical cancer containing both squamous and glandular cell components.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenosquamous carcinoma is a mixed type of cervical cancer with features of both squamous and glandular cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560724</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Neuroendocrine Carcinoma (NECC)">
      <data key="d0">Neuroendocrine Carcinoma (NECC)</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">A rare and aggressive type of cervical cancer originating from neuroendocrine cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560724</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="HPV Strains">
      <data key="d0">HPV Strains</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Over 100 strains of HPV exist, with types such as HPV16 and HPV-Other associated with higher risks of progressing to cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560724</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vaccination">
      <data key="d0">Vaccination</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV vaccination involves administering a vaccine that protects against multiple HPV strains to prevent infection and subsequent cervical dysplasia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560724</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">A cervical biopsy involves removing small tissue samples from the cervix, which may be taken from the ectocervix and/or endocervical canal.&lt;SEP&gt;Procedure for diagnosing cervical cancer by removing tissue samples for testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560724</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cone Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Cone Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Cone biopsy, also known as cervical conization, involves removing a cone-shaped portion of the cervix, including the transformation zone.&lt;SEP&gt;Procedure to remove a cone-shaped section of the cervix for diagnosis or treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Removal of a cone-shaped tissue section from the cervix for diagnosis or treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Cone biopsy is a surgical procedure used to remove a cone-shaped section of abnormal tissue from the cervix margins for diagnostic and treatment purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560724</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cold Knife Conization">
      <data key="d0">Cold Knife Conization</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Cold knife conization (CKC) uses a surgical scalpel to remove cervical tissue in cone biopsy procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560724</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Loop Electrosurgical Excision Procedure">
      <data key="d0">Loop Electrosurgical Excision Procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">LEEP uses a heated wire loop to excise tissue from the cervix during cone biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560725</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Endocervical Curettage">
      <data key="d0">Endocervical Curettage</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Endocervical curettage involves scraping tissue from the cervical canal using a curette after cone biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560725</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Health History and Physical Exam">
      <data key="d0">Health History and Physical Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical assessment involving review of personal health, physical examination, vital signs, and pelvic exam to evaluate cervical health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560725</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="HIV Test">
      <data key="d0">HIV Test</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">A test to determine HIV status, which can influence treatment planning but does not affect cancer therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560725</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Scalpel">
      <data key="d0">Scalpel</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical instrument used in cold knife conization to remove tissue during cone biopsies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560725</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Curette">
      <data key="d0">Curette</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A spoon-shaped tool used for endocervical curettage to scrape tissue from the cervical canal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560725</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Lung Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung cancers are abnormal cell growths originating in the lungs' lining cells, with various subtypes including squamous cell carcinomas, large cell carcinoma, and small cell lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560725</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Carcinomas">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Carcinomas</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A subtype of lung cancer, comprising about 3 out of 10 lung cancers, arising from squamous cells in the lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560725</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Radiotracers">
      <data key="d0">Radiotracers</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiotracers are small amounts of radioactive material used in PET scans to emit energy detectable by the imaging machine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560725</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sugar Radiotracer">
      <data key="d0">Sugar Radiotracer</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A radiotracer made of a sugar compound is injected before PET to highlight areas with high sugar metabolism, such as cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560725</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Small Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix">
      <data key="d0">Small Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A rare type of cervical cancer, for which MRI of the brain is also recommended during initial testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560725</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="MRI of the Brain">
      <data key="d0">MRI of the Brain</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">MRI of the brain involves imaging the brain using MRI to detect possible metastases in cases of small cell neuroendocrine carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560725</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Table">
      <data key="d0">Table</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">In MRI, the patient lies face-up on a table that moves through a scanner tunnel to acquire images.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560725</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility-Sparing Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Fertility-Sparing Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment option for cancer confined to the cervix allowing future pregnancy.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation options are available for women with early-stage cervical cancer who wish to conceive in the future.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility-sparing treatment includes surgical options that preserve the uterus and ovaries, allowing future pregnancy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560725</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Reproductive Endocrinologist">
      <data key="d0">Reproductive Endocrinologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A doctor specializing in fertility options, consulted for fertility preservation strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560725</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Surrogate">
      <data key="d0">Surrogate</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A woman who carries a pregnancy on behalf of another individual or couple, using cryopreserved eggs or embryos.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560725</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Frozen Eggs">
      <data key="d0">Frozen Eggs</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Eggs that have been cryopreserved for later fertility procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560726</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Frozen Embryos">
      <data key="d0">Frozen Embryos</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Embryos stored after fertilization, used for future pregnancy attempts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560726</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Examinations Under Anesthesia">
      <data key="d0">Examinations Under Anesthesia</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Procedures like cystoscopy and proctoscopy performed with general anesthesia to evaluate bladder, bowel, and nearby organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560726</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Proctoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Proctoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Procedure to examine the rectum and bowel for signs of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Proctoscopy involves examining the inside of the anus and rectum with a proctoscope.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560726</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Excision">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Excision</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical excision is a recommended treatment option for high-risk or very high-risk local CSCC, involving removal of a wider margin of normal tissue around the lesion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560726</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cemiplimab-rwlc (Libtayo)">
      <data key="d0">Cemiplimab-rwlc (Libtayo)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Cemiplimab-rwlc (Libtayo) is an immune checkpoint inhibitor used as immunotherapy for CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560726</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA)">
      <data key="d0">Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">FNA is a biopsy technique used to evaluate lymph nodes for cancer involvement.&lt;SEP&gt;A minimally invasive biopsy technique that uses a thin needle to extract tissue or fluid from suspicious thyroid nodules for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560726</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Extracapsular Extension">
      <data key="d0">Extracapsular Extension</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Extracapsular extension refers to cancer growth through the outer layer of lymph nodes, indicating more advanced disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560726</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tobacco">
      <data key="d0">Tobacco</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Tobacco is a plant that contains nicotine and is used for smoking.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560727</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nicotine Withdrawal">
      <data key="d0">Nicotine Withdrawal</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Nicotine withdrawal refers to the challenging symptoms experienced when stopping nicotine consumption.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560727</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="FIGO System">
      <data key="d0">FIGO System</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A standardized staging system used specifically for cervical cancer, categorizing stages from I to IV and various sub-stages.&lt;SEP&gt;An international staging system used to classify the severity and spread of cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560727</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stress and Symptom Control">
      <data key="d0">Stress and Symptom Control</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Interventions such as support groups, therapy, exercise, and medication to help manage emotional distress during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560727</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Distant Organs">
      <data key="d0">Distant Organs</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Organs such as the liver, lungs, and bones where cancer can metastasize.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560727</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Outcomes">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Outcomes</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to the prognosis or expected results of treatment, often better with earlier stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560727</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Progression">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Progression</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The process by which cancer grows and spreads through stages from localized to metastatic.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560727</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Tissue">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Tissue</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Samples of tissue from the cervix tested for dysplasia and cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560727</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A form of radiation therapy where a radiation source is placed close to or in contact with the tumor for a short period.&lt;SEP&gt;A form of radiation therapy involving placing a radiation source close to the tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Internal radiation therapy placed close to or inside the tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy, also known as internal radiation therapy, involves placing radioactive material inside the body near the tumor, using intracavitary or interstitial techniques, for targeted radiation delivery.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy involves placing radioactive material directly inside or near the tumor for targeted radiation treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a form of internal radiation treatment used when cancer or pre-cancer is present and surgery isn't possible.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is used as part of treatment for locally advanced cervical cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560727</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Surgery Types">
      <data key="d0">Surgery Types</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Includes cone biopsy, trachelectomy, hysterectomy, and their variants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560727</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Trachelectomy">
      <data key="d0">Trachelectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical removal of the cervix, with options for fertility preservation.&lt;SEP&gt;Trachelectomy is a fertility-sparing surgery that removes the cervix and possibly part of the vagina.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560727</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ovary Preservation">
      <data key="d0">Ovary Preservation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Deciding whether to retain ovaries during hysterectomy to avoid surgical menopause.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560727</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Menopause">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Menopause</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Menopause caused by ovary removal, leading to hormonal and symptomatic changes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560727</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vaginal Atrophy">
      <data key="d0">Vaginal Atrophy</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Thinning and dryness of vaginal lining due to estrogen deficiency.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560727</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Abdomen">
      <data key="d0">Abdomen</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to the abdominal region involved in surgical procedures like laparotomy and hysterectomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560727</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph nodes located in the pelvic area involved in cancer spread assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;Pelvic lymph nodes are located in the pelvis and may be removed during hysterectomy for staging or treatment purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560727</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Approach">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Approach</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Includes traditional open surgery and minimally invasive (laparoscopic) approaches for treating cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560728</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Laparoscopic Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Laparoscopic Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Minimally invasive surgery using small incisions and cameras.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560728</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Radical Trachelectomy">
      <data key="d0">Radical Trachelectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Removal of the cervix and surrounding tissues while preserving the uterus for fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Radical trachelectomy is a surgical procedure that removes the cervix and surrounding tissues while preserving the uterus for fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560728</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Simple Trachelectomy">
      <data key="d0">Simple Trachelectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Removal of only the cervix, typically for small cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560728</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Extrafascial Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">Extrafascial Hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Removes only the uterus and cervix, performed via vag, abd, or minimally invasive approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Extrafascial hysterectomy is a surgical removal of the uterus, recommended for some cases of cervical cancer without desire for future pregnancy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560728</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Modified Radical Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">Modified Radical Hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Removes uterus, cervix, and some surrounding tissue, typically via abdomen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560728</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Radical Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">Radical Hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Removes entire uterus, cervix, and significant connective tissue and part of the vagina.&lt;SEP&gt;Radical hysterectomy is an extensive surgical procedure involving removal of the uterus, surrounding tissues, and possibly lymph nodes, used for more advanced or high-risk early-stage cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560728</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Margins">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Margins</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">The edges of tissue removed in surgery; clear margins indicate complete excision.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560728</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="International Classification">
      <data key="d0">International Classification</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Standardized system (FIGO) for staging cervical cancer based on disease extent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560729</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Smoking Cessation">
      <data key="d0">Smoking Cessation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Quitting smoking can enhance treatment efficacy and outcomes in cervical cancer management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560729</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Atrophy">
      <data key="d0">Atrophy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Atrophy is a condition characterized by the thinning, dryness, and inflammation of the vaginal lining.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560729</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Radioactive Substance">
      <data key="d0">Radioactive Substance</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A radioactive agent used in sentinel lymph node biopsy to identify sentinel nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560729</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic lymph nodes are the closest lymph nodes to the cervix and are often the first sites of cervical cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560729</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Para-Aortic Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Para-Aortic Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Para-aortic lymph nodes are located in the abdomen and may be removed when testing for advanced cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560729</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pre-Treatment Surgical Staging">
      <data key="d0">Pre-Treatment Surgical Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Involves removing and testing pelvic and para-aortic lymph nodes before treatment to determine cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560729</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Laparoscopic Surgical Staging">
      <data key="d0">Laparoscopic Surgical Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A minimally invasive technique used to access and remove lymph nodes for cancer staging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560729</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Redness">
      <data key="d0">Skin Redness</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A side effect involving redness and irritation of the skin caused by radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560729</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Menopausal Hormone Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Menopausal Hormone Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Menopausal hormone therapy (MHT) is used after radiation to manage menopausal symptoms caused by ovarian damage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560729</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bowel">
      <data key="d0">Bowel</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Bowel function can be affected long-term by radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560729</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Dose">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Dose</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The specific amount of radiation used in therapy, determined during planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560729</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Number of Sessions">
      <data key="d0">Number of Sessions</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The total number of radiation treatments scheduled for the patient.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560729</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Positioning">
      <data key="d0">Patient Positioning</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Involves using devices and skin tattoos to accurately target radiation each session.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560729</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Locally Advanced Cervical Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Locally Advanced Cervical Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancers that have grown beyond the cervix but have not spread to distant organs, often treated with chemoradiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560729</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphadenectomy">
      <data key="d0">Lymphadenectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphadenectomy, also called lymph node dissection, involves surgically removing lymph nodes to test for cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560729</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Testing">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing lymph nodes for cancer involves surgical removal and pathological examination during cancer management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560730</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="EBRT">
      <data key="d0">EBRT</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">EBRT (External Beam Radiation Therapy) involves treatment with external radiation beams aimed at the cervical cancer region, typically delivered in 5 sessions per week over 5 to 6 weeks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560730</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Intracavitary Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intracavitary Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Intracavitary brachytherapy involves placing radioactive sources within body cavities such as the cervix, uterus, and vagina.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560730</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Interstitial Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">Interstitial Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Interstitial brachytherapy involves placing radioactive sources directly into tissues next to the tumor, used for more advanced cancers affecting the parametrium.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560730</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="HDR Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">HDR Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">High Dose Rate brachytherapy is a form of brachytherapy delivering high doses of radiation in short treatment bursts over 2 weeks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560730</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="LDR Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">LDR Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Low Dose Rate brachytherapy delivers radiation over longer periods (often 1 to 2 days) at lower doses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560730</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Applicator">
      <data key="d0">Applicator</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">An applicator is a device inserted into the uterus, often using tandem and ring or tandem and ovoid configurations, to guide radioactive sources during brachytherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560730</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tandem and Ring Applicator">
      <data key="d0">Tandem and Ring Applicator</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A tandem and ring applicator consists of a long tube (tandem) inserted into the uterus and a ring in the vagina to target radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560730</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tandem and Ovoid Applicator">
      <data key="d0">Tandem and Ovoid Applicator</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">This applicator uses a tandem with hollow capsules (ovoid) instead of a ring for radiation delivery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560731</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Wound Healing">
      <data key="d0">Wound Healing</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The process of tissue repair after surgery, which can occur via different methods including secondary intention and linear closure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560731</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Graft">
      <data key="d0">Skin Graft</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A skin graft is a piece of skin removed from one area of the body and transplanted to another to aid wound healing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560731</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Electron">
      <data key="d0">Electron</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Electrons are negatively charged particles used in external beam radiation therapy for superficial tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560731</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Photon">
      <data key="d0">Photon</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Photons are high-energy x-rays used in external beam radiation therapy for deeper tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560731</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CSCC">
      <data key="d0">CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer that can be treated with surgery or radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560731</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Conditions">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Conditions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic conditions such as basal cell nevus syndrome or xeroderma pigmentosum increase the risk of skin cancer caused by radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560731</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Connective Tissue Diseases">
      <data key="d0">Connective Tissue Diseases</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Conditions like lupus or scleroderma that may influence the suitability of radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560731</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Care">
      <data key="d0">Care</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Postoperative wound care, scar management, and aftercare are critical aspects of cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560731</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Procedure">
      <data key="d0">Procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Procedures mentioned include sentinel lymph node biopsy, excisions, skin grafting, and radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560731</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NLM">
      <data key="d0">NLM</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The National Library of Medicine (NLM) offers a database for clinical trials, accessible via clinicaltrials.gov.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560731</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Worldwide">
      <data key="d0">Worldwide</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Worldwide refers to the global scope of clinical trial searches, including resources like NLM's clinicaltrials.gov.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560731</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Costs">
      <data key="d0">Costs</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Participants in clinical trials may incur costs related to transportation, child care, or ancillary services not covered by the study sponsor or insurance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560731</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="PARP Inhibitor">
      <data key="d0">PARP Inhibitor</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A class of drugs used in maintenance therapy for ovarian cancer, including olaparib, niraparib, and rucaparib.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560731</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Niraparib">
      <data key="d0">Niraparib</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Niraparib is a PARP inhibitor that can be given for up to 3 years as maintenance therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Niraparib (Zejula) is a PARP inhibitor used as an alternative to olaparib for maintenance therapy if olaparib is unsuitable.&lt;SEP&gt;Niraparib is used as an alternative maintenance therapy to olaparib, especially if olaparib is not tolerated or suitable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560731</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rucaparib">
      <data key="d0">Rucaparib</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Rucaparib is a PARP inhibitor used for up to 2 years in maintenance therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Rucaparib (Rubraca) is another PARP inhibitor used for maintenance therapy in ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Rucaparib offers another maintenance option for specific ovarian cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560731</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ret">
      <data key="d0">Ret</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">RET mutations are genetic alterations that can be targeted in ovarian cancer therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;RET gene fusion is a rare genetic alteration that may be tested in cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560732</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NTRK">
      <data key="d0">NTRK</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">NTRK gene fusions are genetic changes that may be targeted with specific treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560732</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum-Resistant Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Platinum-Resistant Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian cancer that does not respond to platinum-based chemotherapy or recurs within 6 months, requiring alternative treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560732</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum-Sensitive Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Platinum-Sensitive Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian cancer that recurs after more than 6 months of platinum-based chemotherapy, typically responding well to repeat platinum treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560732</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bevacizumab (Avastin)">
      <data key="d0">Bevacizumab (Avastin)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Bevacizumab is used in recurrence treatment and can be continued as maintenance therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560732</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Secondary Cytoreductive Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Secondary Cytoreductive Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical procedure to remove all visible cancer before recurrence treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560732</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2 Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2 Ovarian Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 2 ovarian cancer is a classification indicating the extent of cancer spread, with limited research on maintenance therapy outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560732</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ongoing Research">
      <data key="d0">Ongoing Research</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Ongoing research refers to continuous scientific studies that may influence future treatment recommendations for ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560732</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Exam">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic examination is a physical exam recommended during surveillance for ovarian cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560733</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Early-Stage Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Early-Stage Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Early-stage cancer refers to initial phases of cancer where the tumor is confined and has not extensively spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560733</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Locally Advanced Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Locally Advanced Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Locally advanced cancer describes cancer that has spread beyond the original site but not to distant parts.&lt;SEP&gt;Locally advanced cervical cancer has grown beyond the cervix but has not spread to the liver, lungs, or bones, often requiring chemoradiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560733</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphovascular Space Invasion">
      <data key="d0">Lymphovascular Space Invasion</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphovascular space invasion (LVSI) indicates tumor cells present in blood or lymphatic vessels, influencing treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560733</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Conization">
      <data key="d0">Conization</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Conization is a surgical procedure involving removal of a cone-shaped tissue sample from the cervix, used for early-stage cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560733</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Lymph Node Evaluation">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Lymph Node Evaluation</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic lymph node evaluation assesses the spread of cancer to pelvic lymph nodes, guiding treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560733</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Para-Aortic Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Para-Aortic Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Para-aortic lymph nodes are located in the abdomen and may be removed in some cervical cancer surgeries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560733</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1A1">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1A1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1A1 is an early cervical cancer stage characterized by tumors smaller than 3 mm with no LVSI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560733</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1A2">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1A2</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1A2 involves slightly larger tumors (up to 4 mm deep and less than 2 cm wide) without LVSI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560733</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1B1">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1B1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1B1 refers to small tumors (less than 2 cm) confined to the cervix, with no LVSI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560733</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1B2">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1B2</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1B2 refers to tumors larger than 2 cm but still confined to the cervix, often with no LVSI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560733</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Platinum Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Platinum chemotherapy uses platinum-based drugs administered during chemoradiation for cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560733</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Laser Ablation">
      <data key="d0">Laser Ablation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Laser ablation is a technique used to remove or destroy cancerous tissue through focused laser energy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560733</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Extrafascial (Simple) Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">Extrafascial (Simple) Hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Extrafascial (simple) hysterectomy is a surgical procedure frequently recommended for treating certain conditions of the uterus, often involving removal of the uterus without extensive tissue removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560733</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pre-cancer">
      <data key="d0">Pre-cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Pre-cancer indicates abnormal cellular changes in the cervix that have the potential to develop into cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560733</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="EBRT (External Beam Radiation Therapy)">
      <data key="d0">EBRT (External Beam Radiation Therapy)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">EBRT is a radiation treatment that may be combined with brachytherapy for more effective treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560733</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1A1 Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1A1 Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1A1 cancer is a classification of early cervical cancer with specific criteria such as size and absence of lymphovascular space invasion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560734</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="LVSI (Lymphovascular Space Invasion)">
      <data key="d0">LVSI (Lymphovascular Space Invasion)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">LVSI indicates cancer cells invading lymphatic or blood vessels, influencing treatment choices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560734</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Low-risk Stage 1B1 and 1A2 Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Low-risk Stage 1B1 and 1A2 Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">These are specific classifications of early-stage cervical cancer characterized by size, absence of LVSI, and no cancer found in margins, with treatment options including extrafascial hysterectomy or modified radical hysterectomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560734</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1B2 and 2A1 Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1B2 and 2A1 Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">These stages refer to larger or more invasive cervical cancers, often treated with radical hysterectomy and lymph node evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560734</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1B3 Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1B3 Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1B3 indicates tumors larger than 4 cm that do not extend beyond the cervix, possibly treated with surgery or chemoradiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560734</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2A2 Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2A2 Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 2A2 involves larger tumors extending into the upper vagina, with treatment options including chemoradiation or surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560734</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Para-aortic Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Para-aortic Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Para-aortic lymph nodes are located in the abdomen and may be removed during surgical staging if lymph node involvement or suspicion exists.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560734</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment with Chemoradiation">
      <data key="d0">Treatment with Chemoradiation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment approach combining radiation and chemotherapy, preferred for certain stages of cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560734</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-up Visits">
      <data key="d0">Follow-up Visits</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Scheduled medical visits post-treatment for physical exams to monitor health changes and detect early recurrence signs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560735</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix">
      <data key="d0">Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Small cell neuroendocrine carcinoma of the cervix (NECC) is a specific aggressive type of cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560735</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1, 2, 3, 4A, 4B">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1, 2, 3, 4A, 4B</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer stages outlining the extent of spread and guiding treatment and follow-up imaging protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560735</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="MRI of pelvis">
