    <node id="Pelvic exenter">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic exenter</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic exenteration is a surgical procedure used for localized recurrent cervical cancer confined to the pelvis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560735</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="RET">
      <data key="d0">RET</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">RET gene fusion is a genetic alteration that may be tested for in cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560735</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Exenteration">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Exenteration</data>
      <data key="d1">surgicalprocedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic exenteration is a radical surgery that removes multiple organs from the pelvis to treat cancer, including the uterus, ovaries, fallopian tubes, vagina, bladder, rectum, and possibly anus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560735</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vaginal Reconstruction">
      <data key="d0">Vaginal Reconstruction</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Vaginal reconstruction is a surgical process to create or restore the vagina, often using muscle tissue such as the rectus abdominus from another area of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560735</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ileal (Incontinent) Conduit">
      <data key="d0">Ileal (Incontinent) Conduit</data>
      <data key="d1">medicaldevice</data>
      <data key="d2">An ileal conduit is a urinary diversion procedure using a segment of intestine to create a stoma for urine drainage after bladder removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560735</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Continent Urinary Diversion">
      <data key="d0">Continent Urinary Diversion</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A continent urinary diversion, or artificial bladder, uses intestine to create a pouch for urine, connecting to the abdominal wall via a stoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560735</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Double-Barreled Wet Colostomy">
      <data key="d0">Double-Barreled Wet Colostomy</data>
      <data key="d1">surgicaltechnique</data>
      <data key="d2">A double-barreled wet colostomy combines urinary and fecal diversion through a single stoma, separating urine and stool.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560736</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ablative Therapies">
      <data key="d0">Ablative Therapies</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Ablative therapies destroy cancerous lesions with minimal harm to surrounding tissue, often using needles or electrodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560736</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Targeted Therapies">
      <data key="d0">Targeted Therapies</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Targeted therapies are treatments designed to specifically target molecular markers or pathways involved in cancer progression, often guided by biomarker testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560736</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Immunotherapies">
      <data key="d0">Immunotherapies</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunotherapies involve stimulating or restoring the immune system's ability to fight cancer, often indicated by biomarker testing results.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560736</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Intravenous Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intravenous Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Intravenous chemotherapy involves administering anti-cancer drugs directly into the bloodstream through a vein, often via a port.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560736</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Intraperitoneal (IP) Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intraperitoneal (IP) Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Intraperitoneal chemotherapy involves injecting chemotherapy drugs into the abdomen through a catheter connected to an internal port.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560736</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Monitoring">
      <data key="d0">Monitoring</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Monitoring during chemotherapy includes physical exams, imaging, blood tests, and tumor marker assessments to evaluate treatment effectiveness and side effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560736</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Myelodysplastic Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Myelodysplastic Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">MDS, a potential side effect of PARP inhibitors and chemotherapy, involves the marrow's inability to produce enough healthy blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;MDS is a rare cancer where the bone marrow produces insufficient healthy blood cells, sometimes progressing to leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560736</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="PARP Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">PARP Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">PARP inhibitors are targeted oral therapies used in maintenance treatment for ovarian cancer, especially in HRD-positive and BRCA-mutated cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560737</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Homologous Recombination Deficiency (HRD)">
      <data key="d0">Homologous Recombination Deficiency (HRD)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">HRD is a condition making tumors more sensitive to PARP inhibitors and a key factor in maintenance therapy decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560737</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2, 3, and 4 Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2, 3, and 4 Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Stages of ovarian cancer that may be suitable for maintenance therapy based on treatment response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560737</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Observation">
      <data key="d0">Observation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Observation is an alternative management strategy involving monitoring without immediate further treatment post-chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560737</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="HRD (Homologous Recombination Deficiency)">
      <data key="d0">HRD (Homologous Recombination Deficiency)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">HRD is a condition where tumors have defective DNA repair, making them more susceptible to PARP inhibitors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560737</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="MDS (Myelodysplastic Syndrome)">
      <data key="d0">MDS (Myelodysplastic Syndrome)</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">MDS is a blood disorder that can be a serious rare side effect of chemotherapy and PARP inhibitor therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560737</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Duration">
      <data key="d0">Duration</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The duration of maintenance therapy varies by drug, with olaparib up to 2 years, niraparib up to 3 years, and rucaparib up to 2 years.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560737</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Local therapies">
      <data key="d0">Local therapies</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Local therapies are treatments targeted directly at the tumor site, which may be completed before systemic therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560737</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Systemic therapy">
      <data key="d0">Systemic therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic therapy is a form of treatment that affects the entire body, often following local therapies for metastatic cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560737</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical cancer">
      <data key="d0">Cervical cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical cancer is a malignancy of the cervix that can be treated with chemotherapy and other systemic therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560737</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Targeted therapy">
      <data key="d0">Targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Targeted therapy refers to treatments such as bevacizumab that target specific molecules or pathways in cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560737</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tisotumab vedotin-tftv">
      <data key="d0">Tisotumab vedotin-tftv</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Tisotumab vedotin-tftv (Tivdak) is a treatment option for cancers without specific biomarkers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560737</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Palliative care">
      <data key="d0">Palliative care</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Palliative care is a form of supportive care focused on comfort and quality of life for advanced cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560737</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical trial">
      <data key="d0">Clinical trial</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A clinical trial is a research study that patients with metastatic cervical cancer may enroll in to access new treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560737</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Advance care planning">
      <data key="d0">Advance care planning</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Advance care planning involves discussions with healthcare providers about prognosis, treatment goals, and end-of-life wishes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560737</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mediastinal gray zone lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Mediastinal gray zone lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Mediastinal gray zone lymphoma (MGZL) is a type of lymphoma with features overlapping primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma and Hodgkin lymphoma, requiring specialized management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560737</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hodgkin lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Hodgkin lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">Hodgkin lymphoma is a type of lymphoma characterized by the presence of Reed-Sternberg cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560738</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma (PMBL) is a distinct lymphoma that occurs in the mediastinum and requires different treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560738</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Reed-Sternberg cells">
      <data key="d0">Reed-Sternberg cells</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Reed-Sternberg cells are large, abnormal lymphocytes characteristic of Hodgkin lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560738</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mediastinal lymphomas">
      <data key="d0">Mediastinal lymphomas</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Mediastinal lymphomas are growths found behind the sternum in the chest, associated with mediastinal lymphomas including MGZL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560738</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Non-mediastinal gray zone lymphomas">
      <data key="d0">Non-mediastinal gray zone lymphomas</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-mediastinal gray zone lymphomas occur outside the mediastinum and have different characteristics and disease spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560738</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Extranodal disease">
      <data key="d0">Extranodal disease</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Extranodal disease involves cancer outside the lymph nodes, more frequent in non-mediastinal gray zone lymphomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560738</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced-stage disease">
      <data key="d0">Advanced-stage disease</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-mediastinal gray zone lymphomas tend to present at more advanced stages compared to mediastinal versions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560738</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Hodgkin primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Non-Hodgkin primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-Hodgkin primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma (PMBL) is a distinct lymphoma, different from mediastinal gray zone lymphoma, involving the mediastinum.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560738</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="DLBCL, not otherwise specified">
      <data key="d0">DLBCL, not otherwise specified</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">DLBCL-NOS (Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma, not otherwise specified) is a diagnosis for cancers outside the mediastinum, distinguished from MGZL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560739</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Older Persons">
      <data key="d0">Older Persons</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Older persons tend to have higher rates of bone marrow involvement and more advanced-stage disease in lymphoma, including extranodal disease outside lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560739</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Involvement">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Involvement</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone marrow involvement is a condition seen more frequently in older persons with certain lymphomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560739</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphomas">
      <data key="d0">Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphomas</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Mediastinal gray zone lymphomas are a type of lymphoma with features of both Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphoma, typically less advanced than other diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560739</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Involved Site Radiation Therapy (ISRT)">
      <data key="d0">Involved Site Radiation Therapy (ISRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">ISRT may be used in cases of localized disease to improve treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560739</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Gland">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Gland</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The thyroid gland is responsible for hormone production, including thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3).&lt;SEP&gt;An endocrine gland in the neck that produces thyroid hormones regulating metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;The thyroid gland is an endocrine organ located in the neck, producing hormones regulating metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560739</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroxine (T4)">
      <data key="d0">Thyroxine (T4)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroxine (T4) is one of the main hormones produced by the thyroid gland, regulating metabolism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560739</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Triiodothyronine (T3)">
      <data key="d0">Triiodothyronine (T3)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Triiodothyronine (T3) is a primary thyroid hormone involved in metabolic regulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560739</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Iodine">
      <data key="d0">Iodine</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A mineral from the diet used by the thyroid to produce hormones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560739</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Parathyroid Glands">
      <data key="d0">Parathyroid Glands</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Four small glands on the back of the thyroid that control blood calcium levels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560739</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Nodules">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Nodules</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Small abnormal growths within the thyroid gland, mostly benign and often showing no symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560739</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A common cancer in adults, often diagnosed early, with risk factors including radiation exposure and family history.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid cancer is described as potentially spreadable and treatable with various therapies including surgery, RAI, and radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;A malignant growth originating in the thyroid gland, including types such as papillary, follicular, medullary, and anaplastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid cancer is a type of cancer originating in the thyroid gland, with various treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid cancer is a type of cancer that starts in the thyroid gland and may be managed with monitoring or active treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid cancer is a malignant growth originating within the thyroid gland, involving various subtypes and treatment strategies discussed in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560739</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation Exposure">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Exposure</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Exposure to head or neck radiation increases thyroid cancer risk, especially after treatment or environmental events.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560739</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Syndromes">
      <data key="d0">Syndromes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic syndromes related to thyroid cancer include familial adenomatous polyposis, Carney complex, Cowden syndrome, and MEN.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560739</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cowden Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Cowden Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An inherited syndrome associated with follicular thyroid cancer and other cancers, also called PTEN hamartoma tumor syndrome.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560739</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="TSH Test">
      <data key="d0">TSH Test</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) blood test assesses hormone levels to evaluate thyroid function and nodules.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560739</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="FAP">
      <data key="d0">FAP</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is a hereditary syndrome associated with increased risk of thyroid cancer and other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560740</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Carney Complex">
      <data key="d0">Carney Complex</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Carney complex is a hereditary syndrome linked to endocrine tumors and increased risk of thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560740</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia (MEN)">
      <data key="d0">Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia (MEN)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">MEN is a hereditary syndrome involving multiple endocrine glands, associated with thyroid cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560740</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Nodule">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Nodule</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A growth or lump within the thyroid gland, which may produce thyroid hormone and can be benign or cancerous, affecting diagnosis and treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560740</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="TSH Level">
      <data key="d0">TSH Level</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The level of thyroid-stimulating hormone in the blood, which indicates thyroid function status, with high levels suggesting hypothyroidism and low levels indicating hyperthyroidism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560740</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Papillary Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Papillary Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The most common type of thyroid cancer, classified as a differentiated thyroid cancer, usually growing and spreading slowly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560740</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of differentiated thyroid cancer that can be identified by molecular testing but often requires surgery for definitive diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Follicular carcinoma is a subtype of differentiated thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560740</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Oncocytic Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Oncocytic Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A lesion in the thyroid that can be evaluated via fine-needle biopsy but may need surgical removal for diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560740</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Medullary Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Medullary Thyroid Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The third most common thyroid cancer, which can be inherited and involve the production of hormones by the cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560741</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Anaplastic Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Anaplastic Thyroid Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A rare, aggressive, and most often older adult-affecting thyroid cancer that is highly malignant.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560741</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lobectomy">
      <data key="d0">Lobectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical removal of one lobe of the thyroid gland, often for small, low-risk cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgical procedure removing a lobe of the thyroid, used for less invasive or minimally invasive thyroid tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobectomy involves removing part of the thyroid and may be suitable for low-risk cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobectomy is a surgical procedure removing one lobe of the thyroid, with different follow-up protocols compared to total thyroidectomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560741</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Total Thyroidectomy">
      <data key="d0">Total Thyroidectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Complete removal of the entire thyroid gland, often with removal of nearby lymph nodes, for more advanced cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgical procedure involving the removal of the entire thyroid gland, recommended for invasive tumors or larger than 4cm.&lt;SEP&gt;Total removal of the thyroid gland, traditionally standard for thyroid cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Total thyroidectomy is a surgical procedure involving removal of the entire thyroid gland, used in treatment and monitoring of thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560741</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Lifelong treatment using medications like levothyroxine after thyroidectomy to replace hormones produced by the thyroid.&lt;SEP&gt;Lifelong levothyroxine treatment used after thyroidectomy to maintain hormonal balance.&lt;SEP&gt;Lifelong therapy with levothyroxine is necessary after total thyroidectomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560741</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Uptake Test">
      <data key="d0">Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Uptake Test</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A nuclear imaging technique used to evaluate thyroid function by measuring iodine uptake in the gland.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560741</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Neck Dissection">
      <data key="d0">Neck Dissection</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical removal of lymph nodes in the neck area suspected of containing cancer in conjunction with thyroidectomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560741</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hypoparathyroidism">
      <data key="d0">Hypoparathyroidism</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">A potential long-term side effect of thyroid surgery causing low blood calcium levels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560741</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nerve Damage">
      <data key="d0">Nerve Damage</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">A possible complication from thyroid surgery affecting nerves controlling voice and swallowing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560742</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Levothyroxine">
      <data key="d0">Levothyroxine</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A common medication used in thyroid hormone replacement therapy to maintain normal thyroid hormone levels after thyroidectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;A synthetic thyroid hormone used as hormone replacement therapy to keep TSH levels low or normal after thyroid surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Levothyroxine is a synthetic thyroid hormone used for lifelong hormone replacement therapy after thyroidectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Levothyroxine is a medication used after treatment to suppress TSH levels and manage thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560742</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="TSH Suppression">
      <data key="d0">TSH Suppression</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A therapy goal involving keeping TSH levels low to aid in preventing recurrence of thyroid cancer after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560742</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Lesion">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Lesion</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Any abnormal tissue or growth within the thyroid that may be benign or malignant, assessed through ultrasound, biopsy, and other diagnostic methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560742</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Techniques">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Techniques</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Methods such as ultrasound, CT, and MRI used to visualize the thyroid and neck structures for cancer detection and staging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560742</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Molecular Tests">
      <data key="d0">Molecular Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced diagnostic tests used to determine the cancerous nature of thyroid nodules and guide surgical decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560742</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Levoxyl">
      <data key="d0">Levoxyl</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Levoxyl, also known as Synthroid, is a commonly used thyroid hormone replacement therapy used to manage thyroid hormone levels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560742</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Synthroid">
      <data key="d0">Synthroid</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Synthroid, similar to Levoxyl, is a thyroid hormone replacement medication for thyroid function management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560742</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) is a hormone used as a marker to regulate thyroid hormone levels during therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560743</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroidectomy">
      <data key="d0">Thyroidectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroidectomy is the surgical removal of the thyroid gland, used as a treatment for some thyroid cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560743</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Radioactive Iodine Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Radioactive Iodine Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Radioactive iodine therapy uses radioactive iodine (iodine-131) to selectively kill thyroid cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;RAI therapy is used after total thyroidectomy to destroy remaining cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Used to kill remaining cancer cells post-surgery in thyroid cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560743</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Iodine-131">
      <data key="d0">Iodine-131</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Iodine-131 is a radioactive isotope used in RAI therapy to target and destroy thyroid cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560743</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thyrogen">
      <data key="d0">Thyrogen</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyrogen is a hormone injection used to increase TSH levels when hormone therapy is stopped temporarily.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560743</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Endocrinologist">
      <data key="d0">Endocrinologist</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An endocrinologist is a specialist who manages hormonal therapy and thyroid function in thyroid cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560743</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nuclear Medicine Specialist">
      <data key="d0">Nuclear Medicine Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A nuclear medicine specialist administers radioactive iodine therapy and performs post-treatment scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560743</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thyrotropin Alfa">
      <data key="d0">Thyrotropin Alfa</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyrotropin alfa, known as Thyrogen, is a hormone used to increase TSH levels to aid in radioactive iodine therapy without stopping thyroid hormone replacement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560743</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Remnant Ablation">
      <data key="d0">Remnant Ablation</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Remnant ablation refers to the use of low-dose iodine-131 after thyroidectomy to destroy remaining normal or cancerous thyroid tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560744</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility and Family Planning">
      <data key="d0">Fertility and Family Planning</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Discusses risks related to fertility and options for family planning, including fertility preservation and counseling, in relation to cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560744</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of cancer affecting the thyroid gland, which can be treated with surgery, RAI therapy, targeted therapy, chemotherapy, or systemic therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560744</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kinase Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">Kinase Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of targeted therapy, generally used as capsules, that target and attack specific cancer cell types, especially for thyroid cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560744</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Phases">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Phases</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Different stages of clinical trials: Phase 1 (safety), Phase 2 (effectiveness), Phase 3 (comparison with standard treatment), and Phase 4 (long-term safety).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560744</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Long-Term Side Effects">
      <data key="d0">Long-Term Side Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Side effects that can develop years after treatment, such as secondary cancers and heart issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560744</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="External Radiation">
      <data key="d0">External Radiation</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation delivered from external sources such as x-rays, gamma rays, and protons, used in radiation therapy to treat cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560744</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Research Study">
      <data key="d0">Research Study</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Research studies include clinical trials to test the safety and effectiveness of new treatments or interventions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560744</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Study Drug">
      <data key="d0">Study Drug</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The study drug is the medication or treatment being tested in a clinical trial.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560744</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CANCER (CANCER)">
      <data key="d0">CANCER (CANCER)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">CANCER is a contact number for the Cancer Information Service providing assistance in finding clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560744</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Papillary Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Papillary Thyroid Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Papillary thyroid cancer is the most common type of differentiated thyroid cancer, usually slow-growing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560744</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Oncocytic Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Oncocytic Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Oncocytic carcinoma, formerly known as Hürthle cell carcinoma, is a rare thyroid cancer more likely to spread to lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Oncocytic carcinoma is another subtype of differentiated thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560744</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Monitoring and Follow-Up">
      <data key="d0">Monitoring and Follow-Up</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Monitoring involves ultrasound and other tests to check for recurrence or metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560744</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Additional Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Additional Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Further surgery may be performed if pathology reveals concerning features after initial surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560744</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Recurrence and Metastasis">
      <data key="d0">Recurrence and Metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Recurrence and metastasis are signs of cancer returning or spreading beyond the thyroid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560744</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Distant Areas">
      <data key="d0">Distant Areas</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Distant areas refer to parts of the body beyond the thyroid, such as lymph nodes or other organs, that may be affected by cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560744</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Suspicious Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Suspicious Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph nodes that appear abnormal or enlarged during evaluation, signaling possible metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560745</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologic Exam">
      <data key="d0">Pathologic Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Pathologic exam involves microscopic analysis of removed tissue to determine cancer features and risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560745</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Specimen">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Specimen</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical specimens, including tumor, tissue, or lymph nodes, are examined and tested after surgery to identify high-risk features and determine further treatment needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560745</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NIFTP">
      <data key="d0">NIFTP</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Noninvasive Follicular Thyroid Neoplasm with Papillary-Like Nuclear Features (NIFTP) is a low-risk, noninvasive thyroid tumor that requires no further treatment after removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560745</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular thyroid carcinoma (FTC) is the second most common type of thyroid cancer, known for spreading to lymph nodes and invading blood vessels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560745</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A treatment involving radioactive iodine to destroy residual thyroid cancer cells post-surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;RAI therapy uses radioactive iodine to target thyroid cancer cells, especially in cases with iodine uptake.&lt;SEP&gt;Rai therapy is recommended for iodine-avid metastatic thyroid cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560745</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="TSH">
      <data key="d0">TSH</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) is regulated by hormone therapy to maintain appropriate thyroid hormone levels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560745</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroglobulin">
      <data key="d0">Thyroglobulin</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroglobulin (Tg) is a protein measured after surgery to monitor for remaining or recurrent thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroglobulin (Tg) is a protein produced only by thyroid tissue, used as a tumor marker in monitoring thyroid cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560745</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hürthle Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Hürthle Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Hürthle cell carcinoma, also known as oncocytic carcinoma, is a rare type of thyroid cancer formerly called Hürthle cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560746</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Complete Blood Count (CBC)">
      <data key="d0">Complete Blood Count (CBC)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A blood test that measures red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets to assess overall health and detect abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560746</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP)">
      <data key="d0">Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A blood test measuring substances to evaluate organ function, including kidney and liver health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560746</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA) Typing">
      <data key="d0">Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA) Typing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A test to identify genetic markers important for organ transplant compatibility and understanding immune response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560746</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Brain CT">
      <data key="d0">Brain CT</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging procedure used without contrast to assess suspected central nervous system bleed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560746</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lumbar Puncture (LP)">
      <data key="d0">Lumbar Puncture (LP)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A procedure to collect cerebrospinal fluid for testing, often used in leukemia assessment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560746</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Organ Function Tests">
      <data key="d0">Organ Function Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Tests such as liver and kidney function tests to evaluate the health of these organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560746</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN)">
      <data key="d0">Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">A waste marker used to evaluate kidney health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560746</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH)">
      <data key="d0">Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH)</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">An enzyme indicating cell damage, released into blood by dying or tumor cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560746</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Function Tests (LFTs)">
      <data key="d0">Liver Function Tests (LFTs)</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood tests that evaluate liver health through chemical measurement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560746</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-up Testing">
      <data key="d0">Follow-up Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Follow-up testing includes physical exams, blood tests, ultrasound, and imaging to monitor for thyroid cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560746</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Neck Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Neck Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Neck ultrasound is used to detect recurrence of thyroid cancer after surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560746</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Tissue">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Tissue</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid tissue is involved in producing thyroglobulin and antibodies and is affected by treatments like lobectomy and total thyroidectomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560746</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Anti-Thyroglobulin Antibodies">
      <data key="d0">Anti-Thyroglobulin Antibodies</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Anti-thyroglobulin antibodies can interfere with Tg measurement and indicate immune response in thyroid cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560746</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="BRAF Gene">
      <data key="d0">BRAF Gene</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The BRAF gene mutation is a biomarker associated with certain thyroid cancers and impacts treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560746</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="ALK Gene Fusion">
      <data key="d0">ALK Gene Fusion</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">ALK gene fusion is a genetic biomarker associated with thyroid cancer, targeted in some treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560746</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="RET Gene Fusion">
      <data key="d0">RET Gene Fusion</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">RET gene fusion is associated with medullary thyroid cancers and guides targeted therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560747</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Microsatellite Instability-High">
      <data key="d0">Microsatellite Instability-High</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Microsatellite instability-high (MSI-H) is a genetic biomarker relevant for some thyroid cancers and therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560747</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Mutational Burden-High">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Mutational Burden-High</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor mutational burden-high (TMB-H) is a biomarker that influences immunotherapy decisions in thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560747</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lenvatinib">
      <data key="d0">Lenvatinib</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Lenvatinib (Lenvima) is a targeted therapy used for progressing or unresectable thyroid cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560747</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cabozantinib">
      <data key="d0">Cabozantinib</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Cabozantinib (Cabometyx) is used when cancer progresses after treatment with lenvatinib or sorafenib.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560747</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic tumors are cancerous growths that originate elsewhere and spread to the bones or other tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560747</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sarcomas">
      <data key="d0">Sarcomas</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Sarcomas are a rare group of cancers that originate in connective tissues such as bone or soft tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560747</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Soft Tissue Sarcomas">
      <data key="d0">Soft Tissue Sarcomas</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Soft tissue sarcomas develop in tissues like fat, muscle, nerves, tendons, blood, and lymph vessels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560747</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Sarcomas">
      <data key="d0">Bone Sarcomas</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone sarcomas, including osteosarcoma, Ewing sarcoma, and chondrosarcoma, originate in cells forming bones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560748</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Osteosarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Osteosarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Osteosarcoma is a type of bone cancer that starts in cells of the bones, especially near growth plates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560748</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chondrosarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Chondrosarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Chondrosarcoma is a type of bone cancer that starts in cartilage-forming cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560748</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Guide">
      <data key="d0">Guide</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The Guide lists recommended therapies and systemic treatment options for metastatic cancer that cannot be treated with surgery or RAI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560748</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Notochord">
      <data key="d0">Notochord</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The notochord is a structure in embryonic development, and chordomas start in notochordal cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560748</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chordoma">
      <data key="d0">Chordoma</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Chordoma is a type of tumor that starts in notochordal cells, usually located in the lower spine or skull base.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560748</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Growth Plate">
      <data key="d0">Growth Plate</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Growth plates are areas in bones where rapid growth occurs, often associated with bone tumors like osteosarcoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560748</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cartilage">
      <data key="d0">Cartilage</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Cartilage is a flexible tissue covering bone ends to facilitate joint movement and provide structural support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560748</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Epidemiology">
      <data key="d0">Epidemiology</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Epidemiology involves the study of the distribution and determinants of health-related states, including cancer types and prevalence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560748</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Thyroid Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic thyroid cancer refers to thyroid cancer that has spread to other parts of the body and may be treated with targeted therapy, immunotherapy, or monitored if slow-growing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560748</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Radioactive Iodine (RAI)">
      <data key="d0">Radioactive Iodine (RAI)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Radioactive iodine is used in the treatment of thyroid cancer, especially when the cancer takes up iodine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560748</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Guides">
      <data key="d0">Guides</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Guides refer to informational resources like the "Guide" that list treatments and systemic therapy options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560748</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Diseases">
      <data key="d0">Bone Diseases</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone diseases include primary bone cancers and metastases affecting bones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560748</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Book">
      <data key="d0">Book</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The book provides an organized overview of bone cancer testing, staging, types of treatment, specific cancers, resources, and patient advocacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560748</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 2">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 2</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 2 offers an overview of testing for bone cancer, including tests like biomarker and tumor mutation testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560748</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 5">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 5</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 5 discusses treatment options for Chondrosarcoma, primarily surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560748</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 6">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 6</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 6 discusses treatment options for Chordoma, including surgery and radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560749</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 7">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 7</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 7 explains systemic therapy as the beginning of treatment for Ewing sarcoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560749</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 8">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 8</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 8 discusses GCTB treatment, emphasizing aggressive treatment to prevent bone damage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560749</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 9">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 9</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 9 describes treatment options for Osteosarcoma, which involves a combination of therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1764560749</data>
